const USFM = `\\id LUK - Restored Context Bible (RCB)
\\usfm 3.0
\\ide UTF-8
\\h Luke
\\toc1 The Good News According to Luke
\\toc2 Luke
\\toc3 Luk
\\mt1 Luke

\\imt Introduction
\\ip This is the only book in the New Testament that opens with a preface, and the preface says plainly what kind of book it is. The writer is not an eyewitness. Others were, and what they saw had already been handed on to him; several people had already tried to set the story down in order. He has gone over the whole of it closely, from the start, and now writes it out for a man he addresses as "most excellent Theophilus" — a form of address he uses in his second volume for Roman governors, so probably a man of standing, possibly the patron paying for the work. Theophilus has already been taught these things. The book exists so that he can know how firm the ground under them is.
\\ip It is the first half of a two-part work. The second half, the Acts of the Apostles, opens by looking back at "the first account" and takes the story up where this one stops; the two are built to be read together, and what begins in a Galilean village ends, in the second volume, in Rome. The writer handles Greek well and knows how a Greek history is put together, but when he tells the birth stories and quotes Israel's scriptures he shifts into the older, heavier style of the Greek Old Testament, the way an English writer might drop into the cadence of the King James. Early tradition names him Luke, a doctor and a traveling companion of Paul; the book itself names no one. It was most likely written in the sixties or later, for readers who did not grow up inside Jewish practice.
\\ip The story runs across a real political map, and the writer keeps naming it: Caesar Augustus, Tiberius, Quirinius governing Syria, Pontius Pilate, the sons of Herod ruling quarters of their father's kingdom by Rome's permission, Annas and Caiaphas holding a high priesthood Rome could hand out and take away. Judea is occupied country. The temple still stands and still runs, and faithful people are still going up to it and waiting for God to keep what he swore to Abraham and to David — rescue from enemies, forgiveness of sins, the restoration of Israel. Into that waiting the book drops two births, and everything after them is an argument about what kind of rescue has actually arrived.
\\ip What marks this account out is who keeps turning up in it. The birth is announced to shepherds working a night shift. The songs at the beginning declare that God pulls rulers off their thrones and lifts up the low. The people who see what God is doing are repeatedly the wrong people — a Roman officer, a Samaritan, a tax collector, a woman with a reputation, a man being executed alongside him — while the people best placed to see it do not. Jesus prays before every turn in the road, the Holy Spirit is named at every stage, and from the middle of the book onward everything happens on one long journey toward Jerusalem, where he has already said he will be killed. The book ends where it began, in the temple — but it ends unfinished on purpose, with power still promised and a message still to be carried to every nation.

\\c 1
\\s1 Why This Book Was Written
\\p
\\v 1 Since many have undertaken to draw up an orderly account of the things that have been brought to fulfillment\\add [i.e. not simply things that happened, but promises kept]\\add* among us,
\\v 2 just as they were handed on to us by those who from the beginning were eyewitnesses and became servants of the word,
\\v 3 it seemed good to me also, having followed everything closely from the start, to write it out for you in order, most excellent Theophilus,\\f + \\fr 1:3 \\ft "Most excellent" (Greek \\tl kratiste\\tl*) is a form of address for a man of rank; this writer uses it in his second volume for the Roman governors Felix and Festus (Acts 23:26; 24:3; 26:25). The name Theophilus means "friend of God" or "loved by God."\\f*
\\v 4 so that you may know how secure the ground is\\add [i.e. that what you were taught will hold under weight]\\add* under the words in which you were instructed.
\\s1 A Priest and His Wife, Without a Child
\\p
\\v 5 There was, in the days of Herod king of Judea, a priest named Zechariah, of the division of Abijah;\\f + \\fr 1:5 \\ft David sorted the priests into twenty-four divisions that took turns serving at the temple, Abijah's being the eighth (1 Chronicles 24:10). Each division came up to Jerusalem for one week twice a year. Herod the Great, ruling Judea by appointment of the Roman senate, was an Idumean and no son of David — a detail worth holding on to, since the chapter is about to announce David's true heir.\\f* and he had a wife of the daughters of Aaron, and her name was Elizabeth.
\\v 6 And they were both righteous before God, walking blamelessly in all the commandments and requirements of the Lord.
\\v 7 And they had no child, because Elizabeth was barren,\\f + \\fr 1:7 \\ft A reader raised on Israel's scriptures hears this and knows what is coming. Sarah was barren, and bore Isaac; Rebekah, and bore Jacob; Rachel, and bore Joseph; Hannah, and bore Samuel. In every case God opened the womb and the child born was decisive for the nation. The couple's blamelessness is stated first so that no one reads the childlessness as punishment.\\f* and both of them were far along in their days.
\\p
\\v 8 And it happened, while he was serving as priest before God when his division came round,
\\v 9 that by the custom of the priesthood the lot fell to him to go into the sanctuary of the Lord and burn the incense.\\f + \\fr 1:9 \\ft Incense was burned on the golden altar in the Holy Place twice a day (Exodus 30:7-8). With so many priests eligible, the lot might fall to a given man once in his life, and never twice. The lot itself was not thought of as chance: "the lot is cast into the lap, but its every decision is from the LORD" (Proverbs 16:33).\\f*
\\v 10 And the whole gathering of the people was praying outside at the hour of the incense.\\add [i.e. the smoke going up inside while the prayers went up outside, the one standing for the other]\\add*
\\p
\\v 11 And an angel of the Lord appeared to him, standing at the right of the altar of incense.
\\v 12 And Zechariah was shaken when he saw him, and fear fell upon him.
\\v 13 But the angel said to him, "Do not be afraid, Zechariah, for your request has been heard, and your wife Elizabeth will bear you a son, and you shall call his name John.\\f + \\fr 1:13 \\ft The name (Hebrew \\tl Yohanan\\tl*) means "the LORD has shown grace." Naming was the father's right; here it is taken out of his hands and settled in advance.\\f*
\\v 14 And there will be joy for you and gladness, and many will rejoice at his birth,
\\v 15 for he will be great before the Lord. Wine and strong drink he shall not drink,\\f + \\fr 1:15 \\ft The terms of the Nazirite vow (Numbers 6:3) — a man set apart to God for a period, or in rare cases from birth. The same restriction was announced to Samson's mother before he was born (Judges 13:4-5, 7), and Samson too was promised to a woman who could not conceive.\\f* and he will be filled with the Holy Spirit while still in his mother's womb.
\\v 16 And many of the sons of Israel he will turn back to the Lord their God.
\\v 17 And he himself will go on ahead of him in the spirit and power of Elijah, to turn the hearts of fathers to their children and the disobedient into the good sense of the righteous, to make ready for the Lord a people prepared."\\f + \\fr 1:17 \\ft This is Malachi's closing promise quoted almost word for word: "I am going to send you Elijah the prophet before the coming of the great and terrible day of the LORD. He will turn the hearts of the fathers to the children" (Malachi 4:5-6), together with Malachi 3:1, "I am going to send my messenger, and he will clear the way before me." The prophetic voice had been silent for centuries. The angel is announcing that the clock has started.\\f*
\\p
\\v 18 And Zechariah said to the angel, "By what shall I know this? For I am an old man, and my wife is far along in her days."
\\v 19 And the angel answered and said to him, "I am Gabriel, the one who stands in the presence of God,\\f + \\fr 1:19 \\ft Gabriel appears in Daniel (8:16; 9:21) as the one sent to explain the visions and to lay out God's timetable for the end of Israel's exile. A reader who catches the name understands that the schedule Daniel was given is now being run.\\f* and I was sent to speak to you and to bring you this good news.
\\v 20 And look — you will be silent and unable to speak until the day these things happen, because you did not trust my words, which will be filled full in their season."
\\p
\\v 21 And the people were waiting for Zechariah, and they wondered at his long delay in the sanctuary.
\\v 22 And when he came out he could not speak to them,\\add [i.e. and so could not give them the blessing a priest was to speak over them at the end of the service]\\add* and they knew that he had seen a vision in the sanctuary. And he kept making signs to them, and he stayed mute.
\\v 23 And it happened, when the days of his service were completed, that he went away to his house.
\\p
\\v 24 And after those days Elizabeth his wife conceived, and she kept herself out of sight for five months, saying,
\\v 25 "This is what the Lord has done for me in the days when he looked on me, to take away my disgrace among men.\\add [i.e. the shame I have carried all these years for having no child]\\add*"
\\s1 Gabriel Comes to Mary
\\p
\\v 26 And in the sixth month the angel Gabriel was sent from God to a town of Galilee named Nazareth,
\\v 27 to a virgin promised in marriage to a man whose name was Joseph, of the house of David;\\f + \\fr 1:27 \\ft Betrothal was legally binding — the woman was already called the man's wife and it took a writ of divorce to undo — but the couple did not yet live together. Joseph's descent from David is stated here because everything the angel is about to say depends on it.\\f* and the virgin's name was Mary.
\\v 28 And coming in to her he said, "Rejoice, you who have been shown favor! The Lord is with you."\\f + \\fr 1:28 \\ft Most translations add "Blessed are you among women" here. The oldest Greek copies do not have the phrase at this point; it belongs to Elizabeth at 1:42, and later copyists moved it up into the angel's greeting.\\f*
\\v 29 But she was badly shaken at the word and kept turning over what sort of greeting this might be.
\\v 30 And the angel said to her, "Do not be afraid, Mary, for you have found favor with God.
\\v 31 And look — you will conceive in your womb and bear a son, and you shall call his name Jesus.\\f + \\fr 1:31 \\ft Greek \\tl Iēsous\\tl*, the Hebrew name \\tl Yeshua\\tl* (Joshua), "the LORD saves." Like John's, the name is assigned before conception and states in one word what the child is for.\\f*
\\v 32 He will be great, and will be called Son of the Most High; and the Lord God will give him the throne of David his father,
\\v 33 and he will reign over the house of Jacob into the ages, and of his kingdom there will be no end."\\f + \\fr 1:33 \\ft The angel is quoting Israel's charter documents. God swore to David, "I will establish the throne of his kingdom forever... I will be a father to him and he will be a son to me" (2 Samuel 7:13-14); Isaiah promised a child on David's throne whose government would have no end (Isaiah 9:6-7); Daniel saw a kingdom given to one like a son of man that "will not pass away" (Daniel 7:14). "Into the ages" is the Greek way of saying: with no limit set to it.\\f*
\\p
\\v 34 And Mary said to the angel, "How will this be, since I do not know a man?"
\\v 35 And the angel answered and said to her, "The Holy Spirit will come upon you, and the power of the Most High will overshadow you;\\f + \\fr 1:35 \\ft "Overshadow" is the word the Greek Old Testament uses for the cloud that settled on the tent of meeting when the glory of the LORD filled it (Exodus 40:35). It is not the language of a god fathering a child, as in the Greek myths; it is the language of God taking up residence.\\f* and so the holy one being born will be called Son of God.
\\v 36 And look — Elizabeth your relative has herself conceived a son in her old age, and this is the sixth month for her who was called barren.
\\v 37 For no word from God will ever be powerless."\\f + \\fr 1:37 \\ft The Greek word \\tl rhēma\\tl* carries the Hebrew \\tl davar\\tl* behind it, which means both "word" and "thing." The sentence therefore says at once that nothing God says can fail and that nothing God undertakes is impossible; English can only hold one of those at a time. The line is lifted from what was said to Abraham and Sarah about the birth of Isaac: "Is anything too wonderful for the LORD?" (Genesis 18:14).\\f*
\\v 38 And Mary said, "Here is the Lord's slave girl. Let it happen to me according to your word." And the angel went away from her.
\\s1 Mary Visits Elizabeth
\\p
\\v 39 And in those days Mary got up and went with haste into the hill country, to a town of Judah,
\\v 40 and she went into the house of Zechariah and greeted Elizabeth.
\\v 41 And it happened, when Elizabeth heard Mary's greeting, that the baby leaped in her womb; and Elizabeth was filled with the Holy Spirit,
\\v 42 and she called out with a loud cry and said, "Blessed are you among women,\\f + \\fr 1:42 \\ft The same superlative was spoken over Jael, who broke the power of an enemy army: "most blessed of women is Jael" (Judges 5:24), and over Judith in the later Jewish story (Judith 13:18). Both were women through whom Israel was delivered. Elizabeth is putting Mary in that company.\\f* and blessed is the fruit of your womb!
\\v 43 And how does this come to me, that the mother of my Lord should come to me?\\add [i.e. that the mother of the one I must call Lord should be the one making the journey to me]\\add*
\\v 44 For look — when the sound of your greeting came into my ears, the baby in my womb leaped for joy.
\\v 45 And blessed is she who trusted that there would be a completing of the things spoken to her from the Lord."\\add [i.e. unlike my husband, who was told the same kind of thing and could not]\\add*
\\s1 Mary's Song
\\p
\\v 46 And Mary said: "My whole self makes the Lord great,\\f + \\fr 1:46 \\ft This song is built almost line by line out of Hannah's prayer after the birth of Samuel (1 Samuel 2:1-10) — another woman who could not conceive, whose son turned Israel's history. Nearly every phrase in it can be found somewhere in Israel's scriptures. Mary is not composing; she is praying her people's words back to God because they have suddenly come true.\\f*
\\v 47 and my spirit has leaped for joy in God my Savior,
\\v 48 because he looked on the low place of his slave girl.\\add [i.e. exactly what Hannah asked of him — that he would look on the low condition of his servant]\\add* For look — from now on all generations will call me blessed,
\\v 49 because the Mighty One has done great things for me, and holy is his name,
\\v 50 and his mercy is on those who fear him from generation to generation.
\\v 51 He has done a strong thing with his arm;\\f + \\fr 1:51 \\ft "The arm of the LORD" is Exodus language — how God got Israel out of Egypt: "I will redeem you with an outstretched arm" (Exodus 6:6; Deuteronomy 4:34). The verbs from here to the end of the song are past tense, but they describe what God characteristically does and what he is doing now. It is the way prophets speak of a thing already decided.\\f* he has scattered the proud in the thoughts of their hearts.
\\v 52 He has pulled rulers down from their thrones and lifted up the low.
\\v 53 The hungry he has filled with good things, and the rich he has sent away empty.
\\v 54 He has taken hold of Israel his servant, remembering mercy,
\\v 55 as he spoke to our fathers, to Abraham and to his seed into the age."\\f + \\fr 1:55 \\ft The song does not end at David but goes back behind him to Abraham — to the promise that through his descendants all the families of the earth would be blessed (Genesis 12:3; 22:16-18). That is the widest promise in Israel's scriptures, and this writer's second volume is the story of it reaching the nations.\\f*
\\v 56 And Mary stayed with her about three months, and went back to her house.
\\s1 John Is Born and Named
\\p
\\v 57 Now the time was filled for Elizabeth to give birth, and she bore a son.
\\v 58 And her neighbors and her relatives heard that the Lord had made his mercy great toward her, and they rejoiced with her.
\\p
\\v 59 And it happened on the eighth day that they came to circumcise the child,\\f + \\fr 1:59 \\ft The eighth day was the day God set for circumcision in the covenant with Abraham (Genesis 17:12; Leviticus 12:3). Naming the boy after his father would have been the ordinary thing to do.\\f* and they were going to call him by his father's name, Zechariah.
\\v 60 And his mother answered and said, "No — he shall be called John."
\\v 61 And they said to her, "There is no one among your relatives who is called by this name."
\\v 62 And they made signs to his father, asking what he wanted him called.
\\v 63 And he asked for a little writing tablet and wrote, "John is his name."\\add [i.e. not what I choose to call him — what he is already called]\\add* And they all marveled.
\\v 64 And at once his mouth was opened and his tongue loosed, and he began to speak, blessing God.
\\v 65 And fear came on all those living around them, and in the whole hill country of Judea all these words were talked over,
\\v 66 and everyone who heard them laid them up in his heart, saying, "What then will this child be?" For indeed the hand of the Lord was with him.
\\s1 Zechariah's Prophecy
\\p
\\v 67 And Zechariah his father was filled with the Holy Spirit and prophesied, saying:
\\v 68 "Blessed be the Lord, the God of Israel, because he has visited his people\\f + \\fr 1:68 \\ft "Visited" does not mean dropped in to look. In the Greek Old Testament it is what God does when he steps in to act: "the LORD had visited the sons of Israel and had seen their affliction" (Exodus 4:31); "God will surely visit you and bring you up out of this land" (Genesis 50:24). It is a rescue word.\\f* and made a ransom for them,\\f + \\fr 1:68 \\ft Greek \\tl lytrōsis\\tl*, the price paid to buy a captive or a slave out. It is the standard word for what God did at the exodus (Psalm 111:9). Zechariah is not speaking in metaphors; he expects Israel to be bought out of bondage.\\f*
\\v 69 and has raised up a horn of rescue for us in the house of David his servant —\\f + \\fr 1:69 \\ft A horn is an animal's weapon, and in Israel's poetry it stands for the strength God gives his king: "he will exalt the horn of his anointed" (1 Samuel 2:10); "there I will make a horn sprout for David" (Psalm 132:17).\\f*
\\v 70 just as he spoke through the mouth of his holy prophets from of old —
\\v 71 rescue from our enemies and out of the hand of all who hate us,\\add [i.e. the nations that hold this land and the men who rule it for them]\\add*
\\v 72 to do mercy with our fathers and to remember his holy covenant,
\\v 73 the oath he swore to Abraham our father, to grant us
\\v 74 that we, rescued out of the hand of enemies, might serve him without fear,
\\v 75 in holiness and righteousness before him all our days.\\add [i.e. this is what the rescue is for — not freedom for its own sake, but the freedom to worship him]\\add*
\\p
\\v 76 And you, child, will be called prophet of the Most High, for you will go on ahead before the face of the Lord to make his roads ready,\\f + \\fr 1:76 \\ft Watch the pronouns. In Malachi 3:1 and Isaiah 40:3 it is the LORD's own road that the messenger prepares, and the LORD who then comes. Zechariah says his son will go ahead of "the Lord." The one coming behind John is put, without comment, in the place these prophecies reserve for God.\\f*
\\v 77 to give his people the knowledge of rescue in the forgiveness of their sins,\\add [i.e. so the rescue turns out to be from something deeper than Rome]\\add*
\\v 78 through the bowels of mercy of our God,\\f + \\fr 1:78 \\ft Greek \\tl splanchna\\tl*, the inward organs. Hebrew thought locates compassion in the gut, not the heart, and the physical word is kept here rather than smoothed into "tender mercy." It means mercy that is felt in the body.\\f* in which the dawn from on high\\f + \\fr 1:78 \\ft Greek \\tl anatolē\\tl* means both the rising of the sun and a shoot springing up, and in the Greek Old Testament it is the word for the promised Branch out of David's line (Jeremiah 23:5; Zechariah 3:8; 6:12). "Dawn" is taken here because of the shining in the next line, but the Branch is standing behind it.\\f* will visit us,\\f + \\fr 1:78 \\ft The oldest Greek has the future — the dawn "will visit"; most English versions have the past, "has visited."\\f*
\\v 79 to shine on those sitting in darkness and in the shadow of death,\\f + \\fr 1:79 \\ft Isaiah 9:2, spoken over the northern tribes after the Assyrians had wrecked them: "the people who walk in darkness have seen a great light." In Isaiah the light is followed at once by the promise of a child on David's throne.\\f* to guide our feet into the road of peace."
\\p
\\v 80 And the child grew and became strong in spirit, and he was in the deserted places until the day he was shown to Israel.\\add [i.e. formed in the wilderness, far from the temple where his father served]\\add*
\\c 2
\\s1 Born in the City of David
\\p
\\v 1 And it happened in those days that a decree went out from Caesar Augustus that the whole inhabited world should be registered.\\f + \\fr 2:1 \\ft Augustus (ruled 27 BC to AD 14) was hailed across the empire as savior, lord, and bringer of peace; an inscription from 9 BC calls his birthday the beginning of good news for the world. Every title the angels are about to give a baby in a feeding trough was already being given to the man who signed this decree.\\f*
\\v 2 This registration was the first, while Quirinius was governing Syria.\\f + \\fr 2:2 \\ft The Greek word can mean "first" or "before." Rendered here as "the first" registration; the other reading is "this registration was before Quirinius was governing Syria."\\f*
\\v 3 And everyone went to be registered, each to his own town.
\\p
\\v 4 And Joseph also went up from Galilee, out of the town of Nazareth, into Judea, to the city of David which is called Bethlehem, because he was of the house and family line of David,\\add [i.e. so an emperor's headcount is what puts David's heir in David's town]\\add*
\\v 5 to be registered along with Mary, who was promised in marriage to him and was pregnant.
\\v 6 And it happened, while they were there, that the days were filled for her to give birth,
\\v 7 and she bore her son, the firstborn, and wrapped him in strips of cloth and laid him in a feeding trough, because there was no space for them in the guest room.\\f + \\fr 2:7 \\ft The word rendered "guest room" (Greek \\tl katalyma\\tl*) is the same one used at 22:11 for the upstairs room where Jesus eats his last Passover. Most English versions have "inn" here. Either way the point stands: the family is put where the animals are fed.\\f*
\\s1 The Announcement to the Shepherds
\\p
\\v 8 And there were shepherds in that same region, out in the fields, keeping the watches of the night over their flock.\\add [i.e. men on a night shift, whose word was not accepted in a court of law]\\add*
\\v 9 And an angel of the Lord stood over them, and the glory of the Lord blazed around them, and they were afraid with a great fear.\\f + \\fr 2:9 \\ft "The glory of the LORD" is the visible presence of God — what settled on Sinai, filled the tent of meeting, and filled Solomon's temple (Exodus 24:16-17; 40:34-35; 1 Kings 8:10-11). It is now in an open field at night, over some hired men.\\f*
\\v 10 And the angel said to them, "Do not be afraid, for look — I bring you good news of a great joy which will be for all the people:
\\v 11 today there was born to you in the city of David a Savior, who is Christ the Lord.\\f + \\fr 2:11 \\ft Three titles at once. Savior and Lord were the emperor's, and "good news" was the word for an imperial bulletin; Christ (Greek \\tl Christos\\tl*, Hebrew \\tl mashiach\\tl*) is Israel's word for the king God installs by anointing. The sentence is a counter-announcement, and everyone who heard it in the Roman world would have known it.\\f*
\\v 12 And this is the sign for you: you will find a baby wrapped in strips of cloth and lying in a feeding trough."\\add [i.e. the sign is not a wonder but the sheer lowliness of the thing — look for the least likely place]\\add*
\\p
\\v 13 And suddenly there was with the angel a mass of the heavenly army, praising God and saying,
\\v 14 "Glory in the highest places to God, and on earth peace among men he has taken pleasure in."\\f + \\fr 2:14 \\ft The oldest Greek reads "men of his good pleasure" — that is, peace for the people God has favored, which is how it is rendered here. The later manuscripts followed by the King James have a slightly different ending on one word, giving "peace on earth, good will toward men."\\f*
\\s1 The Shepherds Go and Tell
\\p
\\v 15 And it happened, when the angels went away from them into heaven, that the shepherds began saying to one another, "Let us go over then as far as Bethlehem and see this word that has happened, which the Lord has made known to us."
\\v 16 And they came in a hurry and found both Mary and Joseph, and the baby lying in the feeding trough.
\\v 17 And when they saw it they made known the word that had been spoken to them about this child.
\\v 18 And all who heard marveled at the things the shepherds told them.
\\v 19 But Mary kept all these words, turning them over in her heart.\\add [i.e. holding them together and comparing them, as pieces of one thing not yet whole]\\add*
\\v 20 And the shepherds went back, giving glory and praise to God for everything they had heard and seen, just as it had been told to them.
\\s1 Circumcised, Named, and Presented
\\p
\\v 21 And when eight days were filled to circumcise him, his name was called Jesus, the name given by the angel before he was conceived in the womb.
\\p
\\v 22 And when the days of their cleansing were filled according to the law of Moses,\\f + \\fr 2:22 \\ft Leviticus 12 required a woman to wait forty days after the birth of a son and then bring an offering. Two separate obligations are being met in one trip: her purification, and the presenting of a firstborn son to God.\\f* they brought him up to Jerusalem to stand him before the Lord —
\\v 23 as it is written in the law of the Lord, "Every male that opens the womb shall be called holy to the Lord" —\\f + \\fr 2:23 \\ft Exodus 13:2, 12. Every firstborn son belonged to God because God spared Israel's firstborn on the night of the exodus. Parents normally bought the child back with five shekels (Numbers 18:15-16); this account does not mention the payment.\\f*
\\v 24 and to give a sacrifice according to what is said in the law of the Lord, "a pair of turtledoves or two young pigeons."\\add [i.e. the offering the law allowed for those who could not afford a lamb]\\add*
\\s1 Simeon Sees the Lord's Christ
\\p
\\v 25 And there was a man in Jerusalem whose name was Simeon, and this man was righteous and careful of God, waiting for the comfort of Israel,\\f + \\fr 2:25 \\ft "Comfort" here is the word Isaiah's great promise opens with: "Comfort, comfort my people, says your God... her hard service is completed" (Isaiah 40:1-2). To be waiting for the comfort of Israel is to be waiting for the exile to be over in earnest.\\f* and the Holy Spirit was upon him.
\\v 26 And it had been shown to him by the Holy Spirit that he would not see death before he had seen the Lord's Christ.
\\v 27 And he came in the Spirit into the temple; and when the parents brought in the child Jesus, to do for him what was customary under the law,
\\p
\\v 28 he took him into his arms and blessed God and said,
\\v 29 "Now, Master, you are letting your slave go in peace, according to your word,\\add [i.e. you promised I would not die before I saw this; I have seen it, and you may discharge me]\\add*
\\v 30 because my eyes have seen your rescue,
\\v 31 which you have made ready before the face of all the peoples —
\\v 32 a light for the unveiling of the nations, and glory for your people Israel."\\f + \\fr 2:32 \\ft Straight out of Isaiah's servant songs: "I will make you a light for the nations, so that my salvation may reach to the end of the earth" (Isaiah 49:6; also 42:6). The two halves are not in competition. Israel's calling was always to be the way the nations were reached, so light going out to them is Israel's glory, not its replacement.\\f*
\\p
\\v 33 And his father and his mother were marveling at the things being said about him.\\f + \\fr 2:33 \\ft The oldest Greek reads "his father and his mother." Most older English versions have "Joseph and his mother," a change made by later copyists uneasy at calling Joseph his father so soon after 1:35.\\f*
\\v 34 And Simeon blessed them and said to Mary his mother, "Look — this one is set for the falling and the rising of many in Israel, and for a sign that is spoken against —\\f + \\fr 2:34 \\ft Isaiah had said the LORD himself would be "a sanctuary, but a stone of stumbling and a rock of offense to both houses of Israel... and many will stumble over it and fall" (Isaiah 8:14-15). The same presence saves one man and trips another. Which it does depends on the response, not on the presence.\\f*
\\v 35 and a sword will go through your own self as well —\\add [i.e. what divides Israel will not spare you either]\\add* so that the reasonings out of many hearts may be uncovered."
\\s1 Anna Speaks of the Child
\\p
\\v 36 And there was a prophetess, Anna, daughter of Phanuel, of the tribe of Asher.\\f + \\fr 2:36 \\ft Prophetess puts her with Miriam, Deborah, and Huldah (Exodus 15:20; Judges 4:4; 2 Kings 22:14). Asher was one of the northern tribes carried off by Assyria in 722 BC; that a daughter of Asher is standing in the Jerusalem temple quietly says that not all of Israel was lost.\\f* She was far along in many days, having lived with a husband seven years from her virginity,
\\v 37 and then a widow to the age of eighty-four,\\f + \\fr 2:37 \\ft The Greek can equally mean "a widow for eighty-four years," which would make her well over a hundred. The age is taken here.\\f* who did not leave the temple, serving night and day with fastings and prayers.
\\v 38 And coming up at that very hour she began to thank God and to speak about him to everyone who was waiting for the ransoming of Jerusalem.
\\s1 The Boy in His Father's House
\\p
\\v 39 And when they had finished everything according to the law of the Lord, they went back to Galilee, to their own town of Nazareth.
\\v 40 And the child grew and became strong, being filled with wisdom; and the favor of God was upon him.
\\p
\\v 41 And his parents went to Jerusalem every year at the feast of the Passover.\\add [i.e. keeping the pilgrimage the law required, year after year]\\add*
\\v 42 And when he was twelve years old, they went up according to the custom of the feast.
\\p
\\v 43 And when they had completed the days, as they were going back, the boy Jesus stayed behind in Jerusalem, and his parents did not know it.\\f + \\fr 2:43 \\ft The oldest Greek reads "his parents"; most older English versions have "Joseph and his mother," the same softening as at 2:33.\\f*
\\v 44 Thinking he was in the traveling company, they went a day's journey, and then they went looking for him among their relatives and acquaintances.\\add [i.e. villages traveled to the feast in one large party, and a boy could be anywhere in it]\\add*
\\v 45 And not finding him, they went back to Jerusalem, still looking for him.
\\p
\\v 46 And it happened, after three days, that they found him in the temple, sitting in the middle of the teachers, both listening to them and questioning them.
\\v 47 And all who heard him were beside themselves at his understanding and his answers.
\\p
\\v 48 And when they saw him they were staggered, and his mother said to him, "Child, why have you done this to us? Look — your father and I have been searching for you in anguish."
\\v 49 And he said to them, "Why were you searching for me? Did you not know that I must be in what belongs to my Father?"\\f + \\fr 2:49 \\ft The Greek says only "in the things of my Father" and leaves it open — the house, or the business. "What belongs to my Father" is chosen to hold both. Note also the exchange: his mother has just said "your father," meaning Joseph; the first words of Jesus in this book quietly correct her about which father is meant. And "I must" is a word this writer will keep using for what God has fixed and cannot be got round (4:43; 9:22; 13:33; 24:7, 26, 44).\\f*
\\v 50 And they did not understand the word he spoke to them.
\\p
\\v 51 And he went down with them and came to Nazareth, and he was under their authority. And his mother kept all these words in her heart.
\\v 52 And Jesus advanced in wisdom and in growth and in favor with God and men.\\f + \\fr 2:52 \\ft Said almost word for word of the boy Samuel in the temple: "the boy Samuel kept growing, and was in favor both with the LORD and with men" (1 Samuel 2:26). Samuel grew up to anoint Israel's kings. This child is the one who gets anointed.\\f*
\\c 3
\\s1 The Word of God Comes to John
\\p
\\v 1 In the fifteenth year of the rule of Tiberius Caesar, while Pontius Pilate was governor of Judea, and Herod was tetrarch of Galilee, and Philip his brother tetrarch of the region of Ituraea and Trachonitis, and Lysanias tetrarch of Abilene,\\f + \\fr 3:1 \\ft The fifteenth year of Tiberius is about AD 28-29. A tetrarch is literally a "ruler of a fourth" — Herod the Great's kingdom was carved up among his sons, and none of them was allowed the title king. The list is not decoration: it is a map of a land parcelled out under foreign management.\\f*
\\v 2 in the high priesthood of Annas and Caiaphas,\\f + \\fr 3:2 \\ft There was supposed to be one high priest, holding office for life (Numbers 35:25). Annas had been appointed by a Roman governor and deposed by another; his son-in-law Caiaphas held the office, and Annas kept the influence. Rome had reached inside the priesthood itself.\\f* the word of God came upon John the son of Zechariah in the wilderness.\\f + \\fr 3:2 \\ft "The word of God came to" is the set formula for a prophet's call in Israel's scriptures — used of Jeremiah, Ezekiel, Hosea, Joel, Micah, Haggai and the rest. After naming seven men who hold all the power in the land, the sentence hands the word of God to none of them, but to a man out in the desert.\\f*
\\p
\\v 3 And he went into all the region around the Jordan, proclaiming an immersion of turning back for the forgiveness of sins,\\f + \\fr 3:3 \\ft Two words that will keep coming back. "Immersion" is Greek \\tl baptisma\\tl*, being put right under water; washings of that kind were ordinary in Jewish practice, but doing it to Israelites, out at the Jordan, treats the covenant people as though they were standing outside and had to come back in. "Turning back" is Greek \\tl metanoia\\tl*, rendered by the Hebrew idea underneath it, \\tl shub\\tl* — to turn round and go back the way you came. Not a feeling of regret; a change of direction.\\f*
\\v 4 as it is written in the book of the words of Isaiah the prophet: "A voice of one shouting in the wilderness: Get the way of the Lord ready, make his paths straight.
\\v 5 Every ravine shall be filled in, and every mountain and hill brought low; and the crooked shall be made straight, and the rough roads smooth;
\\v 6 and all flesh shall see the rescue of God."\\f + \\fr 3:6 \\ft The quotation is Isaiah 40:3-5, and this writer alone carries it through to the last line. In Isaiah the road is being levelled through the desert so that God can bring his people home from exile. "All flesh" — not Israel only — is where he chooses to stop, and it states the direction of both his volumes.\\f*
\\s1 Fruit That Matches the Turning
\\p
\\v 7 So he was saying to the crowds coming out to be immersed by him, "Brood of vipers! Who warned you to run from the wrath that is coming?
\\v 8 Then produce fruit that matches your turning back, and do not start saying among yourselves, 'We have Abraham as father.'\\add [i.e. do not fall back on your descent as though it settled the matter]\\add* For I tell you that God is able out of these stones to raise up children for Abraham.\\f + \\fr 3:8 \\ft In Aramaic, the everyday language, "stones" and "children" sound almost the same — \\tl avnayya\\tl* and \\tl benayya\\tl*. The point is not that God has no use for Abraham's family, but that God's promise to Abraham does not depend on any particular set of descendants keeping their place in it.\\f*
\\v 9 And already the axe is lying at the root of the trees; so every tree that does not produce good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire."
\\s1 What Then Shall We Do?
\\p
\\v 10 And the crowds were asking him, "What then shall we do?"
\\v 11 And he answered and said to them, "Whoever has two tunics, let him share with the one who has none, and whoever has food, let him do the same."
\\p
\\v 12 And tax collectors also came to be immersed, and they said to him, "Teacher, what shall we do?"
\\v 13 And he said to them, "Take in nothing more than what has been set for you."\\f + \\fr 3:13 \\ft Tax collecting was farmed out: a man bid for the right to collect in a district and made his living on whatever he could extract above the contracted sum. Skimming was not an abuse of the system, it was the system. John does not tell them to quit; he tells them to stop.\\f*
\\p
\\v 14 And soldiers too were asking him, "And what shall we do?" And he said to them, "Do not shake anyone down, do not squeeze anyone by threats, and be content with your pay."
\\s1 One Stronger Is Coming
\\p
\\v 15 And while the people were in suspense and everyone was reasoning in their hearts about John, whether he might be the Christ,
\\v 16 John answered them all: "I immerse you in water; but one stronger than I is coming, and I am not fit to untie the strap of his sandals.\\add [i.e. the one job so low that a Hebrew slave could not be made to do it]\\add* He will immerse you in the Holy Spirit and fire.\\f + \\fr 3:16 \\ft Spirit and fire belong together here, not as two options but as one work. The prophets pair them: Jerusalem is cleansed "by a spirit of judgment and a spirit of burning" (Isaiah 4:4), and the coming messenger is "like a refiner's fire," sitting down to purify the sons of Levi (Malachi 3:2-3). Water washes the outside; this goes through.\\f*
\\v 17 His winnowing fork is in his hand to clear his threshing floor right through and to gather the wheat into his barn, but the chaff he will burn up with fire that cannot be put out."\\add [i.e. the grain is thrown up into the wind, and what is light blows away]\\add*
\\p
\\v 18 So then with many other appeals he announced the good news to the people.\\add [i.e. and all of that — the axe, the fire, the winnowing — counts as good news, because it is God finally acting]\\add*
\\p
\\v 19 But Herod the tetrarch, being called to account by him over Herodias his brother's wife and over all the evil things Herod had done,\\f + \\fr 3:19 \\ft Herod Antipas had taken his brother's wife, which the law forbids outright (Leviticus 18:16; 20:21). Confronting the ruler is what prophets do — Nathan to David, Elijah to Ahab — and it is what gets them silenced.\\f*
\\v 20 added this on top of them all: he shut John up in prison.
\\s1 The Immersion of Jesus
\\p
\\v 21 And it happened, when all the people were immersed, and Jesus too had been immersed and was praying,\\add [i.e. taking his place in the line with everyone else]\\add* that heaven was opened
\\v 22 and the Holy Spirit came down on him in bodily form like a dove, and a voice came out of heaven: "You are my Son, the beloved; in you I have taken delight."\\f + \\fr 3:22 \\ft Two scriptures welded together. "You are my Son" is Psalm 2:7, spoken over the king of David's line at his crowning. "In you I have taken delight" opens the first of Isaiah's servant songs (Isaiah 42:1) — and that servant's road runs through suffering and rejection (Isaiah 52:13-53:12). The voice names him king and servant in one sentence, and the rest of the book shows that they are the same office. Note also that this writer has him praying when it happens; he will say the same at every hinge in the story.\\f*
\\s1 The Line of Descent, Back to Adam
\\p
\\v 23 And Jesus himself, when he began, was about thirty years old,\\f + \\fr 3:23 \\ft Thirty is the age at which men took up public office in Israel's memory: Joseph before Pharaoh, David when he began to reign, the Levites entering their service, Ezekiel when the visions came (Genesis 41:46; 2 Samuel 5:4; Numbers 4:3; Ezekiel 1:1).\\f* being the son, as it was thought, of Joseph,\\add [i.e. the legal father, which is what a genealogy records]\\add* of Eli,
\\v 24 of Matthat, of Levi, of Melchi, of Jannai, of Joseph,
\\v 25 of Mattathias, of Amos, of Nahum, of Esli, of Naggai,
\\v 26 of Maath, of Mattathias, of Semein, of Josech, of Joda,
\\v 27 of Joanan, of Rhesa, of Zerubbabel, of Shealtiel, of Neri,
\\v 28 of Melchi, of Addi, of Cosam, of Elmadam, of Er,
\\v 29 of Joshua,\\f + \\fr 3:29 \\ft The Greek here is \\tl Iēsous\\tl* — the same name as Jesus. Rendered "Joshua" to show that it is a common Hebrew name; English versions print it variously as Jose, Jesus, or Joshua.\\f* of Eliezer, of Jorim, of Matthat, of Levi,
\\v 30 of Simeon, of Judah, of Joseph, of Jonam, of Eliakim,
\\v 31 of Melea, of Menna, of Mattatha, of Nathan, of David,\\f + \\fr 3:31 \\ft The line runs through Nathan, another son of David (2 Samuel 5:14), not through Solomon and the royal succession. It establishes descent from David without running through the kings who took Israel into exile.\\f*
\\v 32 of Jesse, of Obed, of Boaz, of Sala, of Nahshon,
\\v 33 of Amminadab, of Admin, of Arni, of Hezron, of Perez, of Judah,\\f + \\fr 3:33 \\ft The oldest Greek has "Amminadab, Admin, Arni, Hezron." Most older English versions have "Aminadab, Aram, Esrom," one name fewer, harmonized with the list at Ruth 4:19 and Matthew 1:3-4.\\f*
\\v 34 of Jacob, of Isaac, of Abraham, of Terah, of Nahor,
\\v 35 of Serug, of Reu, of Peleg, of Eber, of Shelah,
\\v 36 of Cainan, of Arphaxad, of Shem, of Noah, of Lamech,
\\v 37 of Methuselah, of Enoch, of Jared, of Mahalalel, of Cainan,
\\v 38 of Enosh, of Seth, of Adam, of God.\\f + \\fr 3:38 \\ft Matthew's list runs forward from Abraham; this one runs backward past Abraham to the first man, and past him to God. The voice from heaven has just said "You are my Son"; the list ends by calling Adam a son of God. A reader is meant to put the two together, and to notice that the reach of this story is the whole human race, not one nation in it.\\f*
\\c 4
\\s1 Tested in the Wilderness
\\p
\\v 1 And Jesus, full of the Holy Spirit, went back from the Jordan and was led in the Spirit through the wilderness
\\v 2 for forty days, being tested by the devil.\\f + \\fr 4:2 \\ft Forty is the number of testing and formation: Israel forty years in the wilderness, Moses forty days on the mountain, Elijah forty days to Horeb. Israel was called God's son and taken into the desert to be tested there, and failed — grumbling for food, demanding proof, worshipping a calf. The same three tests are about to be run again on another Son, and all three of his answers come out of Deuteronomy's account of those failures.\\f* And he ate nothing in those days, and when they were finished he was hungry.
\\v 3 And the devil said to him, "If you are Son of God, tell this stone to become bread."\\add [i.e. since you are — so use it, and feed yourself]\\add*
\\v 4 And Jesus answered him, "It is written: 'Man shall not live on bread alone.'"\\f + \\fr 4:4 \\ft Deuteronomy 8:3, from Moses' account of why God let Israel go hungry in the desert and then fed them with manna. Most translations continue "but by every word of God"; the oldest Greek copies of this Gospel end the answer at "bread alone."\\f*
\\p
\\v 5 And leading him up, he showed him all the kingdoms of the inhabited world in a point of time,
\\v 6 and the devil said to him, "To you I will give all this authority and their glory, because it has been handed over to me and I give it to whomever I want.
\\v 7 So if you will bow down before me, it will all be yours."\\add [i.e. the throne without the road that leads to it]\\add*
\\v 8 And Jesus answered and said to him, "It is written: 'You shall bow down to the Lord your God, and him only shall you serve.'"\\f + \\fr 4:8 \\ft Deuteronomy 6:13, part of Moses' warning to Israel not to turn to other gods once they were settled and prosperous — which is exactly what Israel then did. Many translations begin the answer with "Get behind me, Satan"; the oldest Greek does not have those words here.\\f*
\\p
\\v 9 And he led him into Jerusalem and stood him on the point of the temple and said to him, "If you are Son of God, throw yourself down from here,
\\v 10 for it is written: 'He will give his angels orders about you, to keep you safe,'
\\v 11 and 'On their hands they will carry you, so that you never strike your foot against a stone.'"\\f + \\fr 4:11 \\ft Psalm 91:11-12, quoted accurately. A promise of God's protection is being turned into a lever: engineer the emergency, and God will have to act. That is not trust, it is a demand for proof on the tester's terms.\\f*
\\v 12 And Jesus answered and said to him, "It has been said: 'You shall not put the Lord your God to the test.'"\\f + \\fr 4:12 \\ft Deuteronomy 6:16, which finishes "as you tested him at Massah" — the place where Israel, thirsty, demanded that God prove he was among them (Exodus 17:1-7). The name Massah means "testing."\\f*
\\v 13 And when the devil had finished every test, he stood off from him until a fitting time.\\add [i.e. he has not gone; he is waiting for his moment]\\add*
\\s1 Back to Galilee
\\p
\\v 14 And Jesus went back in the power of the Spirit into Galilee, and a report about him went out through the whole surrounding country.
\\v 15 And he was teaching in their synagogues, and everyone spoke well of him.
\\s1 In the Synagogue at Nazareth
\\p
\\v 16 And he came to Nazareth, where he had been brought up, and as was his custom he went into the synagogue on the day of the Sabbath, and he stood up to read.\\f + \\fr 4:16 \\ft A synagogue Sabbath service included a reading from the law, a reading from the prophets, and a word of explanation, and a competent visitor could be invited to give it. The reader stood; the one expounding sat down (see verse 20).\\f*
\\v 17 And the scroll of the prophet Isaiah was handed to him, and unrolling the scroll he found the place where it was written:
\\v 18 "The Spirit of the Lord is upon me, because he anointed me to announce good news to the poor. He has sent me to proclaim release to the prisoners of war and new sight to the blind, to send away in release those who have been crushed,\\f + \\fr 4:18 \\ft The reading is Isaiah 61:1-2 with a line from Isaiah 58:6 folded in — "to let the crushed go free," from Isaiah's account of the fast God actually wants. Some translations also include "to heal the brokenhearted"; the oldest Greek copies of this Gospel do not have it here.\\f*
\\v 19 to proclaim the year of the Lord's welcome."\\f + \\fr 4:19 \\ft The Jubilee. Every fiftieth year debts were cancelled, slaves went free, and land went back to the families that had lost it (Leviticus 25). Isaiah had taken that legislation and made it a picture of what God would do when he finally set things right. Note where the reading stops: the next words in Isaiah are "and the day of vengeance of our God," and he does not read them.\\f*
\\p
\\v 20 And rolling up the scroll, he gave it back to the attendant and sat down; and the eyes of everyone in the synagogue were fastened on him.
\\v 21 And he began to say to them, "Today this scripture has been filled full in your ears."\\add [i.e. not someday, and not somewhere else — here, now, while you are listening to it]\\add*
\\v 22 And they were all speaking well of him and marveling at the words of favor coming out of his mouth, and they were saying, "Is this not Joseph's son?"
\\p
\\v 23 And he said to them, "No doubt you will quote me this proverb: 'Doctor, heal yourself. What we have heard happened in Capernaum, do here in your own hometown too.'"
\\v 24 And he said, "Amen, I say to you, no prophet is welcome in his own hometown.
\\s1 Elijah and Elisha Were Sent Outside Israel
\\p
\\v 25 But in truth I tell you, there were many widows in Israel in the days of Elijah, when the sky was shut for three years and six months and a great famine came over the whole land,
\\v 26 and to none of them was Elijah sent — only to Zarephath in Sidon, to a widow woman.\\f + \\fr 4:26 \\ft 1 Kings 17:8-24. Sidon was Gentile territory, and the home country of Jezebel. When Israel would not hear the prophet, God sent him to a foreign widow, kept her alive through the famine, and raised her son from the dead.\\f*
\\v 27 And there were many lepers in Israel in the time of Elisha the prophet, and none of them was cleansed — only Naaman the Syrian."\\f + \\fr 4:27 \\ft 2 Kings 5:1-14. Naaman was not merely a foreigner but the commanding general of an enemy army. These two examples are what turns the room, and they state in advance the pattern of both this book and its sequel: where the message is refused, it goes on to those who will take it, even outside Israel.\\f*
\\s1 The Town Turns on Him
\\p
\\v 28 And all in the synagogue were filled with rage when they heard these things,
\\v 29 and they got up and threw him out of the town and brought him to the brow of the hill their town was built on, so as to hurl him off it.
\\v 30 But he passed through the middle of them and went on his way.
\\s1 Authority at Capernaum
\\p
\\v 31 And he went down to Capernaum, a town of Galilee, and he was teaching them on the Sabbaths.
\\v 32 And they were struck out of themselves at his teaching, because his word carried authority.\\add [i.e. a scribe taught by citing which teacher had said what; this man cited no one]\\add*
\\p
\\v 33 And in the synagogue there was a man who had the spirit of an unclean demon, and he shouted out with a loud voice,
\\v 34 "Ha! What is there between us and you,\\add [i.e. what business have you with us]\\add* Jesus the Nazarene? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are — the Holy One of God!"\\f + \\fr 4:34 \\ft "What is there between us and you" is a stock protest against interference (Judges 11:12; 1 Kings 17:18; 2 Kings 3:13). Notice who is speaking. The town that heard him read Isaiah could not see it; the unclean spirit names him exactly.\\f*
\\v 35 And Jesus rebuked it: "Be muzzled, and come out of him."\\f + \\fr 4:35 \\ft "Be muzzled" is the word for putting a muzzle on an ox. He silences the demon even though what it said was true: his identity is not going to be published by the powers he came to break.\\f* And the demon threw him down in front of them all and came out of him, doing him no harm.
\\p
\\v 36 And astonishment came on them all, and they were saying to one another, "What is this word? For with authority and power he gives orders to the unclean spirits and they come out!"
\\v 37 And a report about him went out into every place in the surrounding country.
\\p
\\v 38 And getting up, he left the synagogue and went into Simon's house.\\add [i.e. Simon, who has not yet been called and is not yet part of the story]\\add* And Simon's mother-in-law was gripped by a high fever, and they asked him about her.
\\v 39 And standing over her, he rebuked the fever and it left her; and at once she got up and began serving them.
\\p
\\v 40 And when the sun was going down,\\add [i.e. once the Sabbath was over and the sick could be carried]\\add* all who had any sick with various diseases brought them to him; and he laid his hands on each one of them and healed them.
\\v 41 And demons also came out of many, shouting and saying, "You are the Son of God!" And he rebuked them and would not let them speak, because they knew that he was the Christ.
\\p
\\v 42 And when day came he went out and made his way into a deserted place; and the crowds were searching for him and came right up to him, and they tried to hold him back from going away from them.
\\v 43 But he said to them, "I must announce the good news of the kingdom of God to the other towns as well, because that is what I was sent for."\\f + \\fr 4:43 \\ft The first mention of "the kingdom of God" in this book, and the first of many uses of "I must" for what God has determined. The kingdom is not a place and not an afterlife; it means God reigning, taking up his rule in the open, which is what Israel had been waiting for (Isaiah 52:7; Daniel 2:44).\\f*
\\v 44 And he was proclaiming in the synagogues of Judea.\\f + \\fr 4:44 \\ft The oldest Greek reads "of Judea"; most older English versions have "of Galilee." This writer sometimes uses "Judea" broadly for the whole Jewish homeland (compare Acts 10:37).\\f*
\\c 5
\\s1 The Catch of Fish
\\p
\\v 1 And it happened, while the crowd was pressing in on him and listening to the word of God, that he was standing beside the lake of Gennesaret,
\\v 2 and he saw two boats standing at the edge of the lake; the fishermen had gotten out of them and were washing their nets.
\\v 3 And getting into one of the boats, which was Simon's, he asked him to put out a little from the land; and sitting down, he taught the crowds from the boat.\\add [i.e. a few yards of water turning the shore into a theater]\\add*
\\p
\\v 4 And when he stopped speaking, he said to Simon, "Put out into the deep water and let down your nets for a catch."
\\v 5 And Simon answered and said, "Master, we worked hard through the whole night and took nothing;\\add [i.e. and night is when the fish are there, so the day is hopeless]\\add* but on your word I will let down the nets."
\\v 6 And when they had done this, they closed in a great mass of fish, and their nets were beginning to tear.
\\v 7 And they signaled to their partners in the other boat to come and help them; and they came and filled both the boats, so that they were sinking.
\\p
\\v 8 When Simon Peter saw it, he fell down at Jesus' knees and said, "Go away from me, Lord, because I am a sinful man."\\f + \\fr 5:8 \\ft The standard reaction in Israel's scriptures to standing too close to God. Isaiah in the temple: "Woe is me, I am ruined, for I am a man of unclean lips" (Isaiah 6:5). Manoah: "We will surely die, for we have seen God" (Judges 13:22). Note also that Simon has moved from "Master" in verse 5 to "Lord" here.\\f*
\\v 9 For amazement had gripped him and all those with him at the catch of fish they had taken,
\\v 10 and so too James and John, sons of Zebedee, who were partners with Simon. And Jesus said to Simon, "Do not be afraid. From now on you will be taking men alive."\\f + \\fr 5:10 \\ft The Greek verb means to take captive alive rather than kill — what is done to prisoners who are spared. It is not the "fishers of men" image of Mark 1:17 and Matthew 4:19, and the difference matters: fish caught die, and these are caught in order to live.\\f*
\\v 11 And bringing the boats to land, they left everything and followed him.\\add [i.e. boats, nets, and the largest catch of their lives, all left on the beach]\\add*
\\s1 A Man Full of Leprosy
\\p
\\v 12 And it happened, while he was in one of the towns, that there was a man full of leprosy.\\f + \\fr 5:12 \\ft The word covers a range of disfiguring skin conditions. Under the law a man in that state lived outside the community, wore torn clothes, and called out "unclean" to warn people off (Leviticus 13:45-46). He was cut off from worship, family, and touch. Anyone who touched him became unclean too.\\f* When he saw Jesus he fell on his face and begged him: "Lord, if you are willing, you can make me clean."
\\v 13 And stretching out his hand he touched him\\add [i.e. the one thing no one had done to him in years]\\add* and said, "I am willing; be cleansed." And immediately the leprosy left him.
\\v 14 And he ordered him to tell no one — "but go, show yourself to the priest and bring the offering for your cleansing, as Moses commanded, as evidence to them."\\f + \\fr 5:14 \\ft Leviticus 14 lays out the procedure: the priest does not heal, he inspects and certifies, and only then is the man restored to the community. Jesus sends him through the proper channel — and the priests will have to certify a cure that the king of Israel once said only God could give (2 Kings 5:7).\\f*
\\p
\\v 15 But the word about him spread all the more, and great crowds were coming together to hear and to be healed of their sicknesses.
\\v 16 But he himself would withdraw into the deserted places and pray.\\add [i.e. as a habit, not once — the busier it became, the more he went off alone]\\add*
\\s1 The Paralyzed Man Let Down Through the Roof
\\p
\\v 17 And it happened on one of the days that he was teaching, and Pharisees and teachers of the law were sitting there who had come from every village of Galilee and Judea and from Jerusalem; and the power of the Lord was there for him to heal.\\add [i.e. this is no local gathering; they have come from everywhere to look him over]\\add*
\\v 18 And look — men carrying on a bed a man who was paralyzed, and they were trying to bring him in and set him before him.
\\v 19 And finding no way to bring him in because of the crowd, they went up on the roof and let him down through the tiles, bed and all, into the middle, right in front of Jesus.\\add [i.e. up the outside stair, and then digging through someone's roof]\\add*
\\p
\\v 20 And seeing their trust, he said, "Man, your sins have been forgiven you."
\\v 21 And the scribes and the Pharisees began to reason: "Who is this who speaks blasphemies? Who can forgive sins except God alone?"\\add [i.e. and they are right — that is exactly the question]\\add*
\\p
\\v 22 But Jesus, knowing their reasonings, answered and said to them, "What are you reasoning about in your hearts?
\\v 23 Which is easier, to say, 'Your sins have been forgiven you,' or to say, 'Get up and walk'?\\add [i.e. one of them nobody can check, and the other everybody can]\\add*
\\v 24 But so that you may know that the son of man has authority on the earth to forgive sins" — he said to the paralyzed man, "I say to you, get up, pick up your bed and go to your house."\\f + \\fr 5:24 \\ft "Son of man" appears here for the first time in this book. In Ezekiel it is simply how God addresses the prophet — "mortal," "human being." But in Daniel 7:13-14 "one like a son of man" comes on the clouds to the Ancient of Days and is given dominion, glory, and a kingdom that will not pass away. Both senses are alive in the way Jesus uses it of himself, and which one is uppermost varies with the saying.\\f*
\\v 25 And at once he stood up in front of them, picked up what he had been lying on, and went off to his house glorifying God.
\\v 26 And astonishment took hold of them all, and they were glorifying God; and they were filled with fear and said, "We have seen strange things today."
\\s1 Levi Leaves the Tax Booth
\\p
\\v 27 And after this he went out and saw a tax collector named Levi sitting at the tax booth, and he said to him, "Follow me."
\\v 28 And leaving everything behind, he got up and followed him.\\add [i.e. and a man who walks away from a tax contract cannot ask for it back]\\add*
\\p
\\v 29 And Levi made a great reception for him in his house, and there was a large crowd of tax collectors and others reclining at table with them.
\\v 30 And the Pharisees and their scribes were grumbling at his disciples: "Why do you eat and drink with the tax collectors and sinners?"\\f + \\fr 5:30 \\ft Sharing a meal was not a casual thing. To eat with people was to accept them and be counted with them. The objection is not that he teaches such people or heals them but that he sits down at their table as though they belonged.\\f*
\\v 31 And Jesus answered and said to them, "The healthy have no need of a doctor, but those who are sick do.
\\v 32 I have not come to call righteous people but sinners, to turning back."
\\s1 Fasting While the Bridegroom Is Here
\\p
\\v 33 And they said to him, "The disciples of John fast often and make their prayers, and so do those of the Pharisees, but yours eat and drink."
\\v 34 And Jesus said to them, "You cannot make the sons of the wedding hall\\add [i.e. the groom's own wedding party]\\add* fast while the bridegroom is with them, can you?\\f + \\fr 5:34 \\ft A wedding ran seven days and fasting during it was out of the question. But the image has an edge: in the prophets the bridegroom of Israel is God himself (Isaiah 54:5; Hosea 2:19). He is explaining why his people are not fasting, and in the explanation he takes that place.\\f*
\\v 35 But days will come — and when the bridegroom is taken away from them, then they will fast, in those days."\\add [i.e. taken away, not departing; this is the first hint of how it ends]\\add*
\\s1 New Wine, New Skins
\\p
\\v 36 And he told them a parable as well: "No one tears a patch out of a new garment and puts it on an old one; if he does, he will tear the new, and the patch from the new will not match the old.
\\v 37 And no one puts new wine into old wineskins; if he does, the new wine will burst the skins and be spilled out, and the skins will be ruined.\\add [i.e. wine still working needs a skin that can still stretch]\\add*
\\v 38 But new wine must be put into new skins.\\f + \\fr 5:38 \\ft Many manuscripts add "and both are preserved" at the end of the verse; the oldest Greek does not.\\f*
\\v 39 And no one who has been drinking old wine wants new, for he says, 'The old is good.'"\\add [i.e. and that, not stupidity, is why you cannot taste what I am pouring]\\add*
\\c 6
\\s1 Lord of the Sabbath
\\p
\\v 1 And it happened on a Sabbath that he was going through grainfields, and his disciples were picking the heads of grain and eating them, rubbing them in their hands.\\f + \\fr 6:1 \\ft Taking grain by hand from a neighbor's standing crop was expressly allowed (Deuteronomy 23:25). The objection is not theft but the day: picking could be classed as reaping and rubbing as threshing, both of them work. Some manuscripts date this to "the second-first sabbath," a term nobody has ever satisfactorily explained; the oldest Greek says simply "a Sabbath."\\f*
\\v 2 And some of the Pharisees said, "Why are you doing what is not permitted on the Sabbath?"
\\v 3 And Jesus answered them, "Have you not even read this — what David did when he was hungry, he and those with him,
\\v 4 how he went into the house of God and took the bread of the presence and ate it and gave it to those with him, which it is not permitted for anyone to eat except the priests alone?"\\f + \\fr 6:4 \\ft 1 Samuel 21:1-6. The twelve loaves set out before the LORD were reserved for the priests (Leviticus 24:5-9), and the account of David eating them carries no word of blame. The choice of example is not random: David was the anointed king, and what his men could do under his authority is the analogy being drawn.\\f*
\\v 5 And he said to them, "The son of man is lord of the Sabbath."\\add [i.e. the day is not yours to fence; it is his to define]\\add*
\\s1 Doing Good on the Sabbath
\\p
\\v 6 And it happened on another Sabbath that he went into the synagogue and taught. And a man was there whose right hand was withered.
\\v 7 And the scribes and the Pharisees were watching him closely to see whether he would heal on the Sabbath, so that they might find something to accuse him of.\\add [i.e. the man in front of them is not a person in need but a test case]\\add*
\\v 8 But he knew their reasonings, and he said to the man with the withered hand, "Get up and stand in the middle." And he got up and stood there.
\\p
\\v 9 And Jesus said to them, "I ask you whether it is permitted on the Sabbath to do good or to do harm, to rescue a life or to destroy it."\\add [i.e. there is no neutral option; refusing to heal is itself a choice]\\add*
\\v 10 And looking around at them all, he said to him, "Stretch out your hand." And he did, and his hand was restored.\\add [i.e. done with a word only, so that there was no work anyone could point to]\\add*
\\v 11 But they were filled with senseless fury and were talking with one another about what they might do to Jesus.
\\s1 He Chooses Twelve
\\p
\\v 12 And it happened in those days that he went out to the mountain to pray, and he spent the whole night in prayer to God.
\\v 13 And when day came, he called his disciples to him and chose twelve of them, whom he also named apostles:\\f + \\fr 6:13 \\ft "Apostle" means one sent out with the sender's authority. The number is the point: twelve tribes, twelve sons of Jacob. Choosing twelve is a claim to be putting Israel back together, and the prophets had promised exactly that gathering (Ezekiel 37:15-28; Isaiah 49:6).\\f*
\\v 14 Simon, whom he also named Peter, and Andrew his brother, and James and John, and Philip and Bartholomew,
\\v 15 and Matthew and Thomas, and James son of Alphaeus, and Simon called the Zealot,\\add [i.e. a tax collector and a man of the resistance party, in the same twelve]\\add*
\\v 16 and Judas son of James, and Judas Iscariot, who became a traitor.\\add [i.e. became one; he was not one when he was chosen]\\add*
\\s1 He Comes Down to the Crowds
\\p
\\v 17 And going down with them, he stood on a level place, and there was a great crowd of his disciples and a great mass of people from all Judea and Jerusalem and the coastland of Tyre and Sidon,\\add [i.e. including Gentile territory — the same coast Elijah was sent to]\\add*
\\v 18 who came to hear him and to be healed of their diseases; and those troubled by unclean spirits were being cured.
\\v 19 And the whole crowd was trying to touch him, because power was going out from him and healing them all.
\\s1 Blessings on the Poor
\\p
\\v 20 And he lifted up his eyes on his disciples and said: "Blessed are you who are poor, because yours is the kingdom of God.\\f + \\fr 6:20 \\ft "Blessed" is not a description of how someone feels. It is a declaration that a person stands where God wants them, however things look. And the poor here are simply the poor — this account has no "in spirit." They are the ones Isaiah 61 named as the first hearers of the good news, and Jesus has already claimed that text as his job (4:18).\\f*
\\v 21 Blessed are you who hunger now, because you will be filled. Blessed are you who weep now, because you will laugh.
\\v 22 Blessed are you when men hate you, and when they cut you off and insult you and throw out your name as evil,\\add [i.e. put out of the synagogue and your name blackened]\\add* on account of the son of man.
\\v 23 Rejoice in that day and leap for joy, for look — your pay is great in heaven; for their fathers did the same things to the prophets.
\\s1 Woes on the Rich
\\p
\\v 24 But woe to you who are rich, because you have your comfort paid in full.\\f + \\fr 6:24 \\ft "Paid in full" is the word written on a receipt: the account is settled, there is nothing further owing. The blessings and the woes match one for one, and together they are Mary's song turned into direct address (1:52-53).\\f*
\\v 25 Woe to you who are full now, because you will go hungry. Woe to you who laugh now, because you will mourn and weep.
\\v 26 Woe when all men speak well of you, for their fathers did the same things to the false prophets.\\add [i.e. being liked by everybody puts you in the line of the men who told kings what they wanted to hear]\\add*
\\s1 Love Your Enemies
\\p
\\v 27 But I say to you who are listening: love your enemies, do good to those who hate you,
\\v 28 bless those who curse you, pray for those who treat you badly.
\\v 29 To the one who strikes you on the cheek, hold out the other as well; and from the one who takes away your coat, do not hold back your tunic either.
\\v 30 Give to everyone who asks you, and from the one who takes what is yours, do not demand it back.
\\v 31 And just as you want men to do to you, do the same to them.
\\p
\\v 32 And if you love those who love you, what favor is that to you? For even sinners love those who love them.
\\v 33 And if you do good to those who do good to you, what favor is that to you? Even sinners do the same.
\\v 34 And if you lend to those you hope to get it back from, what favor is that to you? Even sinners lend to sinners, to get back the same amount.
\\v 35 But love your enemies, and do good, and lend expecting nothing back;\\f + \\fr 6:35 \\ft The Greek can mean either "expecting nothing back" or "despairing of no one." The first is taken here. Behind it stands the law's provision for cancelling debts every seventh year and its warning against refusing a loan because the year of release is near (Deuteronomy 15:1-11).\\f* and your pay will be great, and you will be sons of the Most High, because he himself is kind to the ungrateful and the evil.\\add [i.e. and that is what makes you recognizable as his — you act the way he acts]\\add*
\\p
\\v 36 Be full of pity, just as your Father is full of pity.
\\v 37 And do not judge, and you will never be judged; and do not condemn, and you will never be condemned; let go, and you will be let go.
\\v 38 Give, and it will be given to you: a good measure, pressed down, shaken together, running over, they will pour into your lap.\\add [i.e. into the fold of your garment, the way a grain seller fills a measure past the brim]\\add* For with the measure you measure with, it will be measured back to you."
\\s1 What Comes Out Shows What Is Inside
\\p
\\v 39 And he told them a parable as well: "A blind man cannot lead a blind man, can he? Will they not both fall into a pit?
\\v 40 A disciple is not above the teacher, but everyone fully trained will be like his teacher.\\add [i.e. so it matters enormously whose eyes you are borrowing]\\add*
\\p
\\v 41 And why do you look at the splinter in your brother's eye, and not notice the plank in your own eye?\\add [i.e. a chip of straw against a roof beam]\\add*
\\v 42 How can you say to your brother, 'Brother, let me take out the splinter in your eye,' when you yourself do not see the plank in your own eye? Hypocrite! First take the plank out of your own eye, and then you will see clearly to take out the splinter in your brother's eye.\\f + \\fr 6:42 \\ft "Hypocrite" is the Greek word for a stage actor — a man performing a part behind a mask. The instruction is not to leave your brother alone; it is that self-examination has to come first, because without it the help is blind.\\f*
\\p
\\v 43 For there is no good tree producing rotten fruit, nor again a rotten tree producing good fruit.
\\v 44 For each tree is known by its own fruit; figs are not gathered from thorns, nor are grapes picked from a bramble bush.
\\v 45 The good man out of the good treasure of his heart brings out what is good, and the evil man out of the evil brings out what is evil; for his mouth speaks out of the overflow of the heart.\\f + \\fr 6:45 \\ft "Heart" in Hebrew thinking is not the seat of the feelings but the center of the person — mind, will, and character together. What is stored there is what eventually comes out of the mouth.\\f*
\\s1 The Two Builders
\\p
\\v 46 And why do you call me 'Lord, Lord,' and not do what I say?
\\v 47 Everyone who comes to me and hears my words and does them — I will show you what he is like.
\\v 48 He is like a man building a house, who dug and went down deep and laid a foundation on the rock. And when a flood came, the river burst against that house and could not shake it, because it had been well built.\\f + \\fr 6:48 \\ft The oldest Greek gives the reason as "because it had been well built"; the later manuscripts followed by the King James have "for it was founded on a rock."\\f*
\\v 49 But the one who hears and does not do is like a man who built a house on the ground with no foundation, and the river burst against it, and at once it fell in, and the ruin of that house was great."
\\c 7
\\s1 A Centurion's Trust
\\p
\\v 1 When he had finished all his words in the hearing of the people, he went into Capernaum.
\\v 2 And a certain centurion had a slave who was dear to him and who was sick and about to die.\\f + \\fr 7:2 \\ft A centurion commanded roughly a hundred men. He is a Gentile and an officer of the occupying power, and the note that the slave was "dear to him" is itself unusual — under Roman law a slave was property.\\f*
\\v 3 And when he heard about Jesus, he sent elders of the Jews to him, asking him to come and bring his slave safely through.
\\v 4 And when they came to Jesus they begged him earnestly, saying, "He is worthy for you to do this for him,
\\v 5 for he loves our nation, and he built the synagogue for us himself."\\add [i.e. their argument is that he has earned it]\\add*
\\p
\\v 6 And Jesus went with them. And when he was already not far from the house, the centurion sent friends to say to him, "Lord, do not put yourself to trouble, for I am not fit to have you come in under my roof.
\\v 7 That is why I did not think myself worthy to come to you.\\add [i.e. exactly the opposite of what the elders said on my behalf]\\add* But say it with a word, and let my boy be healed.
\\v 8 For I too am a man set under authority, with soldiers under me, and I say to this one, 'Go,' and he goes, and to another, 'Come,' and he comes, and to my slave, 'Do this,' and he does it."\\add [i.e. I know how a word works when there is authority behind it, and I can see what stands behind yours]\\add*
\\v 9 When Jesus heard this he marveled at him, and turning to the crowd following him he said, "I tell you, not even in Israel have I found trust like this."
\\v 10 And those who had been sent went back to the house and found the slave in good health.
\\s1 The Widow's Son at Nain
\\p
\\v 11 And it happened soon afterward that he went to a town called Nain, and his disciples and a large crowd were going with him.
\\v 12 And as he came near the gate of the town, look — a dead man was being carried out, the only son of his mother, and she was a widow;\\add [i.e. a woman with no husband and now no son, and so no one at all]\\add* and a considerable crowd from the town was with her.
\\v 13 And when the Lord saw her, his heart went out to her,\\f + \\fr 7:13 \\ft The Greek verb is built on the word for the inward organs — a wrench in the gut, not a sentiment. In the Greek Old Testament that kind of compassion is characteristically God's (Psalm 103:13; Isaiah 49:15). Nobody asked him for anything; he saw her and moved.\\f* and he said to her, "Do not weep."
\\p
\\v 14 And going up, he touched the bier, and the bearers stood still;\\add [i.e. touching what carried a corpse, which made a man unclean for seven days]\\add* and he said, "Young man, I say to you, get up."
\\v 15 And the dead man sat up and began to speak, and he gave him to his mother.\\f + \\fr 7:15 \\ft "And he gave him to his mother" is word for word what the Greek Old Testament says of Elijah after he raised the son of the widow of Zarephath (1 Kings 17:23) — the very episode Jesus threw at his hometown in 4:26. He is not only citing the pattern now; he is doing it.\\f*
\\v 16 And fear took hold of them all, and they were glorifying God, saying, "A great prophet has been raised up among us," and "God has visited his people."\\add [i.e. the word Zechariah used when he said God had stepped in at last]\\add*
\\v 17 And this word about him went out in the whole of Judea and in all the surrounding country.
\\s1 John Sends From Prison
\\p
\\v 18 And John's disciples reported all these things to him. And calling two of his disciples to him, John
\\v 19 sent them to the Lord\\f + \\fr 7:19 \\ft The oldest Greek reads "to the Lord"; most older English versions have "to Jesus."\\f* to say, "Are you the one who is coming, or should we look for another?"\\add [i.e. because what I announced was an axe and a winnowing fork, and what I am hearing about is mercy]\\add*
\\v 20 And when the men came to him they said, "John the Immerser sent us to you to say, 'Are you the one who is coming, or should we look for another?'"
\\p
\\v 21 In that hour he healed many of diseases and afflictions and evil spirits, and to many who were blind he granted sight.
\\v 22 And he answered and said to them, "Go and report to John what you saw and heard: the blind see again, the lame walk, lepers are cleansed and the deaf hear, the dead are raised, the poor have the good news announced to them.\\f + \\fr 7:22 \\ft Every item is lifted from Isaiah's descriptions of the restoration: the blind seeing and the deaf hearing and the lame leaping (Isaiah 35:5-6), the dead living again (Isaiah 26:19), good news to the poor (Isaiah 61:1). The answer to John's question is: read Isaiah, and then look at what is happening. The judgment you preached is not cancelled; it is not first.\\f*
\\v 23 And blessed is whoever is not tripped up by me."\\add [i.e. blessed is the man who can accept a Messiah who heals before he judges]\\add*
\\s1 What Jesus Says About John
\\p
\\v 24 And when John's messengers had gone, he began to speak to the crowds about John: "What did you go out into the wilderness to look at? A reed shaken by the wind?
\\v 25 But what did you go out to see? A man dressed in soft clothing? Look — those in splendid clothing and living in luxury are in royal palaces.\\add [i.e. and John is in a royal palace, but in the cells under it]\\add*
\\v 26 But what did you go out to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and much more than a prophet.
\\v 27 This is the one about whom it is written: 'Look, I am sending my messenger before your face, who will build your road ahead of you.'\\f + \\fr 7:27 \\ft Malachi 3:1, with one word altered. In Malachi God says the messenger goes ahead of "my face"; here it is "your face," spoken to Jesus. The road being built was the LORD's own.\\f*
\\v 28 I tell you, among those born of women there is no one greater than John;\\f + \\fr 7:28 \\ft The oldest Greek reads "no one greater than John"; the later manuscripts have "no prophet greater than John."\\f* yet the smallest in the kingdom of God is greater than he."\\add [i.e. not because John is small, but because what is now arriving is that much larger than everything before it]\\add*
\\p
\\v 29 (And all the people who heard, and the tax collectors, declared God righteous, having been immersed with the immersion of John;
\\v 30 but the Pharisees and the lawyers set aside for themselves what God had purposed, not having been immersed by him.)\\add [i.e. God's purpose was aimed at them too, and they waved it off]\\add*
\\s1 Children in the Marketplace
\\p
\\v 31 "To what then shall I compare the men of this generation, and what are they like?
\\v 32 They are like children sitting in a marketplace and calling to one another, who say, 'We played the flute for you and you did not dance; we sang the funeral song and you did not weep.'
\\v 33 For John the Immerser has come neither eating bread nor drinking wine, and you say, 'He has a demon.'
\\v 34 The son of man has come eating and drinking, and you say, 'Look — a glutton and a wine-drinker, a friend of tax collectors and sinners!'\\add [i.e. nothing God sends is ever the right thing]\\add*
\\v 35 And wisdom is declared righteous by all her children."\\add [i.e. and the people who took both messages are the proof that God knew what he was doing]\\add*
\\s1 A Sinful Woman Anoints His Feet
\\p
\\v 36 And one of the Pharisees was asking him to eat with him; and he went into the Pharisee's house and reclined at table.\\add [i.e. lying on one side at a low table, with the feet stretched away behind]\\add*
\\v 37 And look — a woman who was a sinner in that town, when she learned that he was reclining at table in the Pharisee's house, brought an alabaster jar of perfumed oil,
\\v 38 and standing behind him at his feet, weeping, she began to wet his feet with her tears and wipe them with the hair of her head, and she kissed his feet again and again and anointed them with the perfumed oil.\\add [i.e. and a respectable woman did not let her hair down in public]\\add*
\\p
\\v 39 And when the Pharisee who had invited him saw it, he said to himself, "If this man were a prophet he would know who and what sort of woman this is who is touching him — that she is a sinner."\\add [i.e. and would have kept his distance, which is what a holy man does]\\add*
\\p
\\v 40 And Jesus answered and said to him, "Simon, I have something to say to you."\\add [i.e. answering the thought he never spoke aloud]\\add* And he said, "Teacher, say it."
\\v 41 "Two men were debtors to a certain moneylender: one owed five hundred denarii, the other fifty.\\f + \\fr 7:41 \\ft A denarius was a day's wage for a laborer. The debts are roughly a year and a half of work against two months of it.\\f*
\\v 42 When they had nothing to pay with, he freely forgave them both. Which of them, then, will love him more?"
\\v 43 Simon answered and said, "I suppose the one he forgave more." And he said to him, "You have judged rightly."\\add [i.e. and in judging it you have just explained the woman at your feet]\\add*
\\p
\\v 44 And turning to the woman, he said to Simon, "Do you see this woman? I came into your house; you gave me no water for my feet, but she has wet my feet with her tears and wiped them with her hair.\\f + \\fr 7:44 \\ft Water for a guest's feet, a kiss of greeting, and oil for the head were the ordinary courtesies of hospitality (Genesis 18:4; 2 Samuel 19:39; Psalm 23:5). Simon has provided none of the three.\\f*
\\v 45 You gave me no kiss, but from the time I came in she has not stopped kissing my feet.
\\v 46 You did not anoint my head with oil, but she has anointed my feet with perfumed oil.
\\v 47 For this reason I tell you, her many sins have been forgiven, because she loved much; but the one who is forgiven little loves little."\\f + \\fr 7:47 \\ft The Greek can be read either way round, but the parable just told settles it: in the story the debts are cancelled first and the love follows. Her love is not what buys the forgiveness; it is the evidence that she has received it. Simon's small love shows a man who thinks he owed very little.\\f*
\\p
\\v 48 And he said to her, "Your sins have been forgiven."
\\v 49 And those reclining at table with him began to say among themselves, "Who is this who even forgives sins?"
\\v 50 And he said to the woman, "Your trust has rescued you; go in peace."\\add [i.e. not your tears and not the perfume — the trust that brought you through that door]\\add*
\\c 8
\\s1 The Women Who Traveled With Him
\\p
\\v 1 And it happened afterward that he traveled on, town by town and village by village, proclaiming and announcing the good news of the kingdom of God; and the twelve were with him,
\\v 2 and some women who had been healed of evil spirits and sicknesses: Mary called Magdalene, from whom seven demons had gone out,
\\v 3 and Joanna the wife of Chuza, Herod's steward, and Susanna, and many others, who were serving them out of what belonged to them.\\f + \\fr 8:3 \\ft Chuza managed Herod Antipas's estate — a high position in the court of the man who had just executed John. These women are not followers being carried along; they are paying for the whole operation out of their own property. Women funding a teacher's work was known in the Greek world; women traveling with him was not.\\f*
\\s1 The Sower
\\p
\\v 4 And when a great crowd was coming together, and people were making their way to him town after town, he spoke through a parable:
\\v 5 "The sower went out to sow his seed. And as he sowed, some fell along the path and was trampled underfoot, and the birds of the sky ate it up.
\\v 6 And other fell on the rock, and when it came up it withered away, because it had no moisture.\\add [i.e. a skin of soil over a limestone shelf, which warms fast and holds nothing]\\add*
\\v 7 And other fell in among the thorns, and the thorns grew up with it and choked it.
\\v 8 And other fell into the good earth, and when it came up it made fruit a hundred times over."\\add [i.e. a yield nobody had ever seen; ten times over was a good year]\\add* As he said these things he called out, "Whoever has ears to hear, let him hear."
\\p
\\v 9 And his disciples were asking him what this parable might be.
\\v 10 And he said, "To you it has been given to know the mysteries of the kingdom of God, but to the rest in parables, so that seeing they may not see and hearing they may not understand.\\f + \\fr 8:10 \\ft Isaiah 6:9-10, from the prophet's commissioning: his preaching would harden Israel rather than turn it. A parable does not create blindness; it exposes it. The ones who want to know press in and ask, as the disciples have just done, and the rest hear a farming story and walk off. "Mysteries" in this usage does not mean riddles but God's purposes, hidden until he discloses them (Daniel 2:28-30).\\f*
\\p
\\v 11 Now the parable is this: the seed is the word of God.
\\v 12 Those along the path are the ones who hear; then the devil comes and takes the word away from their heart, so that they may not trust and be rescued.
\\v 13 Those on the rock are the ones who welcome the word with joy when they hear it; and these have no root — they trust for a while, and in a time of testing they pull away.\\f + \\fr 8:13 \\ft The verb rendered "pull away" is the ordinary word for defecting or standing off from something one belonged to; the Greek Old Testament uses it for Israel abandoning God (Deuteronomy 32:15; Jeremiah 3:14). The trust in this soil is described as real, and so is the departure. What this soil lacks is not sincerity but root.\\f*
\\v 14 And what fell into the thorns — these are the ones who heard, and as they go on they are choked by worries and riches and the pleasures of life, and they bring nothing to ripeness.
\\v 15 And what is in the good earth — these are the ones who hear the word with a fine and good heart, and hold on to it, and bear fruit with staying power.\\add [i.e. hearing is where all four start; only this one keeps hold and lasts]\\add*
\\s1 Watch How You Hear
\\p
\\v 16 And no one who lights a lamp covers it with a jar or puts it under a bed; he puts it on a lampstand, so that those coming in may see the light.
\\v 17 For nothing is hidden that will not become plain, and nothing is concealed that will not be known and come out into the open.
\\v 18 So watch how you hear; for whoever has, to him it will be given, and whoever does not have, even what he thinks he has will be taken away from him."\\add [i.e. what he thinks he has — a man can be sure of ground he is not actually standing on]\\add*
\\s1 His Real Family
\\p
\\v 19 And his mother and his brothers came to him, and they could not get to him because of the crowd.
\\v 20 And it was reported to him, "Your mother and your brothers are standing outside wanting to see you."
\\v 21 But he answered and said to them, "My mother and my brothers are these — the ones who hear the word of God and do it."\\add [i.e. kinship in this family is not by birth but by hearing and doing]\\add*
\\s1 He Gives Orders to the Wind and the Water
\\p
\\v 22 And it happened on one of the days that he got into a boat with his disciples, and he said to them, "Let us go over to the other side of the lake." And they put out.
\\v 23 And as they were sailing he fell asleep. And a windstorm came down on the lake, and they were being swamped and were in danger.\\add [i.e. the lake sits in a basin, and wind funnels down the valleys onto it without warning]\\add*
\\v 24 And coming to him they woke him, saying, "Master, Master, we are perishing!" And he woke up and rebuked the wind and the surging water, and they stopped, and there was a calm.\\f + \\fr 8:24 \\ft "Rebuked" is the same word used for silencing demons. In Israel's poetry, commanding the sea belongs to God alone: "You rule the raging of the sea; when its waves rise, you still them" (Psalm 89:9); and it is God who "made the storm be still, and the waves of the sea were hushed" (Psalm 107:29).\\f*
\\v 25 And he said to them, "Where is your trust?" And they were afraid and they marveled, saying to one another, "Who then is this, that he gives orders even to the winds and the water, and they obey him?"\\add [i.e. and that is the question the whole book is asking]\\add*
\\s1 Legion
\\p
\\v 26 And they sailed down to the region of the Gerasenes, which is opposite Galilee.\\f + \\fr 8:26 \\ft The oldest Greek has "Gerasenes"; other manuscripts read "Gadarenes" or "Gergesenes," and the same variation occurs again at verse 37. "Opposite Galilee" means the far shore — Gentile country, as the herd of pigs confirms.\\f*
\\v 27 And as he went out onto the land, a man from the town met him who had demons; and for a long time he had worn no clothes, and he did not stay in a house but among the tombs.\\add [i.e. naked, homeless, living with the dead — every mark of a human being stripped off him]\\add*
\\v 28 When he saw Jesus he cried out and fell down before him, and with a loud voice he said, "What is there between me and you, Jesus, Son of the Most High God? I beg you, do not torment me."
\\v 29 For he had been ordering the unclean spirit to come out of the man. For many times it had seized him, and he was kept bound with chains and shackles under guard, and he would break the bonds apart and be driven by the demon into the deserted places.
\\p
\\v 30 And Jesus asked him, "What is your name?" And he said, "Legion," because many demons had gone into him.\\f + \\fr 8:30 \\ft A legion was a Roman army unit of some five to six thousand men. In occupied country, the word had one obvious owner. Whether or not the man chose it deliberately, no one hearing it could have missed what it named.\\f*
\\v 31 And they were begging him not to order them to go off into the abyss.\\f + \\fr 8:31 \\ft The abyss is the prison of rebel spirits in Jewish writings of the period (compare 1 Enoch 10). They are not pleading against being expelled, but against being sentenced.\\f*
\\v 32 Now there was a considerable herd of pigs feeding there on the hillside; and they begged him to let them go into those, and he let them.
\\v 33 And the demons came out of the man and went into the pigs, and the herd rushed down the steep bank into the lake and was drowned.\\add [i.e. an occupying force going into the water, as Pharaoh's army did]\\add*
\\p
\\v 34 And when those feeding them saw what had happened, they ran and reported it in the town and out in the fields.
\\v 35 And people came out to see what had happened, and they came to Jesus and found the man from whom the demons had gone out, sitting at the feet of Jesus, clothed and in his right mind; and they were afraid.\\add [i.e. seated, dressed, sane — every stripped thing given back]\\add*
\\v 36 And those who had seen it reported to them how the demon-possessed man had been rescued.
\\v 37 And the whole mass of people from the surrounding region of the Gerasenes asked him to go away from them, because they were gripped by a great fear. And he got into the boat and went back.
\\p
\\v 38 And the man from whom the demons had gone out was begging to be with him; but he sent him away, saying,
\\v 39 "Go back to your house and tell all that God has done for you." And he went off, proclaiming through the whole town all that Jesus had done for him.\\add [i.e. told to speak of God, he speaks of Jesus, and the writer lets the substitution stand]\\add*
\\s1 Jairus's Daughter and the Woman in the Crowd
\\p
\\v 40 And when Jesus came back, the crowd welcomed him, for they were all waiting for him.
\\v 41 And look — a man named Jairus came, and this man was a ruler of the synagogue; and falling at the feet of Jesus, he begged him to come into his house,\\add [i.e. a leading man of the community on the ground at his feet]\\add*
\\v 42 because he had an only daughter, about twelve years old, and she was dying. And as he went, the crowds were pressing hard against him.
\\p
\\v 43 And a woman with a flow of blood for twelve years, who could not be healed by anyone,\\f + \\fr 8:43 \\ft Twelve years of continuous ritual uncleanness (Leviticus 15:25-30): barred from the temple, and making unclean anything and anyone she touched. She has been untouchable for exactly as long as the dying girl has been alive. Many manuscripts add here that she had "spent all her living on physicians"; the oldest Greek does not have it.\\f*
\\v 44 came up behind him and touched the fringe of his garment, and immediately her flow of blood stopped.\\f + \\fr 8:44 \\ft The fringe is the tassel the law required on the corners of a garment as a reminder of God's commands (Numbers 15:38-39). She reaches, from behind and unseen, for the part of his clothing that stands for covenant faithfulness.\\f*
\\p
\\v 45 And Jesus said, "Who is the one who touched me?" And when they all denied it, Peter said, "Master, the crowds are hemming you in and pressing on you."\\f + \\fr 8:45 \\ft Some manuscripts expand this verse, adding "and those with him" after Peter's name and "and do you say, Who touched me?" at the end. The oldest Greek has neither.\\f*
\\v 46 But Jesus said, "Someone touched me, for I knew that power had gone out from me."
\\v 47 And when the woman saw that she had not gone unnoticed, she came trembling and fell down before him and declared in front of all the people why she had touched him, and how she had been healed on the spot.\\add [i.e. he will not let her slip away healed and still hidden; the restoration has to be public to be complete]\\add*
\\v 48 And he said to her, "Daughter, your trust has rescued you; go in peace."\\f + \\fr 8:48 \\ft Some manuscripts add "be of good cheer" before "Daughter"; the oldest Greek does not. Note the word he uses for her — daughter — spoken to a woman who has been outside the family of Israel's worship for twelve years.\\f*
\\p
\\v 49 While he was still speaking, someone came from the house of the synagogue ruler, saying, "Your daughter has died; trouble the teacher no further."
\\v 50 But Jesus heard and answered him, "Do not be afraid; only trust, and she will be rescued."\\add [i.e. now, with the news already delivered, and nothing left to trust on]\\add*
\\p
\\v 51 And when he came into the house, he did not let anyone go in with him except Peter and John and James, and the child's father and mother.
\\v 52 And they were all weeping and beating their breasts for her. But he said, "Do not weep, for she has not died but is sleeping."\\add [i.e. not a diagnosis — a statement about what death amounts to in front of him]\\add*
\\v 53 And they laughed at him, knowing that she had died.
\\p
\\v 54 But he took hold of her hand and called out, "Child, get up."
\\v 55 And her spirit came back, and she stood up at once; and he ordered that something be given her to eat.
\\v 56 And her parents were beside themselves; but he ordered them to tell no one what had happened.
\\c 9
\\s1 The Twelve Are Sent Out
\\p
\\v 1 And calling the twelve together, he gave them power and authority over all the demons and to cure diseases,\\add [i.e. both the ability to do it and the right to do it in his name]\\add*
\\v 2 and he sent them out to proclaim the kingdom of God and to heal.
\\v 3 And he said to them, "Take nothing for the road — no staff, no bag, no bread, no silver, and do not have two tunics each.\\add [i.e. nothing to live on, so that you must be taken in or move on]\\add*
\\v 4 And whatever house you go into, stay there and go out from there.
\\v 5 And wherever they do not welcome you, as you leave that town shake off the dust from your feet as evidence against them."\\f + \\fr 9:5 \\ft Jews coming home from Gentile country shook off its dust rather than carry it into the land. Doing it to a Jewish town treats that town as though it had put itself outside Israel.\\f*
\\v 6 And they went out and traveled through village after village, announcing the good news and healing everywhere.
\\s1 Herod Wonders Who This Is
\\p
\\v 7 Now Herod the tetrarch heard of everything that was happening, and he was thoroughly at a loss, because it was said by some that John had been raised from the dead,
\\v 8 and by some that Elijah had appeared,\\f + \\fr 9:8 \\ft Elijah did not die but was carried off in a whirlwind (2 Kings 2:11), and Malachi had promised he would be sent back before the day of the LORD (Malachi 4:5). His return was a live expectation.\\f* and by others that one of the old prophets had risen.
\\v 9 And Herod said, "John I beheaded; but who is this about whom I hear such things?" And he was trying to see him.\\add [i.e. and he will get his wish, at the trial]\\add*
\\s1 Five Loaves for Five Thousand
\\p
\\v 10 And when the apostles came back, they told him all they had done. And taking them with him, he withdrew privately to a town called Bethsaida.
\\v 11 But the crowds found out and followed him; and he welcomed them and spoke to them about the kingdom of God, and he cured those who had need of healing.
\\p
\\v 12 And the day began to wear away, and the twelve came and said to him, "Send the crowd off, so they can go into the villages and farms around and find lodging and food, because here we are in a deserted place."
\\v 13 But he said to them, "You give them something to eat." And they said, "We have no more than five loaves and two fish — unless we go and buy food for all these people."\\f + \\fr 9:13 \\ft Moses said almost the same thing in the same kind of place: "Where am I to get meat to give to all this people?" (Numbers 11:13). A crowd in a wilderness with nothing to eat is a scene Israel already knew the end of.\\f*
\\v 14 For there were about five thousand men. And he said to his disciples, "Have them recline in groups of about fifty each."\\add [i.e. drawn up in ordered companies, the way Israel was arranged in the desert]\\add*
\\v 15 And they did so and had them all recline.
\\p
\\v 16 And taking the five loaves and the two fish, he looked up into the sky and blessed them and broke them, and he kept giving them to the disciples to set before the crowd.\\f + \\fr 9:16 \\ft Took, blessed, broke, gave — the four movements of the ordinary Jewish blessing at a meal, where the head of the house blesses God, not the food. The same four verbs come back at the last supper (22:19) and again at Emmaus (24:30), and by the third time they have become the way he is recognized.\\f*
\\v 17 And they all ate and were filled, and what was left over was picked up: twelve baskets of pieces.\\add [i.e. one for each of the twelve who said it could not be done]\\add*
\\s1 The Christ of God
\\p
\\v 18 And it happened, while he was praying alone, that the disciples were with him, and he asked them, "Who do the crowds say that I am?"
\\v 19 And they answered, "John the Immerser; but others, Elijah; and others, that one of the old prophets has risen."
\\v 20 And he said to them, "But you — who do you say that I am?" And Peter answered and said, "The Christ of God."
\\v 21 But he warned them sharply and ordered them to tell this to no one,\\add [i.e. because the title is right and their idea of what it means is not]\\add*
\\v 22 saying, "The son of man must suffer much and be examined and rejected by the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and on the third day be raised."\\f + \\fr 9:22 \\ft The three groups named are the three that made up the ruling council in Jerusalem. And "must" is again the word for what God has determined. Nothing in the messianic hope of the time expected this: the Psalms of Solomon, written about a century earlier, look for a son of David who will smash unrighteous rulers and purge Jerusalem. He says the ruling body of his own nation will condemn him and he will be executed.\\f*
\\s1 Take Up Your Cross Day After Day
\\p
\\v 23 And he said to them all, "If anyone wants to come after me, let him say no to himself and pick up his cross day after day and follow me.\\f + \\fr 9:23 \\ft Not a figure of speech. Crucifixion was the Roman penalty for slaves and rebels, and the condemned man carried the crossbeam out to the place of execution through a jeering crowd. Everyone listening had seen it. "Day after day" turns a single terrible moment into a way of living.\\f*
\\v 24 For whoever wants to rescue his life will lose it, but whoever loses his life for my sake — this one will rescue it.
\\v 25 For what good does it do a man to gain the whole world and destroy or forfeit himself?\\add [i.e. the whole world is not a fair price for one self, and it is the only price on offer]\\add*
\\v 26 For whoever is ashamed of me and of my words, of this one the son of man will be ashamed when he comes in his glory and the glory of the Father and of the holy angels.
\\v 27 But I tell you truly, there are some standing here who will not taste death until they see the kingdom of God."
\\s1 The Mountain, the Glory, and the Voice
\\p
\\v 28 And it happened about eight days after these words that he took Peter and John and James along and went up on the mountain to pray.
\\v 29 And while he was praying, the look of his face became different, and his clothing turned flashing white.\\f + \\fr 9:29 \\ft When Moses came down from speaking with God on Sinai, the skin of his face was shining and had to be veiled (Exodus 34:29-35). Here it is not a reflection borrowed from an encounter; the change comes from inside, and it happens while he prays.\\f*
\\v 30 And look — two men were talking with him, who were Moses and Elijah,\\add [i.e. the law and the prophets, standing together with him]\\add*
\\v 31 who appeared in glory and were speaking of his exodus, which he was about to bring to fulfillment in Jerusalem.\\f + \\fr 9:31 \\ft The Greek word is \\tl exodos\\tl*, which means a departure, including death — and which is also the name of Israel's going out from Egypt. It is kept here because both are meant. What he is going to Jerusalem to do is a going-out, and it is the exodus all over again.\\f*
\\p
\\v 32 But Peter and those with him were weighed down with sleep; and coming fully awake, they saw his glory and the two men standing with him.
\\v 33 And it happened, as they were leaving him, that Peter said to Jesus, "Master, it is good for us to be here; let us make three shelters, one for you and one for Moses and one for Elijah" — not knowing what he was saying.\\add [i.e. three shelters, as though the three of them stood on the same footing]\\add*
\\v 34 And while he was saying this, a cloud came and overshadowed them; and they were afraid as they went into the cloud.\\f + \\fr 9:34 \\ft The cloud is the visible presence of God: it covered Sinai, filled the tent of meeting, and filled the temple (Exodus 24:15-16; 40:34-35; 1 Kings 8:10-11). The same verb, "overshadow," was used of what the power of the Most High would do to Mary (1:35).\\f*
\\v 35 And a voice came out of the cloud, saying, "This is my Son, the Chosen One; listen to him."\\f + \\fr 9:35 \\ft The oldest Greek reads "the Chosen One"; most older English versions have "my beloved Son," matching the words at the river. "Chosen" points to Isaiah's servant — "my chosen one, in whom my soul delights" (Isaiah 42:1) — and that servant's road ends in Isaiah 53. "Listen to him" is what Moses said of the prophet God would raise up like himself (Deuteronomy 18:15), and it lands directly on what Jesus has just said about suffering, which is what Peter has just tried to build a shelter over.\\f*
\\v 36 And when the voice had come, Jesus was found alone. And they kept silent and reported to no one in those days anything of what they had seen.\\add [i.e. not three figures on the mountain in the end — one]\\add*
\\s1 Down From the Mountain
\\p
\\v 37 And it happened on the next day, when they had come down from the mountain, that a large crowd met him.
\\v 38 And look — a man from the crowd shouted out, "Teacher, I beg you, look at my son, because he is my only child.
\\v 39 And look — a spirit takes him and he suddenly screams, and it convulses him until he foams, and it hardly ever leaves him, wearing him down.
\\v 40 And I begged your disciples to throw it out, and they could not."\\add [i.e. the men who were given authority over all the demons, and it did not work]\\add*
\\v 41 And Jesus answered and said, "O generation without trust and twisted out of shape, how long will I be with you and put up with you? Bring your son here."\\f + \\fr 9:41 \\ft The words are Moses' about Israel in the wilderness — "a crooked and twisted generation," "children in whom there is no faithfulness" (Deuteronomy 32:5, 20). Moses came down his mountain to find the people worshipping a calf; this descent from the mountain of glory lands in the same kind of scene.\\f*
\\v 42 And while he was still coming, the demon knocked him down and convulsed him. But Jesus rebuked the unclean spirit and healed the boy and gave him back to his father.\\add [i.e. the same phrase as at Nain — the child handed back to the parent]\\add*
\\s1 Handed Over Into the Hands of Men
\\p
\\v 43 And they were all struck out of themselves at the greatness of God. And while everyone was marveling at everything he was doing, he said to his disciples,
\\v 44 "Put these words into your ears: the son of man is about to be handed over into the hands of men."\\add [i.e. at the top of the acclaim, and to them alone]\\add*
\\v 45 But they did not know what this saying meant, and it was veiled from them so that they would not grasp it; and they were afraid to ask him about this saying.\\add [i.e. they sensed the weight of it and would not go near it]\\add*
\\p
\\v 46 And a reasoning came in among them as to which of them might be greatest.
\\v 47 But Jesus, knowing the reasoning of their heart, took hold of a child and stood him beside himself,
\\v 48 and he said to them, "Whoever welcomes this child in my name welcomes me, and whoever welcomes me welcomes the one who sent me; for the one who is smallest among you all — this one is great."\\add [i.e. a child, who in that world had no standing whatever and nothing to give back]\\add*
\\p
\\v 49 And John answered and said, "Master, we saw someone throwing out demons in your name, and we tried to stop him, because he does not follow along with us."
\\v 50 But Jesus said to him, "Do not stop him, for whoever is not against you is for you."\\add [i.e. the name is not yours to license]\\add*
\\s1 He Sets His Face Toward Jerusalem
\\p
\\v 51 And it happened, as the days of his being taken up were being filled, that he set his face to go to Jerusalem.\\f + \\fr 9:51 \\ft The turning point of the book: from here to 19:28 everything happens on one road. "Set his face" is the language of Isaiah's servant, who says under abuse, "I have set my face like flint" (Isaiah 50:7). "Being taken up" gathers the whole end into one word — death, rising, and ascension together.\\f*
\\v 52 And he sent messengers ahead of his face; and they went and entered a village of Samaritans, to make things ready for him.
\\v 53 And they did not welcome him, because his face was set toward Jerusalem.\\f + \\fr 9:53 \\ft The Samaritans worshipped on Mount Gerizim and rejected the Jerusalem temple; a Jewish teacher heading there for a feast was heading, in their view, to the wrong mountain. The hostility ran both ways and was centuries old.\\f*
\\v 54 And when the disciples James and John saw it, they said, "Lord, do you want us to tell fire to come down from heaven and finish them off?"\\f + \\fr 9:54 \\ft They have Elijah in mind — he called fire down on soldiers sent to arrest him (2 Kings 1:10-12) — and they have just seen Elijah on the mountain. Some manuscripts spell it out by adding "as Elijah did"; the oldest Greek does not.\\f*
\\v 55 But he turned and rebuked them.\\f + \\fr 9:55 \\ft Later manuscripts add a reply here: "and said, You do not know what spirit you are of; for the son of man did not come to destroy men's lives but to save them." The oldest Greek has only the bare rebuke, and gives no reason for it.\\f*
\\v 56 And they went on to another village.
\\s1 What Following Costs
\\p
\\v 57 And as they were going along the road, someone said to him, "I will follow you wherever you go."\\f + \\fr 9:57 \\ft Many manuscripts add "Lord" to the man's offer; the oldest Greek does not.\\f*
\\v 58 And Jesus said to him, "The foxes have dens and the birds of the sky have nests, but the son of man has nowhere to lay his head."\\add [i.e. "wherever you go" turns out to mean nowhere in particular]\\add*
\\p
\\v 59 And he said to another, "Follow me." But he said, "Lord, let me first go and bury my father."
\\v 60 And he said to him, "Leave the dead to bury their own dead; but you go and announce far and wide the kingdom of God."\\f + \\fr 9:60 \\ft Burying a parent was among the most binding obligations in Jewish life — it took precedence over almost every other command, and even a priest forbidden to go near a corpse was expected to bury an abandoned one. The answer is meant to shock. It is not a rule about funerals; it is a measure of how much heavier the other claim weighs.\\f*
\\p
\\v 61 And another also said, "I will follow you, Lord, but first let me say goodbye to those in my house."
\\v 62 And Jesus said to him, "No one who puts his hand to a plow and looks back at what is behind is fit for the kingdom of God."\\f + \\fr 9:62 \\ft When Elijah called Elisha, Elisha asked to go and kiss his father and mother goodbye, and Elijah let him (1 Kings 19:19-21). What the older prophet allowed, this one does not. A plowman who looks over his shoulder cuts a crooked furrow.\\f*
\\c 10
\\s1 Seventy-Two Sent Ahead
\\p
\\v 1 And after these things the Lord appointed seventy-two others and sent them out two by two ahead of his face into every town and place where he himself was about to go.\\f + \\fr 10:1 \\ft The oldest Greek reads "seventy-two," with the "two" marked as uncertain; many English versions have "seventy." Both numbers carry weight. Moses had seventy elders on whom God put his Spirit so that they shared his load (Numbers 11:16-25); and the table of nations in Genesis 10 lists seventy peoples in the Hebrew and seventy-two in the Greek. Either way the sending is being widened well past the twelve.\\f*
\\v 2 And he said to them, "The harvest is large, but the workers are few; so beg the Lord of the harvest to push workers out into his harvest.
\\v 3 Go. Look — I am sending you out as lambs in the middle of wolves.\\add [i.e. with nothing to defend yourselves with, and that is deliberate]\\add*
\\v 4 Carry no purse, no bag, no sandals; and greet no one along the road.\\f + \\fr 10:4 \\ft Not rudeness. An eastern greeting was a long affair with hospitality attached, and this errand cannot spare the time. Elisha gave his servant the same instruction when he sent him on an urgent one (2 Kings 4:29).\\f*
\\p
\\v 5 And into whatever house you go, first say, 'Peace to this house.'
\\v 6 And if a son of peace is there, your peace will rest on him; but if not, it will come back on you.\\add [i.e. peace here is not a wish but something handed over, which either settles or returns]\\add*
\\v 7 And stay in that same house, eating and drinking what they have, for the worker is worthy of his pay. Do not move from house to house.
\\v 8 And into whatever town you go, and they welcome you, eat what is set before you,\\add [i.e. without inquiring into how it was prepared]\\add*
\\v 9 and heal the sick in it, and say to them, 'The kingdom of God has come near to you.'
\\s1 Towns That Will Not Welcome You
\\p
\\v 10 But into whatever town you go and they do not welcome you, go out into its streets and say,
\\v 11 'Even the dust from your town that sticks to our feet we wipe off against you; but know this — the kingdom of God has come near.'\\add [i.e. it has arrived either way; only your standing toward it has changed]\\add*
\\v 12 I tell you that on that day it will be more bearable for Sodom than for that town.\\f + \\fr 10:12 \\ft Sodom was the standing example of a place destroyed for its wickedness (Genesis 19). The argument is not that these towns are worse than Sodom but that they have been offered more, and more given means more answered for (compare Amos 3:2).\\f*
\\p
\\v 13 Woe to you, Chorazin! Woe to you, Bethsaida! For if the works of power done in you had been done in Tyre and Sidon, long ago they would have turned back, sitting in sackcloth and ashes.\\f + \\fr 10:13 \\ft Tyre and Sidon are the Phoenician cities the prophets denounced at length for pride and exploitation (Isaiah 23; Ezekiel 26-28; Joel 3:4-8). The claim is that they would have repented on evidence that Galilean towns have watched and shrugged at.\\f*
\\v 14 But it will be more bearable for Tyre and Sidon at the judgment than for you.
\\v 15 And you, Capernaum — will you be lifted as high as heaven? You will be brought down as far as Hades.\\f + \\fr 10:15 \\ft The words are Isaiah's taunt against the king of Babylon: "You said in your heart, I will ascend to heaven... but you will be brought down to Sheol" (Isaiah 14:13-15). Capernaum was his own base — the town that saw more of this than any other. The oldest Greek puts the first half as a question; older versions have it as a statement, "which art exalted to heaven."\\f*
\\v 16 The one who listens to you listens to me, and the one who sets you aside sets me aside; and the one who sets me aside sets aside the one who sent me."\\f + \\fr 10:16 \\ft The principle of the authorized agent, familiar in Jewish law: a man's representative carries the man's own standing, so how you treat the messenger is how you have treated the sender. Moses had said the same of the prophet to come (Deuteronomy 18:19).\\f*
\\s1 The Seventy-Two Come Back
\\p
\\v 17 And the seventy-two came back with joy, saying, "Lord, even the demons put themselves under us in your name!"\\add [i.e. which was more than we were sent to expect]\\add*
\\v 18 And he said to them, "I was watching Satan fall like lightning out of heaven.\\f + \\fr 10:18 \\ft In Job the adversary has standing in the heavenly court and uses it to accuse (Job 1:6-12). To see him fall out of heaven is to see that standing lost. The tense of the verb suggests watching it happen — while they were out on the road doing this, he was seeing what it meant on the other side.\\f*
\\v 19 Look — I have given you the authority to tread on snakes and scorpions, and over all the power of the enemy, and nothing at all will do you harm.\\f + \\fr 10:19 \\ft "You will tread on the lion and the cobra" is Psalm 91:13, from the psalm the devil quoted in the wilderness. Scorpions are named among the dangers of the desert God brought Israel through (Deuteronomy 8:15).\\f*
\\v 20 But do not rejoice in this, that the spirits put themselves under you; rejoice rather that your names have been written in the heavens."\\f + \\fr 10:20 \\ft A register of God's people kept in heaven appears in Exodus 32:32-33, where Moses offers to be blotted out of it, and in Daniel 12:1, where everyone found written in the book is delivered. Power is delegated and can be withdrawn; this is about where they stand, not what they can do.\\f*
\\s1 Hidden From the Wise, Unveiled to Infants
\\p
\\v 21 In that same hour he leaped for joy in the Holy Spirit and said, "I acknowledge you openly, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because you hid these things from wise and clever men and unveiled them to infants. Yes, Father, because this is what was pleasing in front of you.\\add [i.e. from the trained and the credentialed, and to those with nothing to bring]\\add*
\\v 22 Everything has been handed over to me by my Father, and no one knows who the Son is except the Father, and who the Father is except the Son, and anyone to whom the Son wants to unveil him."
\\p
\\v 23 And turning to the disciples privately he said, "Blessed are the eyes that see what you see.
\\v 24 For I tell you that many prophets and kings wanted to see what you see and did not see it, and to hear what you hear and did not hear it."\\add [i.e. the men who wrote the promises and the men who waited on them, and you are watching it happen]\\add*
\\s1 What Shall I Do to Inherit the Life of the Age?
\\p
\\v 25 And look — a certain lawyer stood up to test him: "Teacher, what shall I do to inherit the life of the age?"\\f + \\fr 10:25 \\ft A "lawyer" here is an expert in the law of Moses. "The life of the age" is the life of the age to come, when God has put the world right (Daniel 12:2) — and note the strain in his own question: an inheritance is not something you earn.\\f*
\\v 26 And he said to him, "What is written in the law? How do you read it?"
\\v 27 And he answered and said, "You shall love the Lord your God out of your whole heart and with your whole self and with your whole strength and with your whole mind, and your neighbor as yourself."\\f + \\fr 10:27 \\ft Two texts joined: Deuteronomy 6:5, the confession recited daily, and Leviticus 19:18. Pairing them as the summary of the whole law was a recognized move, and Jesus does not dispute it.\\f*
\\v 28 And he said to him, "You have answered rightly; do this and you will live."
\\s1 The Samaritan on the Jericho Road
\\p
\\v 29 But wanting to set himself right, he said to Jesus, "And who is my neighbor?"\\add [i.e. draw me a boundary I can be safely inside]\\add*
\\v 30 Jesus took it up and said, "A certain man was going down from Jerusalem to Jericho, and he fell in with robbers, who stripped him and laid blows on him and went off, leaving him half dead.\\f + \\fr 10:30 \\ft Seventeen miles of rock and gorge, dropping over three thousand feet, and notorious for bandits. Note what the robbers leave: stripped and unconscious, the man has no clothing and no speech, and so nothing that would tell a passerby who he is. Nobody can work out whether he is the kind of person they are obliged to help.\\f*
\\v 31 And it happened that a certain priest was going down that road, and when he saw him he passed by on the other side.\\f + \\fr 10:31 \\ft "Going down" means away from Jerusalem, his temple duty finished. A corpse made a man unclean for seven days (Numbers 19:11-16), and a priest was restricted from contact with the dead (Leviticus 21:1-3). He may well be reasoning from the law. But the same law says, "Do not stand idly by the blood of your neighbor" (Leviticus 19:16) — the verse two lines above "love your neighbor as yourself."\\f*
\\v 32 And a Levite too, coming to the place and seeing him, passed by on the other side.
\\v 33 But a certain Samaritan on a journey came to where he was, and when he saw him his heart went out to him,\\add [i.e. the one man on the road who owed this Jew nothing whatever]\\add*
\\v 34 and going to him he bandaged his wounds, pouring on oil and wine; and setting him on his own animal, he brought him to an inn and looked after him.\\add [i.e. and walked, so the man could ride]\\add*
\\v 35 And on the next day he took out two denarii and gave them to the innkeeper and said, 'Look after him, and whatever more you spend, I will pay you back when I come back.'\\add [i.e. two days' wages down, and his name on the rest of the bill]\\add*
\\v 36 Which of these three seems to you to have become a neighbor to the man who fell in with the robbers?"\\add [i.e. the question is no longer who qualifies for your love, but whether you are the kind of man who gives it]\\add*
\\v 37 And he said, "The one who did mercy with him."\\add [i.e. unable, even now, to say the word Samaritan]\\add* And Jesus said to him, "Go, and you do the same."
\\s1 Martha and Mary
\\p
\\v 38 And as they were going on, he went into a certain village; and a woman named Martha welcomed him into her house.
\\v 39 And she had a sister called Mary, who sat down at the Lord's feet and was listening to his word.\\f + \\fr 10:39 \\ft Sitting at a teacher's feet is the posture of a student — Paul says he was "brought up at the feet of Gamaliel" (Acts 22:3). It was not a place women were normally given, and the writer records without comment that Mary took it.\\f*
\\v 40 But Martha was being pulled about by all the serving; and coming up she said, "Lord, do you not care that my sister has left me to serve alone? Tell her then to take hold with me."
\\v 41 But the Lord answered and said to her, "Martha, Martha, you are worried and stirred up about many things,
\\v 42 but there is need of few things, or of one.\\f + \\fr 10:42 \\ft The oldest Greek reads "of few things, or of one"; most English versions have only "one thing is needed."\\f* For Mary chose the good portion, which will not be taken away from her."\\add [i.e. the food you are preparing will be eaten and gone; what she is taking in will not]\\add*
\\c 11
\\s1 Teach Us to Pray
\\p
\\v 1 And it happened, while he was praying in a certain place, that when he stopped one of his disciples said to him, "Lord, teach us to pray, as John also taught his disciples."\\add [i.e. a teacher's prayer was what marked out his movement]\\add*
\\v 2 And he said to them, "When you pray, say: Father, let your name be kept holy. Let your kingdom come.\\f + \\fr 11:2 \\ft This is the short form, and it is what the oldest Greek copies have. Familiar words are missing from it, added later from Matthew's longer version: "our, who is in the heavens" after "Father," and "let your will be done, as in heaven, so on earth" here, and "but deliver us from the evil one" at the end of verse 4. Note also the bare address. Jews prayed to God as "our Father"; simply "Father" is startlingly close, and it is how Jesus himself prays (10:21; 22:42; 23:34, 46).\\f*
\\v 3 Give us each day our bread for the day ahead.\\f + \\fr 11:3 \\ft The word rendered "for the day ahead" occurs nowhere else in Greek before this prayer and its sense is disputed: "daily," "needed for existence," or "for the coming day." Behind it stands the manna, which had to be gathered fresh each morning and could not be stored (Exodus 16:4, 19-20). Asking for one day's bread is a way of refusing to secure yourself against tomorrow.\\f*
\\v 4 And forgive us our sins, for we ourselves also forgive everyone in debt to us. And do not bring us into testing."
\\s1 The Friend at Midnight
\\p
\\v 5 And he said to them, "Which of you will have a friend and go to him at midnight and say to him, 'Friend, lend me three loaves,
\\v 6 since a friend of mine has come to me off the road and I have nothing to set before him' —\\add [i.e. and failing a guest shames the whole village, not only me]\\add*
\\v 7 and that one from inside will answer and say, 'Do not put me to trouble; the door has already been shut, and my children are with me in bed; I cannot get up and give you anything'?\\add [i.e. one room, one bolted door, and the whole household asleep on the floor]\\add*
\\v 8 I tell you, even if he will not get up and give to him because he is his friend, yet because of the shamelessness of it he will get up and give him as much as he needs.\\f + \\fr 11:8 \\ft The Greek word means shamelessness, and it does not say whose. It may be the caller's — brazen enough to keep knocking; or the sleeper's — unwilling to be the man who would not open. Rendered so as to leave both standing. Either way the argument runs from the lesser to the greater: if that gets a result from a reluctant neighbor, consider who you are actually asking.\\f*
\\s1 Ask, Search, Knock
\\p
\\v 9 And I say to you: ask, and it will be given to you; search, and you will find; knock, and it will be opened to you.
\\v 10 For everyone who asks receives, and the one who searches finds, and to the one who knocks it will be opened.
\\v 11 And which father among you, if his son asks for a fish, will hand him a snake instead of a fish?\\f + \\fr 11:11 \\ft Many manuscripts begin the verse with a further pair, "if he asks for bread, will he hand him a stone?" The oldest Greek starts at the fish.\\f*
\\v 12 Or if he asks for an egg, will hand him a scorpion?\\add [i.e. a scorpion curled up is about the size and color of an egg]\\add*
\\v 13 So if you, being evil, know how to give good gifts to your children, how much more will the Father out of heaven give the Holy Spirit to those who ask him!"\\f + \\fr 11:13 \\ft Where Matthew's version says the Father gives "good things," this one names the gift: the Holy Spirit. That is the gift the prophets attached to the age of restoration (Joel 2:28-29; Ezekiel 36:26-27), and it is what the second volume of this work opens with.\\f*
\\s1 By Whose Power?
\\p
\\v 14 And he was throwing out a demon, and it was mute. And it happened, when the demon had gone out, that the mute man spoke, and the crowds marveled.
\\v 15 But some of them said, "By Beelzebul, the ruler of the demons, he throws out the demons."\\f + \\fr 11:15 \\ft Beelzebul is a mangled form of a Canaanite title of Baal, used in Israel as a name for the chief of the demons; a version of it turns up as "Baal-zebub, the god of Ekron," whom a king of Israel once sent to consult (2 Kings 1:2-3). The charge is not that the exorcism failed. It is that it worked, and by the wrong power.\\f*
\\v 16 And others, testing him, were seeking from him a sign out of heaven.
\\p
\\v 17 But he knew their thoughts and said to them, "Every kingdom divided against itself is laid waste, and house falls on house.
\\v 18 And if Satan too is divided against himself, how will his kingdom stand? — because you say that I throw out the demons by Beelzebul.
\\v 19 And if I throw out the demons by Beelzebul, by whom do your sons throw them out? For this reason they will be your judges.\\add [i.e. your own exorcists, who will not thank you for the standard you have just set]\\add*
\\v 20 But if I throw out the demons by the finger of God, then the kingdom of God has come upon you.\\f + \\fr 11:20 \\ft "The finger of God" is what Pharaoh's magicians said when they met a power they could not copy (Exodus 8:19), and it is what wrote the commandments on the stone tablets (Exodus 31:18). Pagan magicians recognized it; these men will not. And the verb is stronger than the one the seventy-two were given: the kingdom has not merely come near, it has arrived on top of you.\\f*
\\s1 The Strong Man and the Stronger
\\p
\\v 21 When the strong man, fully armed, guards his own courtyard, his belongings are in peace.
\\v 22 But when one stronger than he comes upon him and beats him, he takes away the armor he had trusted in, and divides up his spoils.\\f + \\fr 11:22 \\ft Isaiah asked whether prey can be taken from a strong man or captives rescued from a tyrant, and answered that God will do exactly that (Isaiah 49:24-25). The "spoils" being handed round are people. Every exorcism is a piece of the plunder.\\f*
\\v 23 The one who is not with me is against me, and the one who does not gather with me scatters.\\add [i.e. and in this fight there is no third place to stand]\\add*
\\s1 A House Swept and Left Empty
\\p
\\v 24 When the unclean spirit goes out of a man, it passes through waterless places looking for rest, and finding none it says, 'I will go back to my house from which I came out.'
\\v 25 And when it comes it finds it swept and put in order.\\add [i.e. clean, tidy, and unoccupied]\\add*
\\v 26 Then it goes and takes along seven other spirits more evil than itself, and they go in and settle there; and the last state of that man becomes worse than the first."\\add [i.e. being emptied of evil is not the same as being filled with anything]\\add*
\\p
\\v 27 And it happened, while he was saying these things, that a certain woman out of the crowd lifted up her voice and said to him, "Blessed is the womb that carried you and the breasts you nursed at!"
\\v 28 But he said, "Rather, blessed are those who hear the word of God and keep it."\\add [i.e. which is what Elizabeth said made my mother blessed in the first place]\\add*
\\s1 No Sign but the Sign of Jonah
\\p
\\v 29 And as the crowds were massing together, he began to say, "This generation is an evil generation; it seeks a sign, and no sign will be given to it except the sign of Jonah.
\\v 30 For just as Jonah became a sign to the Ninevites, so also the son of man will be to this generation.\\f + \\fr 11:30 \\ft Jonah performed no wonder in Nineveh. He arrived out of the sea and preached, and the city turned. He himself, having come through something like death, was the sign — which is as far as this account takes it.\\f*
\\v 31 The queen of the south will be raised at the judgment with the men of this generation and will condemn them, because she came from the ends of the earth to hear the wisdom of Solomon — and look, something more than Solomon is here.\\f + \\fr 11:31 \\ft The queen of Sheba (1 Kings 10:1-13). Both witnesses called here are foreigners: a Gentile queen who traveled to hear wisdom, and a Gentile city that repented at preaching. Neither asked for a sign first.\\f*
\\v 32 Men of Nineveh will stand up at the judgment with this generation and will condemn it, because they turned back at what Jonah proclaimed — and look, something more than Jonah is here.
\\s1 The Lamp and the Eye
\\p
\\v 33 No one who lights a lamp puts it in a cellar or under a basket, but on a lampstand, so that those coming in may see the light.\\add [i.e. the light is not hidden — so if you cannot see, the trouble is at your end]\\add*
\\v 34 The lamp of the body is your eye. When your eye is single, your whole body is full of light too; but when it is evil, your body is dark too.\\f + \\fr 11:34 \\ft The literal words are kept. In Hebrew idiom a "single" or "sound" eye is a generous one and an "evil" eye is a grudging, stingy one (Proverbs 22:9; 28:22; Deuteronomy 15:9). Both senses are running at once: how you see and how you give.\\f*
\\v 35 So watch out that the light in you is not darkness.\\add [i.e. the worst case is not blindness but a man certain he can see]\\add*
\\v 36 So if your whole body is full of light, with no part of it dark, it will be full of light all through, as when the lamp lights you with its flash."
\\s1 Woes on the Pharisees
\\p
\\v 37 And as he was speaking, a Pharisee asked him to take the morning meal with him; and he went in and reclined.
\\v 38 And the Pharisee, when he saw it, marveled that he did not first wash himself before the meal.\\f + \\fr 11:38 \\ft Not hygiene. The washing of hands before eating was a matter of ritual purity, and it was not a command of the law but a practice of the elders' tradition, designed to carry the holiness of the temple out into ordinary life.\\f*
\\v 39 But the Lord said to him, "Now you Pharisees clean the outside of the cup and the dish, but inside you are full of robbery and evil.
\\v 40 Fools! Did not the one who made the outside make the inside too?
\\v 41 But give what is inside as mercy to the poor, and look — everything is clean for you.\\add [i.e. real cleanness comes out through an open hand, not a scrubbed rim]\\add*
\\p
\\v 42 But woe to you Pharisees, because you tithe the mint and the rue and every garden herb, and you walk right past justice and the love of God. These you had to do — and not leave those aside.\\f + \\fr 11:42 \\ft The law requires tithing grain, wine and oil (Deuteronomy 14:22-23); tithing the herbs in the kitchen garden went beyond it. He does not tell them to stop. The charge is that men this careful over a leaf step over the two things the prophets said God actually wants (Micah 6:8; Isaiah 1:17).\\f*
\\v 43 Woe to you Pharisees, because you love the front seat in the synagogues and the greetings in the marketplaces.
\\v 44 Woe to you, because you are like the unmarked graves, and the men walking over them do not know it."\\f + \\fr 11:44 \\ft Graves were whitewashed so that people would not step on them and become unclean for seven days (Numbers 19:16). The charge against men whose whole concern is purity is that they are an unmarked source of the opposite, and that those they teach are defiled without ever knowing it.\\f*
\\s1 Woes on the Lawyers
\\p
\\v 45 And one of the lawyers answered and said to him, "Teacher, when you say these things you insult us too."
\\v 46 And he said, "Woe to you lawyers as well, because you load men down with loads hard to carry, and you yourselves do not touch the loads with one of your fingers.\\add [i.e. one finger, when the finger of God is out lifting people up]\\add*
\\v 47 Woe to you, because you build the tombs of the prophets — and your fathers killed them.
\\v 48 So you are witnesses, and you agree with the works of your fathers, because they killed them and you build.\\add [i.e. they did the burying and you do the monuments, which is the same job finished]\\add*
\\p
\\v 49 For this reason also the wisdom of God said, 'I will send them prophets and apostles, and some of them they will kill and drive out,'
\\v 50 so that the blood of all the prophets that has been poured out from the founding of the world may be required of this generation,
\\v 51 from the blood of Abel to the blood of Zechariah, who was destroyed between the altar and the house.\\f + \\fr 11:51 \\ft Abel is the first murder in the scriptures (Genesis 4:8-10) and Zechariah son of Jehoiada, stoned in the temple court, is the last (2 Chronicles 24:20-22) — last because Chronicles stands at the end of the Hebrew Bible. The pair means: the whole record, first page to last.\\f* Yes, I tell you, it will be required of this generation.
\\v 52 Woe to you lawyers, because you took away the key of knowledge; you did not go in yourselves, and you blocked those who were going in."\\add [i.e. you were given the keys in order to open the door]\\add*
\\p
\\v 53 And when he went out from there, the scribes and the Pharisees began to press him hard and to interrogate him about many things,
\\v 54 lying in wait for him, to catch something out of his mouth.\\f + \\fr 11:54 \\ft The verbs are from hunting: lying in ambush, and taking prey. Many manuscripts add "so that they might accuse him" at the end; the oldest Greek does not, and does not need to.\\f*
\\c 12
\\s1 The Leaven of the Pharisees
\\p
\\v 1 Meanwhile, when the crowd had gathered by the tens of thousands, so that they were trampling on one another, he began to say to his disciples first: "Guard yourselves against the leaven of the Pharisees, which is hypocrisy.\\f + \\fr 12:1 \\ft Leaven is the image for something small that works all the way through a batch; before Passover every trace of it had to be cleared out of the house (Exodus 12:15). "Hypocrisy" is the actor's word again — the gap between the part being played and the person playing it.\\f*
\\v 2 But there is nothing covered up that will not be uncovered, and nothing hidden that will not be known.
\\v 3 So whatever you have said in the darkness will be heard in the light, and what you have spoken into the ear in the inner rooms will be proclaimed from the housetops.\\add [i.e. the mask comes off eventually, so do not put one on]\\add*
\\s1 Whom to Fear
\\p
\\v 4 And I say to you, my friends: do not be afraid of those who kill the body and after that have nothing more they can do.
\\v 5 But I will show you whom to fear: fear the one who, after killing, has authority to throw into Gehenna. Yes, I tell you, fear this one.\\f + \\fr 12:5 \\ft Gehenna is a real place — the Valley of Hinnom, on the south side of Jerusalem, where children had been burned in sacrifice under the worst of the kings and which Josiah then defiled to put a stop to it (2 Kings 23:10; Jeremiah 7:31-32). Its name had become the standard word for the fire of God's judgment.\\f*
\\v 6 Are not five sparrows sold for two pennies? And not one of them is forgotten in front of God.\\add [i.e. the cheapest thing on the market, and he keeps count of them]\\add*
\\v 7 But even the hairs of your head have all been counted. Do not be afraid; you are worth more than many sparrows.
\\s1 Saying Openly That You Are His
\\p
\\v 8 And I tell you, everyone who says openly that he is mine in front of men, the son of man will say the same of him in front of the angels of God.
\\v 9 But the one who denies me in front of men will be denied in front of the angels of God.\\add [i.e. what is done in the human court is matched in the heavenly one]\\add*
\\v 10 And everyone who speaks a word against the son of man, it will be forgiven him; but to the one who blasphemes against the Holy Spirit it will not be forgiven.\\f + \\fr 12:10 \\ft Read against the accusation just made (11:15). Failing to recognize the man is pardonable; his identity is genuinely veiled. Watching the Spirit of God at work and calling it demonic is a different thing — it is not a failure of sight but an inversion of it, and it leaves no faculty by which the truth could be received.\\f*
\\v 11 And when they bring you before the synagogues and the rulers and the authorities, do not be anxious about how or what you should say in your defense, or what you should say,
\\v 12 for the Holy Spirit will teach you in that very hour what must be said."\\add [i.e. not provision laid up in advance, but given at the moment]\\add*
\\s1 The Rich Man and His Barns
\\p
\\v 13 And someone out of the crowd said to him, "Teacher, tell my brother to divide the inheritance with me."
\\v 14 But he said to him, "Man, who set me as judge or divider over you?"\\f + \\fr 12:14 \\ft Almost the words thrown at Moses by an Israelite who did not want his arbitration: "Who made you a ruler and judge over us?" (Exodus 2:14). Settling estates was ordinary rabbinic business; he refuses the role, and then goes after the thing underneath the request.\\f*
\\v 15 And he said to them, "Watch out and guard yourselves against every kind of greed, because a man's life is not in the abundance of what he owns."
\\p
\\v 16 And he told them a parable: "The land of a certain rich man produced well.
\\v 17 And he was reasoning within himself, 'What shall I do, because I have nowhere to gather my crops?'
\\v 18 And he said, 'This is what I will do: I will pull down my barns and build bigger ones, and there I will gather all the grain and my goods,
\\v 19 and I will say to my own self, "Self, you have many good things laid up for many years; take your rest, eat, drink, be glad."'\\add [i.e. count the pronouns — there is nobody else in this man's world]\\add*
\\v 20 But God said to him, 'Fool! This very night they are demanding your self back from you; and the things you got ready — whose will they be?'\\f + \\fr 12:20 \\ft In Israel's wisdom writing the "fool" is not the stupid man but the one who lives as though God were not there: "the fool has said in his heart, There is no God" (Psalm 14:1). He never said it; he simply planned a whole life without mentioning him. And the self he addressed as his own is now being called in.\\f*
\\v 21 So it is with the one who piles up treasure for himself and is not rich toward God."
\\s1 Do Not Be Anxious
\\p
\\v 22 And he said to his disciples, "For this reason I tell you, do not be anxious about your life, what you will eat, nor about your body, what you will wear.
\\v 23 For life is more than food, and the body more than clothing.
\\v 24 Think about the ravens: they neither sow nor reap; they have no storeroom or barn, and God feeds them. How much more are you worth than the birds!\\f + \\fr 12:24 \\ft The raven is on the list of unclean birds (Leviticus 11:15) — and God once used ravens to keep Elijah alive (1 Kings 17:4-6). They have no barn; the man in the parable had barns, and died. The words are deliberately the same.\\f*
\\v 25 And which of you by being anxious can add a single forearm's length to the span of his life?\\f + \\fr 12:25 \\ft The Greek word means both a span of life and a person's height, and the measure is a cubit — a forearm's length. So the sentence is either "add a cubit to his height" or, as here, add a short stretch to his lifetime. Either way the point is that anxiety cannot move the measure at all.\\f*
\\v 26 So if you cannot do even the smallest thing, why are you anxious about the rest?
\\p
\\v 27 Think about the lilies, how they grow: they neither work nor spin; but I tell you, not even Solomon in all his glory was dressed like one of these.
\\v 28 And if God so clothes the grass in the field, which is there today and tomorrow is thrown into an oven, how much more you, you of little trust!\\add [i.e. dried weeds were the fuel a village oven burned]\\add*
\\v 29 And you, do not go searching for what you will eat and what you will drink, and do not be left hanging in the air.
\\v 30 For all the nations of the world go hard after these things, and your Father knows that you need them.\\add [i.e. that scramble is what people do who have no father to go to]\\add*
\\v 31 But seek his kingdom, and these things will be added to you.\\f + \\fr 12:31 \\ft The oldest Greek reads "his kingdom"; most older English versions have "the kingdom of God."\\f*
\\s1 Treasure That Does Not Wear Out
\\p
\\v 32 Do not be afraid, little flock, because your Father was pleased to give you the kingdom.\\add [i.e. pleased to — this is not grudging, and it is not earned]\\add*
\\v 33 Sell what you have and give as mercy to the poor; make yourselves purses that do not wear out, an unfailing treasure in the heavens, where no thief comes near and no moth destroys.\\add [i.e. the seventy-two were told to carry no purse; here is the one to carry]\\add*
\\v 34 For where your treasure is, there your heart will be too.
\\s1 Dressed and Waiting for the Master
\\p
\\v 35 Let your loins be belted and your lamps burning,\\f + \\fr 12:35 \\ft Belting up the long robe frees the legs to move. It is how Israel was told to eat the first Passover — "your loins girded, your sandals on your feet, your staff in your hand, and you shall eat it in haste" (Exodus 12:11). Ready to go at a moment's notice.\\f*
\\v 36 and you yourselves like men waiting for their lord to come back from the wedding feast, so that when he comes and knocks they may open to him at once.
\\v 37 Blessed are those slaves whom the lord, when he comes, finds watching. Amen, I say to you, he will belt himself and have them recline at table, and he will come and serve them.\\add [i.e. the master waiting on his own slaves, which no household in the world had ever seen]\\add*
\\v 38 And if he comes in the second watch, or in the third, and finds them so, blessed are they.\\add [i.e. the dead middle of the night, when staying awake is hardest]\\add*
\\v 39 But know this: if the master of the house had known at what hour the thief was coming, he would not have let his house be broken into.\\f + \\fr 12:39 \\ft Many manuscripts add "he would have watched, and" before "he would not have let"; the oldest Greek does not.\\f*
\\v 40 You too be ready, because at an hour you do not think it, the son of man comes."
\\s1 The Steward the Master Finds at Work
\\p
\\v 41 And Peter said, "Lord, are you telling this parable for us, or for everyone as well?"
\\v 42 And the Lord said, "Who then is the trustworthy steward, the wise one, whom the lord will set over his household staff to give them their measure of grain at the right time?\\add [i.e. a slave himself, put in charge of feeding the others]\\add*
\\v 43 Blessed is that slave whom his lord, when he comes, will find doing so.
\\v 44 Truly I tell you, he will set him over everything he owns.
\\v 45 But if that slave says in his heart, 'My lord is slow in coming,' and begins to beat the boys and the girls, and to eat and drink and get drunk,\\add [i.e. delay is what exposes him — the man appointed to feed the household starts feeding on it]\\add*
\\v 46 the lord of that slave will come on a day he does not expect and at an hour he does not know, and will cut him in two and put his portion with those who cannot be trusted.
\\v 47 And that slave who knew his lord's will and did not get ready or act according to his will, will be beaten with many blows.
\\v 48 But the one who did not know, and did things that deserve blows, will be beaten with few. And from everyone to whom much was given, much will be looked for; and from the one they entrusted much to, they will ask all the more.\\add [i.e. which answers Peter's question — it is for everyone, and hardest of all for you]\\add*
\\s1 Fire, and Division
\\p
\\v 49 I came to throw fire on the earth, and how I wish it were already kindled!\\f + \\fr 12:49 \\ft Fire in the prophets is God arriving — at the bush, on Sinai, in the pillar — and it is also judgment and refining (Malachi 3:2-3; 4:1). John had said the one coming would immerse in the Holy Spirit and fire (3:16). All of that is behind the sentence.\\f*
\\v 50 But I have an immersion to be immersed with, and how hemmed in I am until it is finished!\\f + \\fr 12:50 \\ft Not water. To be immersed here is to be gone under, and the psalms use exactly that image for being overwhelmed by disaster: "all your breakers and your waves have gone over me" (Psalm 42:7; 69:1-2). He is speaking of his death, and the word rendered "hemmed in" means squeezed, under pressure.\\f*
\\v 51 Do you think that I came to give peace on the earth? No, I tell you, but rather division.\\add [i.e. the peace the angels announced is real, but it is not social calm, and not yet]\\add*
\\v 52 For from now on there will be five in one house divided, three against two and two against three.
\\v 53 They will be divided, father against son and son against father, mother against daughter and daughter against mother, mother-in-law against her daughter-in-law and daughter-in-law against the mother-in-law."\\f + \\fr 12:53 \\ft The list is Micah 7:6, where it describes a society coming apart: "a man's enemies are the men of his own household." He applies it to the effect of his own arrival, because what he asks for cannot be given by half a household.\\f*
\\s1 You Cannot Read This Present Time
\\p
\\v 54 And he was saying to the crowds as well, "When you see a cloud rising in the west, at once you say, 'A rainstorm is coming,' and so it happens.\\add [i.e. off the Mediterranean, and it always means rain]\\add*
\\v 55 And when a south wind is blowing, you say, 'There will be scorching heat,' and it happens.\\add [i.e. off the desert]\\add*
\\v 56 Hypocrites! You know how to read the face of the earth and the sky; how is it that you do not know how to read this present time?\\add [i.e. you can do it; you are choosing not to]\\add*
\\s1 Settle on the Road
\\p
\\v 57 And why do you not judge for yourselves what is right?
\\v 58 For as you are going with your opponent to a ruler, make an effort on the road to be done with him, so that he does not drag you to the judge, and the judge hand you over to the officer, and the officer throw you into prison.\\add [i.e. and you are on that road now, with the courthouse in sight]\\add*
\\v 59 I tell you, you will never come out from there until you have paid back even the last small coin."
\\c 13
\\s1 Unless You Turn Back
\\p
\\v 1 And there were some present at that very time who reported to him about the Galileans whose blood Pilate had mixed with their sacrifices.\\f + \\fr 13:1 \\ft No other surviving source records this. Pilate's soldiers evidently cut men down while they were in the act of offering — a political killing and a desecration in one stroke, in the one place a worshipper was supposed to be safe.\\f*
\\v 2 And he answered and said to them, "Do you think that these Galileans were sinners beyond all the other Galileans, because they suffered these things?\\add [i.e. you are asking what they did to deserve it, and that is the wrong question]\\add*
\\v 3 No, I tell you; but unless you turn back, you will all be destroyed in the same way.
\\v 4 Or those eighteen on whom the tower in Siloam fell and killed them — do you think that they were in debt beyond all the men living in Jerusalem?\\add [i.e. and here is a second case, which nobody brought to me]\\add*
\\v 5 No, I tell you; but unless you turn back, you will all be destroyed in the same way."
\\s1 One More Year for the Fig Tree
\\p
\\v 6 And he was telling this parable: "A certain man had a fig tree planted in his vineyard, and he came looking for fruit on it and found none.\\f + \\fr 13:6 \\ft A vineyard planted by an owner who comes looking for fruit and finds none is Isaiah 5:1-7, and Isaiah says outright what the vineyard is: "the vineyard of the LORD of hosts is the house of Israel." The fig tree standing fruitless in it is the same picture the prophets use of the nation (Jeremiah 8:13; Hosea 9:10; Micah 7:1).\\f*
\\v 7 And he said to the vinedresser, 'Look — for three years I have been coming and looking for fruit on this fig tree and finding none. Cut it down. Why should it use up the ground?'\\add [i.e. a barren tree is not harmless; it is taking the place of something that would bear]\\add*
\\v 8 But he answered and said to him, 'Lord, leave it this year too, until I dig around it and throw on manure.
\\v 9 And if it makes fruit next year — well; but if not, you will cut it down.'"\\add [i.e. there is more time, and there is not unlimited time]\\add*
\\s1 A Daughter of Abraham Released on the Sabbath
\\p
\\v 10 And he was teaching in one of the synagogues on the Sabbath.
\\v 11 And look — a woman who had a spirit of weakness for eighteen years, and she was bent double and could not straighten up at all.\\add [i.e. eighteen years of seeing nothing but the ground]\\add*
\\v 12 And when Jesus saw her he called out to her and said, "Woman, you have been released from your weakness."\\add [i.e. she has not asked him for anything; he sees her and calls her over]\\add*
\\v 13 And he laid his hands on her, and at once she was made straight and began to glorify God.\\f + \\fr 13:13 \\ft "Made straight" is the word the Greek psalm uses of God: "the LORD raises up those who are bowed down" (Psalm 146:8).\\f*
\\p
\\v 14 But the ruler of the synagogue answered, full of indignation because Jesus had healed on the Sabbath, and he said to the crowd, "There are six days on which work must be done; so come on those and be healed, and not on the day of the Sabbath."\\add [i.e. as though eighteen years of this could be fitted into an appointment]\\add*
\\v 15 But the Lord answered him and said, "Hypocrites! Does not each of you on the Sabbath untie his ox or his donkey from the feeding trough and lead it off to give it a drink?
\\v 16 And this woman, a daughter of Abraham, whom Satan bound — look — for eighteen years, should she not be untied from this bond on the day of the Sabbath?"\\f + \\fr 13:16 \\ft The argument runs from the small case to the large one: you untie livestock on the Sabbath, so what about a member of the covenant people? "Daughter of Abraham" is a rare phrase and it is doing work — she is not a marginal figure but family. And the Sabbath itself was given as a memorial of coming out of slavery (Deuteronomy 5:15), which makes it exactly the right day to cut somebody loose.\\f*
\\v 17 And as he said this, all who were set against him were put to shame, and the whole crowd was rejoicing at all the glorious things being done by him.
\\s1 Mustard Seed and Leaven
\\p
\\v 18 So he was saying, "What is the kingdom of God like, and to what shall I compare it?
\\v 19 It is like a mustard seed, which a man took and threw into his own garden; and it grew and became a tree, and the birds of the sky nested in its branches."\\f + \\fr 13:19 \\ft Birds nesting in the branches is a prophet's picture of a kingdom big enough to shelter the nations (Ezekiel 17:22-24; Daniel 4:10-12). The contrast is the point: what is happening in rural synagogues looks like nothing, and it is that.\\f*
\\p
\\v 20 And again he said, "To what shall I compare the kingdom of God?
\\v 21 It is like leaven, which a woman took and hid in three measures of flour until the whole of it was leavened."\\add [i.e. some fifty pounds of flour, and a pinch of leaven working invisibly all the way through it]\\add*
\\s1 The Narrow Door
\\p
\\v 22 And he was going through town after town and village after village, teaching and making his journey toward Jerusalem.
\\v 23 And someone said to him, "Lord, are those being rescued few?" And he said to them,
\\v 24 "Struggle to get in through the narrow door, because many, I tell you, will look to get in and will not be strong enough.\\add [i.e. you asked me how many; I am telling you to get through]\\add*
\\v 25 From the time the master of the house gets up and shuts the door, and you begin to stand outside and knock at the door, saying, 'Lord, open to us,' he will answer and say to you, 'I do not know you, where you are from.'
\\v 26 Then you will begin to say, 'We ate and drank in front of you, and you taught in our streets.'\\add [i.e. we were there — which turns out not to be the same as having answered]\\add*
\\v 27 And he will say, speaking to you, 'I do not know where you are from. Stand away from me, all you workers of injustice.'
\\p
\\v 28 There will be weeping and grinding of teeth, when you see Abraham and Isaac and Jacob and all the prophets in the kingdom of God, and yourselves thrown outside.
\\v 29 And they will come from east and west and from north and south, and they will recline at table in the kingdom of God.\\f + \\fr 13:29 \\ft The feast Isaiah promised on God's mountain "for all peoples" (Isaiah 25:6). The four directions mean everywhere, and it is the outsiders who are taking the places at the table.\\f*
\\v 30 And look — there are last who will be first, and there are first who will be last."
\\s1 Go and Tell That Fox
\\p
\\v 31 In that same hour some Pharisees came and said to him, "Get out and go from here, because Herod wants to kill you."
\\v 32 And he said to them, "Go and tell that fox, 'Look — I am throwing out demons and finishing healings today and tomorrow, and on the third day I am brought to my goal.'\\f + \\fr 13:32 \\ft A fox is sly, and it is also small; in Nehemiah 4:3 a fox is the thing so slight that even it could knock the wall down. The three days are a Hebrew way of saying a short, fixed span (Hosea 6:2). Herod does not set this timetable.\\f*
\\v 33 But I must go on today and tomorrow and the next day, because it cannot be that a prophet should be destroyed outside Jerusalem.\\add [i.e. said bitterly — that city has the franchise]\\add*
\\s1 Jerusalem, Jerusalem
\\p
\\v 34 Jerusalem, Jerusalem, the one who kills the prophets and stones those sent to her! How often I wanted to gather your children together, the way a hen gathers her chicks under her wings — and you were not willing.\\f + \\fr 13:34 \\ft Sheltering under wings is God's own image for what he does with his people: an eagle carrying its young (Deuteronomy 32:11), refuge in the shadow of his wings (Psalm 17:8; 36:7; 91:4; Ruth 2:12). It is a mother's word for it, and it is offered here by the one being turned away.\\f*
\\v 35 Look — your house is left to you.\\f + \\fr 13:35 \\ft Many manuscripts add "desolate"; the oldest Greek reads simply "your house is left to you." The threat is in what is not said: left to you, and no longer occupied. Ezekiel watched the glory of God move out of the temple by stages before the Babylonians came (Ezekiel 10-11).\\f* And I tell you, you will never see me until the time comes when you say, 'Blessed is the one who comes in the name of the Lord.'"\\f + \\fr 13:35 \\ft Psalm 118:26, the greeting sung to pilgrims arriving at the temple — and the words the crowd will shout at 19:38. The same psalm contains the line about the stone the builders rejected, which he will quote against the builders at 20:17.\\f*
\\c 14
\\s1 At a Pharisee's Table on the Sabbath
\\p
\\v 1 And it happened, when he went into the house of one of the rulers of the Pharisees on a Sabbath to eat bread, that they were watching him closely.
\\v 2 And look — a certain man was in front of him who had dropsy.\\f + \\fr 14:2 \\ft A condition in which the body swells with fluid; in ancient writing it was the standard image for a craving that only grows the more it is fed. The man is placed directly in front of Jesus at a meal where he is already under observation.\\f*
\\v 3 And Jesus answered and said to the lawyers and Pharisees, "Is it permitted to heal on the Sabbath, or not?"\\add [i.e. answering a question no one had asked out loud]\\add*
\\v 4 But they kept quiet. And taking hold of him, he healed him and sent him away.
\\v 5 And he said to them, "Which of you, if a son or an ox of his falls into a well, will not pull him out at once on the day of the Sabbath?"\\f + \\fr 14:5 \\ft The oldest Greek reads "a son or an ox"; some manuscripts have "a donkey or an ox," matching the pairing at 13:15.\\f*
\\v 6 And they were not able to answer back to this.
\\s1 Take the Last Place
\\p
\\v 7 And he told a parable to those who had been invited, when he noticed how they were picking out the first places at table. He said to them,\\f + \\fr 14:7 \\ft At a formal meal the guests reclined, and where a man reclined stated exactly where he stood — the places nearest the host were the honored ones. Working out the seating, and jockeying for it, was a recognized social business.\\f*
\\v 8 "When you are invited by someone to a wedding feast, do not recline in the first place, in case someone more honored than you has been invited by him,
\\v 9 and the one who invited you both will come and say to you, 'Give this man the place,' and then you will begin with shame to take the last place.
\\v 10 But when you are invited, go and recline in the last place, so that when the one who invited you comes he will say to you, 'Friend, move up higher.' Then you will have honor in front of all who are reclining with you.\\f + \\fr 14:10 \\ft On the surface this is the practical advice of Proverbs 25:6-7 — do not put yourself forward in the king's presence, because being told to come up is better than being told to move down. The next line lifts it out of the realm of tactics.\\f*
\\v 11 Because everyone who lifts himself up will be brought low, and whoever brings himself low will be lifted up."\\add [i.e. and it is God who does the lifting and the lowering, so this is not a technique]\\add*
\\s1 Invite Those Who Cannot Pay You Back
\\p
\\v 12 And he also said to the one who had invited him, "When you make a lunch or a dinner, do not call your friends or your brothers or your relatives or rich neighbors, in case they invite you back in return and it becomes your repayment.\\add [i.e. an invitation that will be returned is not a gift; it is a transaction]\\add*
\\v 13 But when you make a reception, invite poor people, crippled, lame, blind —\\f + \\fr 14:13 \\ft This is the list of conditions that barred a man from serving at the altar (Leviticus 21:17-23), and the community at Qumran used a list like it to bar people from the messianic banquet. The people excluded from the holy table are exactly the ones he says to seat at yours.\\f*
\\v 14 and you will be blessed, because they have nothing to repay you with. For you will be repaid at the standing up of the righteous."
\\s1 The Great Dinner
\\p
\\v 15 When one of those reclining with him heard this, he said to him, "Blessed is whoever will eat bread in the kingdom of God."\\add [i.e. a pious remark, made by a man who assumes he will be at that table]\\add*
\\p
\\v 16 But he said to him, "A certain man was making a great dinner, and he invited many.
\\v 17 And at the hour of the dinner he sent his slave to say to those who had been invited, 'Come, because it is ready now.'\\f + \\fr 14:17 \\ft Invitations came in two stages: a first notice, accepted in advance, and then the summons when the food was ready. These guests have already said yes. What follows is not a refusal of an invitation but the breaking of a commitment at the last moment, with the food on the table.\\f*
\\v 18 And with one accord they all began to excuse themselves. The first said to him, 'I bought a field and I must go out and see it; I ask you, hold me excused.'\\add [i.e. a field he has already bought, which will still be there tomorrow]\\add*
\\v 19 And another said, 'I bought five yoke of oxen and I am going to try them out; I ask you, hold me excused.'
\\v 20 And another said, 'I married a wife, and for this reason I cannot come.'\\f + \\fr 14:20 \\ft The law released a newly married man from military service for a year (Deuteronomy 24:5). He is stretching an exemption from war to cover a dinner, and unlike the other two he does not even ask to be excused.\\f*
\\p
\\v 21 And the slave came and reported these things to his lord. Then the master of the house grew angry and said to his slave, 'Go out quickly into the streets and lanes of the town, and bring in here the poor and crippled and blind and lame.'\\add [i.e. the very four he had just told his host to invite]\\add*
\\v 22 And the slave said, 'Lord, what you ordered has been done, and there is still room.'
\\v 23 And the lord said to the slave, 'Go out into the roads and the hedgerows and press them to come in, so that my house may be filled.\\add [i.e. out past the town altogether, to people who would never believe the invitation was meant for them]\\add*
\\v 24 For I tell you that none of those men who were invited will taste my dinner.'"
\\s1 Counting What It Costs
\\p
\\v 25 And large crowds were going along with him, and he turned and said to them,\\add [i.e. turning round on the people following him, to tell them what they are following]\\add*
\\v 26 "If anyone comes to me and does not hate his own father and mother and wife and children and brothers and sisters — and his own self as well — he cannot be my disciple.\\f + \\fr 14:26 \\ft In Hebrew usage "hate" beside "love" is the language of preference, not of feeling: Leah is called "hated" in a sentence that explains Jacob loved Rachel more (Genesis 29:30-31), and the law protects the inheritance of the son of the "hated" wife (Deuteronomy 21:15-17). The demand is for absolute priority, and the list is drawn to cover every tie a person has, including the tie to himself.\\f*
\\v 27 Whoever does not carry his own cross and come after me cannot be my disciple.
\\p
\\v 28 For which of you, wanting to build a tower, does not first sit down and count the cost, whether he has enough to finish it?
\\v 29 Otherwise, when he has laid the foundation and is not able to finish, everyone who sees it will begin to mock him,
\\v 30 saying, 'This man began to build and was not able to finish.'
\\p
\\v 31 Or what king, going out to meet another king in war, does not first sit down and take counsel whether he is able with ten thousand to meet the one coming against him with twenty thousand?
\\v 32 And if not, while the other is still far off he sends a delegation and asks for terms of peace.\\add [i.e. and the terms, at those odds, are surrender]\\add*
\\v 33 So then, every one of you who does not say goodbye to all his own belongings cannot be my disciple.
\\s1 Salt Gone Flat
\\p
\\v 34 Salt then is good; but if even the salt goes flat, with what will it be seasoned?\\f + \\fr 14:34 \\ft The salt sold in that country was scraped from deposits around the Dead Sea and was full of other minerals. Once the salt itself had leached out, what was left looked exactly the same and did nothing at all.\\f*
\\v 35 It is fit neither for the soil nor for the manure pile; they throw it out. Whoever has ears to hear, let him hear."
\\c 15
\\s1 This Man Welcomes Sinners
\\p
\\v 1 And all the tax collectors and the sinners were drawing near to him to hear him.
\\v 2 And both the Pharisees and the scribes were grumbling and saying, "This man welcomes sinners and eats with them."\\add [i.e. and the three stories that follow are his answer to that one sentence]\\add*
\\v 3 And he told them this parable:\\f + \\fr 15:3 \\ft "This parable," singular, though three stories follow. They are one argument, and the third is where it lands.\\f*
\\s1 The Lost Sheep
\\p
\\v 4 "What man among you, having a hundred sheep and losing one of them, does not leave the ninety-nine in the wilderness and go after the lost one until he finds it?\\f + \\fr 15:4 \\ft God had already said he would do this himself, and said it against the leaders of Israel who would not: "Because there was no shepherd, my sheep became food... I myself will search for my sheep and seek them out... I will seek the lost and bring back the strayed" (Ezekiel 34:8, 11, 16). The men grumbling at him are being told a story in which they are the shepherds who did not go.\\f*
\\v 5 And when he finds it, he lays it on his shoulders, rejoicing.\\add [i.e. and the sheep does nothing at all — it is carried]\\add*
\\v 6 And coming into the house, he calls together his friends and his neighbors and says to them, 'Rejoice with me, because I found my sheep that was lost.'
\\v 7 I tell you that in the same way there will be joy in heaven over one sinner turning back, more than over ninety-nine righteous people who have no need of turning back.
\\s1 The Lost Drachma
\\p
\\v 8 Or what woman, having ten drachmas, if she loses one drachma, does not light a lamp and sweep the house and search carefully until she finds it?\\f + \\fr 15:8 \\ft A drachma was about a day's wage. Ten of them, for a woman of modest means, is a great deal; and a village house had a packed earth floor, few windows, and cracks a coin could disappear into. One in ten is a heavier loss than one in a hundred, and the stakes go up again with the third story.\\f*
\\v 9 And when she finds it, she calls together her friends and neighbors and says, 'Rejoice with me, because I found the drachma I lost.'
\\v 10 In the same way, I tell you, there is joy in front of the angels of God over one sinner turning back."\\add [i.e. joy in front of them, which is to say God's own joy, with the court of heaven watching]\\add*
\\s1 A Man Had Two Sons
\\p
\\v 11 And he said, "A certain man had two sons.
\\v 12 And the younger of them said to the father, 'Father, give me the share of the property that falls to me.'\\f + \\fr 15:12 \\ft The elder son took a double share (Deuteronomy 21:17), so the younger is asking for a third — and asking for it while his father is alive and well, which amounts to wishing him dead. That the father simply divides it is as startling as the request.\\f* And he divided his living between them.
\\p
\\v 13 And not many days later the younger son gathered everything together and traveled away into a distant country, and there he scattered his property, living wastefully.\\add [i.e. a far country, which in Israel's memory is where you go when you are sent away]\\add*
\\v 14 And when he had spent everything, a severe famine came over that whole country, and he began to be in need.
\\v 15 And he went and attached himself to one of the citizens of that country, who sent him into his fields to feed pigs.\\add [i.e. an Israelite hired to keep unclean animals, which is as far out as it goes]\\add*
\\v 16 And he was longing to be fed from the carob pods that the pigs were eating, and no one was giving him anything.
\\p
\\v 17 But coming to himself he said, 'How many of my father's hired men have more than enough bread, and here I am, being destroyed by famine!\\add [i.e. as though he had been out of his mind and had just woken up in it]\\add*
\\v 18 I will get up and go to my father and say to him, "Father, I sinned against heaven and in front of you;
\\v 19 I am no longer worthy to be called your son. Make me like one of your hired men."'
\\p
\\v 20 And he got up and went to his own father. And while he was still a long way off, his father saw him\\add [i.e. saw him, because he had been watching that road]\\add* and his heart went out to him, and he ran and fell on his neck and kissed him.\\f + \\fr 15:20 \\ft An older man of standing did not run. It meant gathering up the long robe and showing the legs, and it cost him his dignity in front of the village. He does it anyway, and he reaches the boy before the boy can reach the house.\\f*
\\v 21 And the son said to him, 'Father, I sinned against heaven and in front of you; I am no longer worthy to be called your son.'\\add [i.e. and the third line he had rehearsed, about being made a hired man, he never gets to say]\\add*
\\p
\\v 22 But the father said to his slaves, 'Quick, bring out the best robe and put it on him, and put a ring on his hand and sandals on his feet.\\f + \\fr 15:22 \\ft Three things, and none of them ornament. The best robe is the honor of the house put back on him; the ring carries the household's authority, the seal used to transact its business; sandals are what a free man wears and a slave does not. He asked to be made a hired hand and is dressed as a son before he can finish the sentence.\\f*
\\v 23 And bring the fattened calf, slaughter it, and let us eat and be glad,\\add [i.e. the animal kept back for the one occasion big enough to need it]\\add*
\\v 24 because this son of mine was dead and has come back to life; he was lost and has been found.' And they began to be glad.
\\s1 The Older Son Outside the House
\\p
\\v 25 Now his older son was out in the field; and as he came and drew near to the house, he heard music and dancing.
\\v 26 And calling one of the boys to him, he began to ask what these things might be.
\\v 27 And he said to him, 'Your brother has come, and your father has slaughtered the fattened calf, because he got him back in good health.'
\\p
\\v 28 But he was angry and did not want to go in. And his father came out and began to plead with him.\\add [i.e. out of the house a second time, to a second son who is not inside it]\\add*
\\v 29 But he answered and said to his father, 'Look — all these years I have been slaving for you, and I never went past a command of yours, and you never gave me a young goat so that I could be glad with my friends.\\f + \\fr 15:29 \\ft Every word gives him away. He describes life in his father's house as slavery and his relationship to him as a record of commands not broken. And the property was already divided (verse 12): everything he is complaining of not being given is his.\\f*
\\v 30 But when this son of yours came, the one who ate up your living with prostitutes, you slaughtered the fattened calf for him.'\\add [i.e. this son of yours, not my brother — and the prostitutes are his own addition to the story]\\add*
\\p
\\v 31 But he said to him, 'Child, you are always with me, and everything of mine is yours.
\\v 32 But it was right to be glad and rejoice, because this brother of yours was dead and has come to life; he was lost and has been found.'"\\f + \\fr 15:32 \\ft "This brother of yours," correcting him. And the story stops there. We are never told whether the older son goes in, because the men Jesus is telling it to are standing where he is standing, and the ending is theirs to write.\\f*
\\c 16
\\s1 The Steward Who Acted Shrewdly
\\p
\\v 1 And he was also saying to the disciples, "There was a certain rich man who had a steward, and this man was accused to him of scattering his belongings.\\f + \\fr 16:1 \\ft A steward ran the estate and transacted business in the owner's name — a position of complete trust, and one in which nothing he handled was his own. "Scattering" is the same verb used of the younger son wasting his inheritance (15:13). The difference is that this man is scattering someone else's.\\f*
\\v 2 And calling him, he said to him, 'What is this I hear about you? Hand over the account of your stewardship, for you can no longer be steward.'
\\v 3 And the steward said within himself, 'What shall I do, since my lord is taking the stewardship away from me? I am not strong enough to dig, and I am ashamed to beg.
\\v 4 I know what I will do, so that when I am put out of the stewardship they will welcome me into their houses.'
\\p
\\v 5 And calling each one of his lord's debtors to him, he said to the first, 'How much do you owe my lord?'
\\v 6 And he said, 'A hundred baths of oil.' And he said to him, 'Take your bill and sit down quickly and write fifty.'\\f + \\fr 16:6 \\ft Something like eight hundred gallons of olive oil — the yield of a large grove. The reductions differ sharply, half off the oil and a fifth off the wheat, which has suggested to many that what he is striking out is either his own commission or interest concealed in the stated principal. Lending at interest to a fellow Israelite was forbidden (Exodus 22:25; Leviticus 25:36-37), which would leave the master unable to object without incriminating himself. The story does not say, and does not need to: what is commended is not his honesty but his nerve.\\f*
\\v 7 Then he said to another, 'And you, how much do you owe?' And he said, 'A hundred kors of wheat.' He said to him, 'Take your bill and write eighty.'
\\p
\\v 8 And the lord praised the unjust steward, because he had acted shrewdly; because the sons of this age are shrewder toward their own generation than the sons of light.\\add [i.e. and that is the sting — they apply more intelligence to their world than you do to yours]\\add*
\\v 9 And I tell you, make friends for yourselves out of the mammon of injustice, so that when it gives out they may welcome you into the tents of the age.\\f + \\fr 16:9 \\ft "Mammon" is simply the Aramaic word for wealth or property. Money will give out — that is the one thing certain about it — so spend it now on what will still be standing when it does. The "tents" are the lasting kind, unlike the temporary shelter the steward bought himself.\\f*
\\s1 God, or Mammon
\\p
\\v 10 The one who is trustworthy in the smallest thing is trustworthy in much too, and the one who is unjust in the smallest thing is unjust in much too.
\\v 11 So if you have not been trustworthy with the unjust mammon, who will trust you with what is true?\\add [i.e. money is the small thing here, whatever the world thinks of it]\\add*
\\v 12 And if you have not been trustworthy with what belongs to another, who will give you what is your own?\\add [i.e. and none of it was ever yours; you are the steward in the story]\\add*
\\v 13 No household slave can be a slave to two lords; for either he will hate the one and love the other, or he will hold to the one and think nothing of the other. You cannot be a slave to God and to mammon."
\\s1 What Is High Among Men
\\p
\\v 14 And the Pharisees, who were lovers of silver, heard all these things and were turning up their noses at him.\\f + \\fr 16:14 \\ft The verb means literally to turn up the nose, and it occurs only twice in this book: here, and at 23:35, where the rulers sneer at him on the cross.\\f*
\\v 15 And he said to them, "You are the ones who set yourselves right in front of men, but God knows your hearts; because what is high among men is a disgusting thing in front of God.\\f + \\fr 16:15 \\ft The word rendered "disgusting thing" is the one the Greek Old Testament reserves for idolatry and for what defiles the temple (Deuteronomy 7:25-26; Daniel 11:31). It is not a mild word. What society ranks highest, God files with the idols.\\f*
\\p
\\v 16 The law and the prophets were until John; from then on the good news of the kingdom of God is announced, and everyone is forcing his way into it.\\f + \\fr 16:16 \\ft The Greek verb can be read as "everyone is forcing his way in" or "everyone is being pressed in." The first is taken here, and it matches the narrow door of 13:24. Note who is doing the forcing in this book: tax collectors and sinners, while the men who know the law best stand outside sneering.\\f*
\\v 17 But it is easier for heaven and earth to pass away than for one stroke of a letter of the law to fall.\\add [i.e. the smallest hook on the smallest letter]\\add*
\\v 18 Everyone who divorces his wife and marries another commits adultery, and the one who marries a woman divorced from her husband commits adultery.\\f + \\fr 16:18 \\ft Placed here as a working example of the line just drawn. Deuteronomy 24:1-4 regulated divorce, and in the first century the schools argued over how wide the grounds were, one of them allowing almost anything. Divorce also had money in it — the settlement, the next marriage. He goes behind the regulation to what it was protecting, which is what "not one stroke will fall" actually means.\\f*
\\s1 The Rich Man and Lazarus
\\p
\\v 19 Now there was a certain rich man, and he used to dress in purple and fine linen, day after day glad in his splendor.\\f + \\fr 16:19 \\ft Purple came from a sea snail and cost a fortune; fine linen is the fabric of the priestly vestments and the tabernacle curtains. He wears daily, for his own pleasure, what was made for sacred use — and the word for his feasting is the same one used of the celebration for the son who came home (15:23-24, 32).\\f*
\\v 20 And a certain poor man named Lazarus had been laid at his gateway, covered with sores\\f + \\fr 16:20 \\ft The only person in any of these parables who is given a name, and the name means "God helps" — which is precisely what nothing in his life shows. He does not put himself at the gate; the verb says he was dumped there.\\f*
\\v 21 and longing to be fed from what fell from the rich man's table; and even the dogs would come and lick his sores.\\add [i.e. he is not turned away, he is simply not noticed; the dogs come closer to him than the household does]\\add*
\\p
\\v 22 And it happened that the poor man died and was carried away by the angels into Abraham's lap. And the rich man also died and was buried.
\\v 23 And in Hades, lifting up his eyes as he was in torments, he sees Abraham far off and Lazarus in his lap.\\add [i.e. the gate is still there, and he is on the wrong side of it now]\\add*
\\p
\\v 24 And he called out and said, 'Father Abraham, have mercy on me, and send Lazarus to dip the tip of his finger in water and cool my tongue, because I am in anguish in this flame.'\\add [i.e. he knows the man's name — he always did — and he is still trying to send him on errands]\\add*
\\v 25 But Abraham said, 'Child, remember that you got your good things in full during your life, and Lazarus likewise the bad things; but now he is comforted here, and you are in anguish.\\add [i.e. in full — as at 6:24, the account has been settled and there is nothing further owing]\\add*
\\v 26 And besides all this, between us and you a great chasm has been fixed, so that those wanting to cross over from here to you cannot, nor can they cross from there to us.'\\add [i.e. the crossing that is impossible now was the length of his own driveway then]\\add*
\\p
\\v 27 And he said, 'Then I ask you, father, to send him to my father's house —
\\v 28 for I have five brothers — so that he may bear witness to them, in case they too come into this place of torment.'
\\v 29 But Abraham said, 'They have Moses and the prophets; let them listen to them.'\\f + \\fr 16:29 \\ft And Moses and the prophets are not vague on the point. "You shall not harden your heart or shut your hand against your poor brother" (Deuteronomy 15:7-8); the fast God chooses is to share your bread with the hungry (Isaiah 58:6-7); and Ezekiel names the sin of Sodom as "arrogance, abundant food, and careless ease, and she did not help the poor and needy" (Ezekiel 16:49). The brothers do not need new information.\\f*
\\p
\\v 30 But he said, 'No, father Abraham, but if someone from the dead goes to them, they will turn back.'
\\v 31 But he said to him, 'If they do not listen to Moses and the prophets, neither will they be persuaded if someone rises from the dead.'"\\add [i.e. and a reader of this book knows that someone did, and how it was received]\\add*
\\c 17
\\s1 Trip-ups, Rebuke, and Forgiving Again
\\p
\\v 1 And he said to his disciples, "It is impossible for the trip-ups not to come, but woe to the one through whom they come.\\f + \\fr 17:1 \\ft The Greek word was originally the trigger stick of a trap. Not an obstacle someone happens to fall over, but something set that catches a person and brings them down.\\f*
\\v 2 It would pay him better if a millstone were hung around his neck and he were thrown into the sea, than that he should trip up one of these little ones.\\add [i.e. the upper stone of a mill, so heavy it took a donkey to turn it]\\add*
\\p
\\v 3 Watch yourselves. If your brother sins, rebuke him; and if he turns back, forgive him.\\f + \\fr 17:3 \\ft Rebuking is not optional here. The law puts it this way: "You shall not hate your brother in your heart; you shall surely reprove your neighbor" (Leviticus 19:17) — saying nothing is the form hatred takes.\\f*
\\v 4 And if he sins against you seven times in the day, and turns back to you seven times, saying, 'I am turning back,' you shall forgive him."\\add [i.e. and you are not asked to assess whether he means it]\\add*
\\s1 Trust Like a Mustard Seed
\\p
\\v 5 And the apostles said to the Lord, "Add trust to us."\\add [i.e. as though what they lack is a quantity]\\add*
\\v 6 And the Lord said, "If you have trust like a mustard seed, you would say to this mulberry tree, 'Be pulled up by the roots and planted in the sea,' and it would obey you.\\add [i.e. a tree famous for how deep it roots, and salt water that would kill it — the point is that the size of the trust is not the issue]\\add*
\\s1 Slaves With Nothing Owed Them
\\p
\\v 7 And which of you, having a slave plowing or shepherding, will say to him when he comes in from the field, 'Come at once and recline at table'?
\\v 8 Will he not rather say to him, 'Get something ready for me to eat, and belt yourself and serve me while I eat and drink, and after that you will eat and drink'?
\\v 9 Does he thank the slave because he did what he was ordered?
\\v 10 So you too, when you have done everything you were ordered, say, 'We are slaves with nothing owed us; we have done what we were bound to do.'"\\f + \\fr 17:10 \\ft The word rendered "with nothing owed us" means unprofitable in the accounting sense — no surplus generated, no credit standing. Full obedience discharges the obligation and creates no claim. It is the exact refutation of the older brother's ledger at 15:29.\\f*
\\s1 Ten Lepers, and the One Who Came Back
\\p
\\v 11 And it happened, as he was making his way to Jerusalem, that he was passing between Samaria and Galilee.\\add [i.e. along the border, where the two peoples touch]\\add*
\\v 12 And as he went into a certain village, ten leprous men met him. They stood at a distance\\add [i.e. as the law required of them, calling out from where they were]\\add*
\\v 13 and lifted up their voice: "Jesus, Master, have mercy on us!"
\\v 14 And when he saw them he said to them, "Go, show yourselves to the priests." And it happened, as they went, that they were cleansed.\\add [i.e. sent to be certified clean while still visibly diseased, and cleansed on the road]\\add*
\\p
\\v 15 And one of them, when he saw that he had been healed, came back, glorifying God with a loud voice.
\\v 16 And he fell on his face at his feet, thanking him — and he was a Samaritan.\\add [i.e. and the writer keeps that back until the last word of the sentence]\\add*
\\p
\\v 17 And Jesus answered and said, "Were not the ten cleansed? And the nine — where are they?\\add [i.e. gone on to the priests, doing exactly what they were told]\\add*
\\v 18 Were none found coming back to give glory to God except this foreigner?"\\f + \\fr 17:18 \\ft The word for foreigner is the one carved on the stone barrier in the temple courts, warning that no foreigner might go further in on pain of death. The man who could not enter the sanctuary is the one who came back to give God the glory.\\f*
\\v 19 And he said to him, "Get up and go. Your trust has rescued you."\\add [i.e. all ten were made clean; this one was made whole]\\add*
\\s1 The Kingdom of God Is Already Among You
\\p
\\v 20 And being asked by the Pharisees when the kingdom of God is coming, he answered them and said, "The kingdom of God does not come with watching for it,\\add [i.e. it is not something you spot by keeping an eye out for portents]\\add*
\\v 21 nor will they say, 'Look, here!' or 'There!' For look — the kingdom of God is within you."\\f + \\fr 17:21 \\ft The Greek can mean "within you" or "among you, in your midst." "Within" is taken here. Weigh the other one, though: he is speaking to Pharisees who have opposed him throughout, so a claim about the state of their inner lives would be odd, while "it is standing here among you and you are asking when it will come" fits the scene exactly.\\f*
\\s1 The Day of the Son of Man
\\p
\\v 22 And he said to the disciples, "Days will come when you will long to see one of the days of the son of man, and you will not see it.
\\v 23 And they will say to you, 'Look, there!' or 'Look, here!' Do not go off and do not run after them.
\\v 24 For just as the lightning, when it flashes, shines from one part under heaven to another part under heaven, so will the son of man be in his day.\\add [i.e. nobody has to be told where lightning struck]\\add*
\\v 25 But first he must suffer much and be rejected by this generation.\\add [i.e. the glory is real and it is on the far side of that]\\add*
\\p
\\v 26 And just as it happened in the days of Noah, so it will be in the days of the son of man too:
\\v 27 they were eating, they were drinking, they were marrying, they were being given in marriage, until the day Noah went into the ark, and the flood came and destroyed them all.\\add [i.e. none of these things is wicked — that is the point; life simply went on with nobody looking up]\\add*
\\v 28 It is the same as it happened in the days of Lot: they were eating, drinking, buying, selling, planting, building;
\\v 29 but on the day Lot went out of Sodom, fire and sulfur rained from heaven and destroyed them all.\\add [i.e. planting and building, which are what men do who are certain of next year]\\add*
\\v 30 It will be like that on the day the son of man is unveiled.
\\s1 Remember Lot's Wife
\\p
\\v 31 On that day, whoever is on the housetop and his belongings are in the house, let him not go down to take them away; and likewise the one in the field, let him not turn back to what is behind.
\\v 32 Remember Lot's wife.\\f + \\fr 17:32 \\ft Two words, and they carry the whole argument about possessions that has been running since chapter 12. She was out of the city and walking away when she turned to look back, and it killed her (Genesis 19:26). Her feet had left Sodom; the rest of her had not.\\f*
\\v 33 Whoever tries to keep his life safe will lose it, and whoever loses it will keep it alive.
\\p
\\v 34 I tell you, on that night there will be two on one bed: the one will be taken along and the other left.
\\v 35 There will be two women grinding at the same place: the one will be taken along, and the other left."\\f + \\fr 17:35 \\ft The verse numbering jumps here. A verse 36 — "two men will be in the field; one will be taken and the other left" — appears in many manuscripts, copied in from Matthew 24:40; the oldest Greek of this Gospel does not have it. And weigh which of the pair is the fortunate one. In the two examples just given, what came and "took them all away" was the flood and the fire; the ones left standing were Noah and Lot.\\f*
\\v 37 And they answered and said to him, "Where, Lord?" And he said to them, "Where the body is, there the eagles will gather too."\\f + \\fr 17:37 \\ft The Greek word can mean eagles or vultures; "eagles" is taken here. Either way it is a proverb, and the answer to "where?" is: you will not have to ask. Carrion birds circling tell everyone where the body is.\\f*
\\c 18
\\s1 The Widow and the Judge
\\p
\\v 1 And he told them a parable about their need to pray always and not lose heart,\\add [i.e. told to people facing a long wait for God to act]\\add*
\\v 2 saying, "There was a certain judge in a certain town who did not fear God and had no regard for man.\\f + \\fr 18:2 \\ft The two things named are exactly the two a judge in Israel was required to have. Jehoshaphat charged his judges, "let the fear of the LORD be upon you" (2 Chronicles 19:6-7), and the law forbade partiality (Leviticus 19:15). He is drawn as the worst possible case on purpose.\\f*
\\v 3 And there was a widow in that town, and she kept coming to him and saying, 'Get me justice from my opponent.'\\add [i.e. a woman with no man to plead for her, which is why the law names widows again and again]\\add*
\\v 4 And for a time he was not willing; but afterward he said within himself, 'Even though I do not fear God nor have regard for man,
\\v 5 yet because this widow gives me trouble, I will get her justice, so that she does not wear me out in the end with her coming.'"\\f + \\fr 18:5 \\ft "Wear me out" translates a word from boxing — to give someone a black eye. He is not moved by her case or by pity; he gives in because the pounding will not stop.\\f*
\\p
\\v 6 And the Lord said, "Listen to what the unjust judge says.
\\v 7 And will God not get justice for his chosen ones who cry out to him day and night? And is he slow about them?\\f + \\fr 18:7 \\ft The argument runs from the worse to the better: if persistence gets a result out of that man, consider who you are actually crying out to. The last clause can be read either as the question printed here or as a statement, "though he is patient with them." Either way it names the thing that makes the waiting hard.\\f*
\\v 8 I tell you that he will get justice for them quickly. But when the son of man comes, will he find the trust on the earth?"\\add [i.e. the question is not whether God will act, but whether anyone will still be asking]\\add*
\\s1 The Pharisee and the Tax Collector
\\p
\\v 9 And he also told this parable to some who trusted in themselves that they were righteous and thought nothing of the rest:
\\v 10 "Two men went up into the temple to pray, the one a Pharisee and the other a tax collector.\\add [i.e. one of them belongs there and one of them does not, and everybody listening knows which]\\add*
\\v 11 The Pharisee stood and was praying these things to himself: 'God, I thank you that I am not like the rest of men — robbers, unjust, adulterers — or even like this tax collector.
\\v 12 I fast twice in the week; I tithe everything I take in.'\\f + \\fr 18:12 \\ft Both go beyond what the law requires: it commands one fast a year, on the Day of Atonement, and tithes on produce. He is not lying and he is not boasting emptily. He really does all of it. The prayer thanks God for nothing God has done, asks for nothing, and mentions another man only to stand on him.\\f*
\\p
\\v 13 But the tax collector, standing far off, would not even lift his eyes to heaven, but was beating his breast and saying, 'God, make atonement for me, the sinner.'\\f + \\fr 18:13 \\ft The verb is the one belonging to the mercy seat and the Day of Atonement — the covering of sin by sacrifice (Leviticus 16). He is not asking to be let off; he is asking God to do for him what the whole sacrificial system exists to do. Usual English has "be merciful to me." And it is "the sinner," not "a sinner": he is not comparing.\\f*
\\v 14 I tell you, this man went down to his house set right rather than that one; because everyone who lifts himself up will be brought low, and whoever brings himself low will be lifted up."\\add [i.e. set right — and it was done to him, not by him]\\add*
\\s1 Welcoming the Kingdom Like a Child
\\p
\\v 15 And they were bringing him even the babies, so that he might touch them; but when the disciples saw it, they rebuked them.
\\v 16 But Jesus called them to him and said, "Let the children come to me, and do not stop them, for of such is the kingdom of God.
\\v 17 Amen, I say to you, whoever does not welcome the kingdom of God like a child will never go into it."\\f + \\fr 18:17 \\ft The word in verse 15 is not "children" but infants — babies who cannot speak, cannot fast, cannot tithe, and cannot do anything for themselves. What a baby does with what it is given is receive it. Set beside the two men in the temple, the point is exact.\\f*
\\s1 The Ruler Who Had Too Much
\\p
\\v 18 And a certain ruler asked him, "Good Teacher, what shall I do to inherit the life of the age?"\\add [i.e. what must I achieve in order to receive an inheritance — the question contradicts itself]\\add*
\\v 19 And Jesus said to him, "Why do you call me good? No one is good except one — God.\\add [i.e. do you know what you are saying, or is it a courtesy?]\\add*
\\v 20 You know the commandments: Do not commit adultery, do not murder, do not steal, do not bear false witness, honor your father and mother."\\f + \\fr 18:20 \\ft Only the commands about dealing with other people are listed. The ones about God come first in the Ten, and are left out; so is the last one, do not covet. Both omissions turn out to be where the conversation is going.\\f*
\\v 21 And he said, "All these I have kept from my youth."
\\p
\\v 22 And when Jesus heard it he said to him, "One thing you still lack: sell everything you have and distribute it to the poor, and you will have treasure in the heavens; and come, follow me."
\\v 23 But when he heard these things he became deeply grieved, for he was extremely rich.\\add [i.e. he is not indifferent; he understands exactly, and he cannot do it]\\add*
\\v 24 And Jesus, seeing him, said, "How hard it is for those who have money to go into the kingdom of God!
\\v 25 For it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle than for a rich man to go into the kingdom of God."\\f + \\fr 18:25 \\ft The largest animal anyone in that country handled, and the smallest hole in daily use. There is no small gate in Jerusalem called the Needle's Eye; that story is a medieval invention, and it exists to soften a sentence that was meant to describe something that cannot happen.\\f*
\\p
\\v 26 And those who heard said, "And who can be rescued?"\\f + \\fr 18:26 \\ft The question makes sense because prosperity was widely read as a mark of God's blessing (Deuteronomy 28:1-14). If the people who look blessed cannot get in, the whole reckoning of who is in and who is out has just collapsed.\\f*
\\v 27 But he said, "What is impossible with men is possible with God."\\add [i.e. he does not take back the camel; he says God is not limited by it]\\add*
\\p
\\v 28 And Peter said, "Look — we left what was ours and followed you."\\add [i.e. we did what that man could not]\\add*
\\v 29 And he said to them, "Amen, I say to you, there is no one who has left house or wife or brothers or parents or children for the sake of the kingdom of God
\\v 30 who will not get back many times as much in this time, and in the age that is coming the life of the age."\\add [i.e. the very thing the ruler came asking how to get]\\add*
\\s1 A Third Time: What Will Happen in Jerusalem
\\p
\\v 31 And taking the twelve along, he said to them, "Look — we are going up to Jerusalem, and everything written through the prophets about the son of man will be brought to fulfillment.
\\v 32 For he will be handed over to the nations, and he will be mocked and insulted and spat on;\\f + \\fr 18:32 \\ft Isaiah's servant says it first: "I gave my back to those who strike me, and my cheeks to those who pull out the beard; I did not hide my face from disgrace and spitting" (Isaiah 50:6). "Handed over to the nations" means to the Gentiles — the Romans, who alone could execute.\\f*
\\v 33 and after they have whipped him they will kill him, and on the third day he will rise."
\\v 34 And they understood none of these things, and this word was hidden from them, and they did not know what was being said.\\add [i.e. said three times over now, in plain words, and still shut to them]\\add*
\\s1 A Blind Man Near Jericho
\\p
\\v 35 And it happened, as he drew near to Jericho, that a certain blind man was sitting beside the road, begging.
\\v 36 And hearing a crowd going through, he was asking what this might be.
\\v 37 And they reported to him that Jesus the Nazorean was passing by.
\\p
\\v 38 And he shouted out, "Jesus, son of David, have mercy on me!"\\f + \\fr 18:38 \\ft The crowd told him where the man was from; the blind man answers with a royal title. "Son of David" is the promised king (2 Samuel 7:12-16; Isaiah 11:1-5). The man who cannot see has just said, out loud on a public road, what the disciples have been ordered not to say.\\f*
\\v 39 And those going ahead were rebuking him to make him keep quiet; but he was shouting all the more, "Son of David, have mercy on me!"\\add [i.e. the same rebuke the babies got, and the same widow's refusal to be quiet]\\add*
\\p
\\v 40 And Jesus stopped and ordered him to be brought to him. And when he came near, he asked him,
\\v 41 "What do you want me to do for you?" And he said, "Lord, that I may see again."\\add [i.e. asked not because he does not know, but so the man can say it]\\add*
\\v 42 And Jesus said to him, "See again. Your trust has rescued you."
\\v 43 And at once he saw again, and he followed him, glorifying God. And all the people, when they saw it, gave praise to God.\\add [i.e. told to do nothing, he does what the rich ruler could not — he follows]\\add*
\\c 19
\\s1 Zacchaeus
\\p
\\v 1 And he went in and was passing through Jericho.
\\v 2 And look — a man called by the name of Zacchaeus, and he was a chief tax collector, and he was rich.\\f + \\fr 19:2 \\ft "Chief tax collector" is a title found nowhere else in surviving Greek: he holds the contract for the whole Jericho district and takes a cut from the men under him. Jericho sat on a trade road and grew balsam; the money there was real. And "rich" is the same word used of the ruler who walked away one chapter ago.\\f*
\\v 3 And he was trying to see who Jesus was, and he could not because of the crowd, because he was small in height.
\\v 4 And running on ahead, he climbed up a sycamore-fig tree so as to see him, because he was about to come that way.\\add [i.e. a man of his standing running and climbing, which no man of his standing did]\\add*
\\p
\\v 5 And when he came to the place, Jesus looked up and said to him, "Zacchaeus, hurry and come down, for today I must stay in your house."\\add [i.e. and "I must" is the word this book keeps for what God has appointed]\\add*
\\v 6 And he hurried and came down and welcomed him, rejoicing.
\\v 7 And when they all saw it, they were grumbling and saying, "He has gone in to lodge with a sinful man."\\add [i.e. all of them this time, not only the Pharisees]\\add*
\\p
\\v 8 But Zacchaeus stood and said to the Lord, "Look, Lord — half of my belongings I give to the poor, and if I have squeezed anything out of anyone by false accusation, I pay it back four times over."\\f + \\fr 19:8 \\ft Fourfold is the heaviest restitution in the law, the penalty for stealing and slaughtering a sheep (Exodus 22:1); for ordinary fraud the law asks the principal plus a fifth (Numbers 5:7). He takes the harshest measure available. The Greek verbs are present tense and could describe either what he already does or what he undertakes from this moment; they are rendered here as the plain present.\\f*
\\v 9 And Jesus said to him, "Today rescue came to this house, since he too is a son of Abraham.\\add [i.e. his trade did not put him outside the family; it only made everyone else say so]\\add*
\\v 10 For the son of man came to search out and to rescue what had been lost."\\f + \\fr 19:10 \\ft God's own words through Ezekiel about the flock the shepherds had abandoned: "I will seek the lost" (Ezekiel 34:16) — the text standing behind the lost sheep in chapter 15. The sentence is the nearest thing this book has to a statement of the whole mission, and it comes at the house of a tax collector.\\f*
\\s1 The Nobleman and the Ten Minas
\\p
\\v 11 And while they were listening to these things, he went on and told a parable, because he was near Jerusalem and they thought that the kingdom of God was about to appear at once.\\add [i.e. the parable exists to correct that; there is going to be an interval]\\add*
\\v 12 So he said, "A certain man of high birth went into a distant country to get a kingdom for himself and come back.\\f + \\fr 19:12 \\ft Everyone in Jericho would have recognized the story. When Herod the Great died, his son Archelaus sailed to Rome to have his kingship confirmed by Augustus, and a delegation of fifty Jews followed him there to argue against it. He came back with the territory, and he settled accounts. Archelaus had built the palace at Jericho where this parable is being told.\\f*
\\v 13 And calling ten of his own slaves, he gave them ten minas and said to them, 'Do business until I come.'\\add [i.e. one mina each, about three months' wages — the same stake for every man]\\add*
\\v 14 But his citizens hated him, and they sent a delegation after him to say, 'We do not want this man to reign over us.'
\\p
\\v 15 And it happened, when he came back after getting the kingdom, that he said for these slaves to be called to him, the ones he had given the silver to, so that he might know what business they had done.
\\v 16 And the first came up and said, 'Lord, your mina has made ten minas more.'
\\v 17 And he said to him, 'Well done, good slave! Because you were trustworthy in the very smallest thing, have authority over ten towns.'\\add [i.e. the return on a small sum, paid out in cities]\\add*
\\v 18 And the second came and said, 'Your mina, lord, has made five minas.'
\\v 19 And he said to this one too, 'And you, be over five towns.'
\\p
\\v 20 And the other came and said, 'Lord, look — here is your mina, which I kept laid away in a cloth.\\add [i.e. wrapped up and put away, like something dead]\\add*
\\v 21 For I was afraid of you, because you are a hard man: you take up what you did not lay down, and you reap what you did not sow.'
\\v 22 He said to him, 'Out of your own mouth I will judge you, evil slave. You knew that I am a hard man, taking up what I did not lay down and reaping what I did not sow?
\\v 23 Then why did you not put my silver on the table, and I would have collected it with interest when I came?'\\add [i.e. if that is really what you thought of me, even your own account of me should have made you act]\\add*
\\p
\\v 24 And he said to those standing by, 'Take the mina from him and give it to the one who has the ten minas.'
\\v 25 And they said to him, 'Lord, he has ten minas!'
\\v 26 'I tell you that to everyone who has, it will be given; but from the one who does not have, even what he has will be taken away.
\\v 27 But these enemies of mine, who did not want me to reign over them — bring them here and slaughter them in front of me.'"\\add [i.e. the citizens of verse 14, who are a separate matter from the slaves]\\add*
\\s1 Riding Into Jerusalem
\\p
\\v 28 And having said these things, he went on ahead, going up to Jerusalem.
\\v 29 And it happened, as he drew near to Bethphage and Bethany, at the mountain called Olives, that he sent two of the disciples,\\f + \\fr 19:29 \\ft Zechariah had placed the LORD's own coming on the Mount of Olives: "on that day his feet will stand on the Mount of Olives" (Zechariah 14:4). Everything from here to the temple happens on that ground.\\f*
\\v 30 saying, "Go into the village opposite, and as you go in you will find a colt tied, on which no man has ever sat; untie it and bring it.\\add [i.e. an animal never put to common use, as sacred service required]\\add*
\\v 31 And if anyone asks you, 'Why are you untying it?' you shall say this: 'Its lord has need of it.'"
\\p
\\v 32 And those who had been sent went off and found it just as he had told them.
\\v 33 And as they were untying the colt, its owners said to them, "Why are you untying the colt?"
\\v 34 And they said, "Its lord has need of it."
\\p
\\v 35 And they brought it to Jesus, and throwing their garments on the colt, they set Jesus on it.\\f + \\fr 19:35 \\ft Zechariah 9:9: "Look, your king is coming to you, righteous and bringing salvation, humble and mounted on a donkey, on a colt, the foal of a donkey." A king riding a warhorse is coming to fight; a king on a colt is not. The words are never quoted here, and they do not need to be.\\f*
\\v 36 And as he went, they were spreading out their garments on the road.\\add [i.e. what was done for Jehu when he was made king (2 Kings 9:13)]\\add*
\\p
\\v 37 And as he was already drawing near, at the descent of the Mount of Olives, the whole mass of the disciples began rejoicing to praise God with a loud voice for all the works of power they had seen,
\\v 38 saying, "Blessed is the one who comes, the king, in the name of the Lord! In heaven peace, and glory in the highest places!"\\f + \\fr 19:38 \\ft Psalm 118:26, with one word added: "the king." Note also how the second half echoes what the angels sang at the birth (2:14) and how it differs — they said peace on earth, and the disciples say peace in heaven. On earth, at this moment, what is coming is anything but.\\f*
\\p
\\v 39 And some of the Pharisees from the crowd said to him, "Teacher, rebuke your disciples."
\\v 40 And he answered and said, "I tell you, if these keep quiet, the stones will shout."\\f + \\fr 19:40 \\ft Habakkuk 2:11: "the stone will cry out from the wall." Creation itself gives evidence when human beings will not.\\f*
\\s1 He Weeps Over the City
\\p
\\v 41 And as he drew near, when he saw the city he wept over it,\\add [i.e. audibly, out loud — the only place in this book where he does]\\add*
\\v 42 saying, "If only you had known, you too, on this day, the things that make for peace — but as it is, they were hidden from your eyes.\\add [i.e. the city whose name people heard as "peace"]\\add*
\\v 43 For days will come upon you when your enemies will throw up a barricade against you and surround you and press you in from every side,
\\v 44 and they will dash you and your children within you to the ground, and they will not leave one stone on another in you, because you did not know the time of your visitation."\\f + \\fr 19:44 \\ft A siege wall, encirclement, the city levelled — the standard prophetic vocabulary for what Babylon did once already (Isaiah 29:3; Ezekiel 4:2), and what Rome did in the year 70. "Visitation" is the same word Zechariah used when he blessed God for visiting his people (1:68). The visit came, and the city did not recognize it.\\f*
\\s1 He Clears the Temple
\\p
\\v 45 And he went into the temple and began to throw out those who were selling,
\\v 46 saying to them, "It is written, 'And my house shall be a house of prayer' — but you have made it a cave of robbers."\\f + \\fr 19:46 \\ft Two prophets in one sentence. "My house shall be called a house of prayer" is Isaiah 56:7, and in Isaiah the sentence finishes "for all peoples" — it is a promise about foreigners and outcasts being let in. "A den of robbers" is Jeremiah 7:11, from the sermon Jeremiah preached in the temple gate against people who committed injustice and then sheltered in the building; God's answer there was to do to that house what he had already done to Shiloh, and destroy it.\\f*
\\p
\\v 47 And he was teaching daily in the temple. But the chief priests and the scribes and the leading men of the people were seeking to destroy him,
\\v 48 and they could not find what they might do, for all the people were hanging on him, listening.\\add [i.e. the crowd is the only thing keeping him alive, and both sides know it]\\add*
\\c 20
\\s1 By What Authority?
\\p
\\v 1 And it happened on one of the days, while he was teaching the people in the temple and announcing the good news, that the chief priests and the scribes came up with the elders,\\add [i.e. all three groups of the ruling council, arriving as a delegation]\\add*
\\v 2 and they said to him, "Tell us, by what authority do you do these things, or who is it that gave you this authority?"
\\p
\\v 3 And he answered and said to them, "I too will ask you a word; and you tell me:
\\v 4 the immersion of John — was it from heaven or from men?"\\add [i.e. answer that, and you have answered your own question]\\add*
\\v 5 And they reasoned it out among themselves: "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will say, 'Why did you not trust him?'
\\v 6 But if we say, 'From men,' all the people will stone us, for they are convinced that John was a prophet."
\\v 7 And they answered that they did not know where it was from.\\add [i.e. the men whose office is to judge such questions, declaring that they cannot]\\add*
\\v 8 And Jesus said to them, "Neither do I tell you by what authority I do these things."
\\s1 The Vineyard and the Tenants
\\p
\\v 9 And he began to tell the people this parable: "A man planted a vineyard and leased it out to vinedressers, and he went abroad for a long time.\\f + \\fr 20:9 \\ft Isaiah's song of the vineyard, which everyone listening knew, ends by saying plainly what the vineyard is: "the vineyard of the LORD of hosts is the house of Israel" (Isaiah 5:1-7). The tenants are a new element. The story is being told in the temple, to the men who run it.\\f*
\\v 10 And in the season he sent a slave to the vinedressers, so that they would give him some of the fruit of the vineyard; but the vinedressers beat him and sent him away empty.
\\v 11 And he went on to send another slave; and that one they beat and dishonored and sent away empty.
\\v 12 And he went on to send a third; and this one too they wounded and threw out.\\add [i.e. one after another, worse each time — which is what the prophets got]\\add*
\\p
\\v 13 And the lord of the vineyard said, 'What shall I do? I will send my beloved son; perhaps they will be turned around by him.'\\add [i.e. "my beloved son" — the words the voice from heaven used]\\add*
\\v 14 But when the vinedressers saw him, they reasoned with one another and said, 'This is the heir. Let us kill him, so that the inheritance may become ours.'
\\v 15 And throwing him out of the vineyard, they killed him. What then will the lord of the vineyard do to them?
\\v 16 He will come and destroy those vinedressers and give the vineyard to others." And when they heard it they said, "May it never be!"\\add [i.e. they have understood it exactly]\\add*
\\p
\\v 17 But he looked at them and said, "What then is this that is written: 'The stone the builders rejected — this one became the head of the corner'?\\f + \\fr 20:17 \\ft Psalm 118:22 — from the same psalm the crowd sang at his entry a few days earlier (19:38). The men whose trade is building are the ones who threw the stone out, and it ended up carrying the whole structure.\\f*
\\v 18 Everyone who falls on that stone will be broken to pieces; and whoever it falls on, it will grind him to dust."\\f + \\fr 20:18 \\ft Two more stones welded on. Isaiah's, which the LORD himself becomes, "a stone of stumbling and a rock of offense," over which many fall and are broken (Isaiah 8:14-15); and Daniel's, cut without hands, which smashes the statue of the empires to powder (Daniel 2:34-35, 44-45).\\f*
\\v 19 And the scribes and the chief priests tried to lay hands on him in that very hour, and they were afraid of the people; for they knew that he had told this parable against them.\\add [i.e. and by reaching for him they do the very thing the parable describes]\\add*
\\s1 Give Back to Caesar What Is Caesar's
\\p
\\v 20 And watching him closely, they sent in men planted to watch, who pretended to be righteous, so that they might take hold of his word, in order to hand him over to the rule and the authority of the governor.\\add [i.e. the charge has to be political, because only Rome can execute]\\add*
\\v 21 And they questioned him: "Teacher, we know that you speak and teach rightly, and you do not take a face, but you teach the way of God in truth.\\add [i.e. flattery laid down first, so that he cannot decline to answer]\\add*
\\v 22 Is it permitted for us to give tax to Caesar, or not?"\\f + \\fr 20:22 \\ft The poll tax, paid by every subject as the visible token of Roman ownership. Refusing it had already caused an armed revolt in Galilee a generation earlier, on the argument that paying it acknowledged a lord besides God. Say yes and the crowd turns; say no and the men in front of him have their capital charge.\\f*
\\p
\\v 23 But he saw through their trickery and said to them,
\\v 24 "Show me a denarius. Whose image and inscription does it have?" And they said, "Caesar's."\\f + \\fr 20:24 \\ft The coin carried the emperor's head and an inscription naming him son of the deified Augustus. Asking them to produce one makes the point before he says a word: they are carrying it.\\f*
\\v 25 And he said to them, "Then give back to Caesar the things of Caesar, and to God the things of God."\\f + \\fr 20:25 \\ft The verb means to give back what belongs to someone. The coin carries Caesar's image, so let Caesar have it. Then ask what carries God's image: "God created man in his own image" (Genesis 1:27). The second half of the sentence is not the smaller half. It swallows the first.\\f*
\\v 26 And they were not able to take hold of his word in front of the people, and they marveled at his answer and were silent.
\\s1 The Sadducees and the Resurrection
\\p
\\v 27 And some of the Sadducees came up — those who say there is no resurrection — and they questioned him,\\f + \\fr 20:27 \\ft The Sadducees were the priestly aristocracy, and they held that only the five books of Moses carry full authority. Since they found no resurrection taught there, they denied it. The argument that follows is therefore fought entirely on their ground.\\f*
\\v 28 saying, "Teacher, Moses wrote for us that if someone's brother dies having a wife, and this man is childless, his brother shall take the wife and raise up seed for his brother.\\f + \\fr 20:28 \\ft Deuteronomy 25:5-6. The law exists to keep a dead man's name and land in Israel — which is to say, it is a provision made necessary by death.\\f*
\\p
\\v 29 Now there were seven brothers; and the first took a wife and died childless.
\\v 30 And the second,\\f + \\fr 20:30 \\ft The oldest Greek has only "And the second." Later manuscripts fill the line out to match the pattern: "took her as wife, and he died childless."\\f*
\\v 31 and the third took her, and in the same way the seven left no children and died.
\\v 32 Afterward the woman died too.
\\v 33 So in the resurrection, whose wife does the woman become? For the seven had her as wife."\\add [i.e. a case built to make the whole idea look ridiculous]\\add*
\\p
\\v 34 And Jesus said to them, "The sons of this age marry and are given in marriage,
\\v 35 but those counted worthy to reach that age and the resurrection out of the dead neither marry nor are given in marriage.
\\v 36 For they can no longer die, because they are like angels; and they are sons of God, being sons of the resurrection.\\add [i.e. marriage answers death by replacing the dead; where nobody dies, the answer is not needed]\\add*
\\v 37 But that the dead are raised, even Moses showed at the bush, when he calls the Lord 'the God of Abraham and the God of Isaac and the God of Jacob.'\\f + \\fr 20:37 \\ft Exodus 3:6 — inside the five books, the only authority they accept. God introduces himself to Moses by his relation to three men centuries dead. A God who names himself by a bond with people who have simply ceased to exist is naming himself by nothing.\\f*
\\v 38 And he is not God of the dead but of the living, for to him all are alive."
\\p
\\v 39 And some of the scribes answered and said, "Teacher, you spoke well."
\\v 40 For they no longer dared to ask him anything.\\add [i.e. three challenges, three answers, and the questioning stops]\\add*
\\s1 David Calls Him Lord
\\p
\\v 41 And he said to them, "How can they say that the Christ is David's son?\\add [i.e. now he asks them one]\\add*
\\v 42 For David himself says in the book of Psalms: 'The Lord said to my lord, Sit at my right hand
\\v 43 until I make your enemies a footstool for your feet.'
\\v 44 So David calls him lord — and how is he his son?"\\f + \\fr 20:44 \\ft Psalm 110:1, taken as David's own. He is not denying descent from David; the blind man at Jericho called him son of David and was not corrected. He is showing that the title is too small. In that world a father is always greater than his son, and no father calls his descendant "my lord." He leaves the question standing. The reader has already been told the answer at 1:32-35.\\f*
\\s1 Watch Out for the Scribes
\\p
\\v 45 And while all the people were listening, he said to the disciples,
\\v 46 "Watch out for the scribes, who want to walk around in long robes and love greetings in the marketplaces and the front seats in the synagogues and the first places at dinners,
\\v 47 who eat up widows' houses and for a show make long prayers. These will receive a heavier judgment."\\f + \\fr 20:47 \\ft "Eat up" is a predator's word. How it was done is not spelled out — scribes acted as trustees and estate managers, and lived on the support of the devout — but the charge is that the men whose whole expertise is a law commanding the protection of widows (Exodus 22:22; Deuteronomy 24:17) are consuming them, and that the long prayers are the instrument, not an unrelated fault. Read straight on into the first four verses of the next chapter.\\f*
\\c 21
\\s1 A Widow's Two Coins
\\p
\\v 1 And looking up, he saw the rich throwing their gifts into the treasury.
\\v 2 And he saw a certain needy widow throwing in there two small copper coins,\\f + \\fr 21:2 \\ft The smallest coin in circulation; two of them together came to about a hundredth of a day's wage. Read this straight on from the sentence before it — he has just said that the scribes eat up widows' houses (20:47), and here is a widow at the treasury of the institution those scribes run, putting in the last of what she has.\\f*
\\v 3 and he said, "Truly I tell you that this poor widow threw in more than all of them.
\\v 4 For all these threw in gifts out of what they had over; but she out of her lack threw in the whole of the living she had."\\add [i.e. everything she had to live on, given to the house that is about to be pulled down]\\add*
\\s1 Not One Stone Left on Another
\\p
\\v 5 And while some were speaking about the temple, that it was decorated with fine stones and offerings, he said,\\add [i.e. Herod's masonry, and centuries of gifts hung up in it]\\add*
\\v 6 "These things you are looking at — days will come in which not one stone will be left on another that will not be thrown down."\\f + \\fr 21:6 \\ft It had happened before, and the prophets had said it would happen again: God let Shiloh be destroyed, and warned Jerusalem he would do the same to the house they were sheltering in (Jeremiah 7:12-14); Micah said Zion would be plowed like a field (Micah 3:12). The Romans levelled the temple in the year 70.\\f*
\\s1 What Is Not the Sign
\\p
\\v 7 And they questioned him: "Teacher, when then will these things be, and what is the sign when these things are about to happen?"\\add [i.e. one question, about one event — the temple coming down]\\add*
\\v 8 And he said, "Watch out that you are not led astray; for many will come in my name saying, 'I am he,' and, 'The time has come near.' Do not go after them.\\f + \\fr 21:8 \\ft The decades before the war produced a string of such figures; Josephus names several who gathered followers on a promise of deliverance and got them killed.\\f*
\\v 9 And when you hear of wars and unrest, do not be terrified; for these things must happen first, but the end does not come at once."\\add [i.e. none of that is the sign you asked for]\\add*
\\p
\\v 10 Then he said to them, "Nation will be raised against nation and kingdom against kingdom,
\\v 11 and there will be great earthquakes, and in place after place famines and plagues, and there will be terrors and great signs from heaven.\\f + \\fr 21:11 \\ft The standard prophetic list for a season of divine visitation (Isaiah 19:2; Amos 4:6-11). Josephus records portents circulating in Jerusalem before the war — a comet standing over the city, a light around the altar at night, the eastern gate opening by itself.\\f*
\\s1 Before All This
\\p
\\v 12 But before all these things they will lay their hands on you and persecute you, handing you over to the synagogues and prisons, dragging you before kings and governors on account of my name.\\add [i.e. before, not among — your own trouble comes first, and it is not the sign either]\\add*
\\v 13 It will turn out for you as an opening to bear witness.\\add [i.e. the dock becomes the platform]\\add*
\\v 14 So settle it in your hearts not to prepare beforehand how to defend yourselves,
\\v 15 for I will give you a mouth and a wisdom that none of those set against you will be able to stand up to or contradict.\\f + \\fr 21:15 \\ft Where the parallel in Mark 13:11 credits the Holy Spirit, this one has Jesus himself supplying it. And note what is promised: not acquittal, but an answer they cannot meet. Stephen's opponents "could not stand up against the wisdom and the Spirit with which he spoke" (Acts 6:10), and they stoned him.\\f*
\\p
\\v 16 And you will be handed over even by parents and brothers and relatives and friends, and they will put some of you to death,
\\v 17 and you will be hated by everyone on account of my name.
\\v 18 And a hair of your head will never be destroyed.\\add [i.e. said in the same breath as "they will put some of you to death" — so it is not the body he means]\\add*
\\v 19 By your staying power you will gain your lives.\\f + \\fr 21:19 \\ft The oldest Greek has the future, "you will gain"; the later manuscripts have a command, "possess your souls." Either way the condition named is endurance. Not the moment of setting out, but the holding on — the same thing the good soil was marked by (8:15).\\f*
\\s1 When You See Jerusalem Surrounded
\\p
\\v 20 But when you see Jerusalem surrounded by camped armies, then know that her desolation has come near.\\f + \\fr 21:20 \\ft This is the answer to the question in verse 7. Where Mark's account has the veiled phrase from Daniel about the abomination of desolation, with a note telling the reader to work it out, this one simply says: armies around the city. There is nothing to decode.\\f*
\\v 21 Then let those in Judea run to the mountains, and let those inside her get out, and let those out in the countryside not go into her,\\add [i.e. the exact reverse of every instinct — the walled city is the trap, not the refuge]\\add*
\\v 22 because these are days of vengeance, for everything written to be filled full.\\f + \\fr 21:22 \\ft "Days of vengeance" is prophetic language for the settling of a long account (Hosea 9:7; Isaiah 63:4). And what is being filled full is the whole warning written into the covenant from the beginning — Deuteronomy 28:49-57 describes in detail a nation coming from far off, besieging the cities, and the horrors inside the walls.\\f*
\\v 23 Woe to those who are pregnant and those nursing in those days! For there will be great distress on the land and wrath against this people.\\add [i.e. woe to them because they are the ones who cannot run]\\add*
\\v 24 And they will fall by the edge of the sword and will be led away captive into all the nations, and Jerusalem will be trampled by the nations until the times of the nations are filled full.\\f + \\fr 21:24 \\ft The shape of the Babylonian exile all over again: the sword, the captivity, foreign occupation — and a term set to it. "Until" is the load-bearing word. Daniel had seen a sequence of Gentile empires holding power for an allotted span before God's own kingdom took it (Daniel 2:44; 7:27), and "the times of the nations" appears to be that.\\f*
\\s1 The Son of Man Coming in a Cloud
\\p
\\v 25 And there will be signs in sun and moon and stars, and on the earth distress of nations at a loss before the roaring and surging of the sea,\\f + \\fr 21:25 \\ft Sun, moon and stars going dark is how the prophets announce the fall of a great power — of Babylon (Isaiah 13:10), of Edom (Isaiah 34:4), of Egypt (Ezekiel 32:7-8). None of those passages is about the end of the physical universe; the language says that an order which looked as fixed as the sky is coming down. The roaring sea is the nations in turmoil (Isaiah 17:12-13; Psalm 46:3).\\f*
\\v 26 men fainting from fear and from what they see coming on the inhabited world; for the powers of the heavens will be shaken.
\\v 27 And then they will see the son of man coming in a cloud with power and great glory.\\f + \\fr 21:27 \\ft Daniel 7:13-14, and the direction of travel there is worth noticing: the one like a son of man comes with the clouds to the Ancient of Days and is given dominion, glory and a kingdom. It is an enthronement scene. The events that tear down the temple are what will show that authority has been handed to the man its rulers condemned.\\f*
\\v 28 But when these things begin to happen, straighten up and lift up your heads, because your ransoming is drawing near."\\add [i.e. while the nations are fainting, you stand up — the same events, and two opposite meanings]\\add*
\\s1 The Fig Tree
\\p
\\v 29 And he told them a parable: "Look at the fig tree and all the trees.
\\v 30 When they put out their leaves, you see it and know for yourselves that the summer is already near.
\\v 31 So also you, when you see these things happening, know that the kingdom of God is near.\\add [i.e. no special knowledge required — anyone can read a tree]\\add*
\\v 32 Amen, I say to you that this generation will not pass away until it all happens.\\f + \\fr 21:32 \\ft "This generation" is used eight times in this book, and every other time it plainly means the people alive while he is speaking (7:31; 9:41; 11:29-32, 50-51; 17:25). The temple came down some forty years later.\\f*
\\v 33 Heaven and earth will pass away, but my words will never pass away.
\\s1 Stay Awake
\\p
\\v 34 But watch yourselves, so that your hearts are not weighed down with hangover and drunkenness and the worries of daily life, and that day come upon you suddenly\\add [i.e. two ways of not being awake — numbing yourself, and being eaten by ordinary anxieties]\\add*
\\v 35 like a trap; for it will come in on all those sitting on the face of the whole earth.\\f + \\fr 21:35 \\ft Isaiah 24:17 speaks of terror and pit and snare coming on the inhabitants of the earth. A bird never sees the snare until it has already closed.\\f*
\\v 36 But stay awake at every moment, praying that you may have the strength to escape all these things that are about to happen, and to stand in front of the son of man."\\add [i.e. to stand — not to cower, and not to be found flat]\\add*
\\p
\\v 37 And by day he was teaching in the temple, and at night he would go out and lodge on the mountain called Olives.\\f + \\fr 21:37 \\ft The pattern matters twice over. It is the fixed routine that will let Judas find him away from the crowd. And Ezekiel watched the glory of God leave the doomed temple and come to rest on that same mountain east of the city (Ezekiel 11:23).\\f*
\\v 38 And all the people would come to him early in the morning in the temple to hear him.
\\c 22
\\s1 Satan Goes Into Judas
\\p
\\v 1 Now the feast of unleavened bread, called Passover, was drawing near.\\f + \\fr 22:1 \\ft Strictly, Passover was the meal on one evening and Unleavened Bread the seven days following (Leviticus 23:5-6), but by this period the names were used for the whole festival. Everything in this chapter takes its shape from what Passover remembers: a lamb killed, blood that turns away death, and a people brought out of slavery.\\f*
\\v 2 And the chief priests and the scribes were seeking how they might do away with him, for they were afraid of the people.
\\p
\\v 3 And Satan went into Judas, the one called Iscariot, who was of the number of the twelve.\\f + \\fr 22:3 \\ft At 4:13, after the testing in the wilderness, the devil "stood off from him until a fitting time." That sentence has been left hanging over the whole book, and this is where it is picked up. The time has come, and the door he comes back through is one of the twelve.\\f*
\\v 4 And he went off and talked with the chief priests and officers about how he might hand him over to them.\\add [i.e. the temple police commanders]\\add*
\\v 5 And they were glad, and they agreed to give him silver.
\\v 6 And he consented, and he began looking for a good moment to hand him over to them away from a crowd.\\add [i.e. which is precisely what they had not been able to arrange]\\add*
\\s1 Preparing the Passover
\\p
\\v 7 And the day of unleavened bread came, on which the Passover had to be sacrificed.\\add [i.e. "had to" — the same word this book uses for what God has appointed]\\add*
\\v 8 And he sent Peter and John, saying, "Go and prepare the Passover for us, so that we may eat it."
\\v 9 And they said to him, "Where do you want us to prepare it?"
\\p
\\v 10 And he said to them, "Look — when you have gone into the city, a man carrying a jar of water will meet you. Follow him into the house he goes into,\\add [i.e. a man carrying water, which was women's work and so unmistakable in a crowd]\\add*
\\v 11 and you shall say to the master of the house, 'The Teacher says to you: Where is the guest room where I may eat the Passover with my disciples?'\\add [i.e. the same word used at the birth, where there was no room in it]\\add*
\\v 12 And he will show you a large upstairs room, furnished. Prepare it there."
\\v 13 And they went off and found it just as he had told them, and they prepared the Passover.
\\s1 The Last Passover, and the New Covenant
\\p
\\v 14 And when the hour came, he reclined, and the apostles with him.\\f + \\fr 22:14 \\ft Most translations read "the twelve apostles"; the oldest Greek has only "the apostles."\\f*
\\v 15 And he said to them, "With longing I have longed to eat this Passover with you before I suffer.\\add [i.e. a Hebrew way of doubling the verb for weight — I have wanted this badly]\\add*
\\v 16 For I tell you that I will not eat it until it is filled full in the kingdom of God."
\\p
\\v 17 And taking a cup, he gave thanks and said, "Take this and divide it among yourselves.
\\v 18 For I tell you that from now on I will not drink from the fruit of the vine until the kingdom of God comes."\\add [i.e. a vow, and the last thing he will drink is the sour wine at the cross]\\add*
\\p
\\v 19 And taking bread, he gave thanks and broke it and gave it to them, saying, "This is my body given for you. Do this for the remembering of me."\\f + \\fr 22:19 \\ft The four movements — took, gave thanks, broke, gave — are the ordinary blessing at a Jewish table, and the same four are used at the feeding of the five thousand (9:16) and at Emmaus (24:30). What is not ordinary is the object of the remembering. Passover was itself a commanded memorial (Exodus 12:14): Israel remembered the going out from Egypt. He redirects it to himself. A few manuscripts, and one or two English versions in the margin, stop the supper at "This is my body," omitting the rest of this verse and the next; the oldest and best copies have the whole of it.\\f*
\\v 20 And the cup in the same way after the meal, saying, "This cup is the new covenant in my blood, which is poured out for you.\\f + \\fr 22:20 \\ft Two texts stand behind this. At Sinai, Moses threw blood over the people and said, "Behold the blood of the covenant that the LORD has made with you" (Exodus 24:8). And Jeremiah promised a new covenant, made necessary because the first was broken, in which the law would be written on the heart and sins remembered no more (Jeremiah 31:31-34). He is saying that the promised one is being cut, tonight, and this is the blood of it.\\f*
\\p
\\v 21 But look — the hand of the one handing me over is with me on the table.\\f + \\fr 22:21 \\ft "Even my close friend, whom I trusted, who ate my bread, has lifted his heel against me" (Psalm 41:9). At a Passover table, sharing the dish is the sign of being one household.\\f*
\\v 22 For the son of man goes according to what has been marked out; but woe to that man through whom he is handed over."\\add [i.e. both at once — the road is fixed, and the man who walks him onto it answers for it]\\add*
\\v 23 And they began to argue among themselves which of them it might be who was about to do this.
\\s1 Greatness Is Serving
\\p
\\v 24 And a rivalry also came in among them, as to which of them seemed to be greatest.\\add [i.e. from "which of us would betray him" straight to "which of us is the greatest"]\\add*
\\v 25 But he said to them, "The kings of the nations lord it over them, and those who have authority over them are called benefactors.\\f + \\fr 22:25 \\ft "Benefactor" was a formal royal title — two of the Ptolemies of Egypt carried it officially — and the whole system of public giving ran on it: a man gave in order to be honored, and the honor was the power. Both halves of the sentence describe the same machine.\\f*
\\v 26 But you are not to be so; rather, let the greatest among you become like the youngest, and the one who leads like the one who serves.
\\v 27 For who is greater, the one reclining at table or the one serving? Is it not the one reclining? But I am in your midst as the one serving.\\add [i.e. and he has just proved it — the meal they are eating is his body handed round]\\add*
\\p
\\v 28 And you are the ones who have stayed with me through my trials.
\\v 29 And I make a covenant with you, just as my Father made a covenant with me, for a kingdom,\\f + \\fr 22:29 \\ft The verb is built on the same root as the word "covenant" used of the cup a few lines earlier. The kingdom is not simply handed over; it is covenanted — from the Father to the Son, and from the Son to these men, in that order, at this table.\\f*
\\v 30 so that you may eat and drink at my table in my kingdom, and sit on thrones judging the twelve tribes of Israel.\\add [i.e. twelve men on twelve thrones over twelve tribes: Israel put back together]\\add*
\\s1 Simon, Satan Asked to Have You All
\\p
\\v 31 Simon, Simon, look — Satan asked to have you all, to sift you like wheat.\\f + \\fr 22:31 \\ft "You" here is plural: the demand covers the whole group. The request for permission is the scene in Job, where the accuser has to ask before he can touch anything (Job 1:6-12). Sifting shakes grain hard in a sieve to find out what is wheat and what is chaff. Many manuscripts open the verse with "And the Lord said"; the oldest Greek does not.\\f*
\\v 32 But I begged for you, that your trust may not give out; and you, when you have turned back, put strength into your brothers."\\add [i.e. singular now, to Simon alone — and "when you have turned back" takes the falling for granted]\\add*
\\v 33 And he said to him, "Lord, I am ready to go with you both to prison and to death."
\\v 34 And he said, "I tell you, Peter, a rooster will not crow today until you have three times denied that you know me."\\add [i.e. he offers to die, and is told he will not manage to admit acquaintance]\\add*
\\s1 But Now
\\p
\\v 35 And he said to them, "When I sent you out with no purse and no bag and no sandals, did you lack anything?" And they said, "Nothing."
\\v 36 And he said to them, "But now, the one who has a purse, let him take it, and a bag as well; and the one who has none, let him sell his coat and buy a sword.\\add [i.e. the season of being welcomed and provided for is over]\\add*
\\v 37 For I tell you that this that is written must be brought to its end in me: 'And he was counted with the lawless.' For indeed what concerns me has an end."\\f + \\fr 22:37 \\ft Isaiah 53:12, the only place in this book where the servant song is quoted outright — and he applies it to himself. The line about the swords is best read through it: a teacher taken at night surrounded by armed men will be counted with the outlaws. When one of them actually uses a sword a few hours later, he stops it and undoes the damage (verses 50-51).\\f*
\\v 38 And they said, "Lord, look — here are two swords." And he said to them, "It is enough."\\add [i.e. two swords against the temple guard — he is ending the conversation, not arming a rising]\\add*
\\s1 On the Mount of Olives
\\p
\\v 39 And he went out and made his way, as was his custom, to the Mount of Olives; and the disciples also followed him.\\add [i.e. the nightly habit of 21:37, which is how he can be found]\\add*
\\v 40 And when he came to the place, he said to them, "Pray not to come into testing."\\add [i.e. the very petition he taught them at 11:4]\\add*
\\p
\\v 41 And he pulled away from them about a stone's throw, and kneeling down he was praying,
\\v 42 saying, "Father, if you are willing, take this cup away from me — but let not my will, but yours, be done."\\f + \\fr 22:42 \\ft The cup in the prophets is the cup of God's wrath, handed to a people to drink (Isaiah 51:17, 22; Jeremiah 25:15-17; Ezekiel 23:31-34). And the prayer is the one he taught: "let your will be done." He asks for the cup to pass — that is a real will, wanting a real thing — and then puts it under the Father's.\\f*
\\v 43 And an angel from heaven appeared to him, putting strength into him.\\f + \\fr 22:43 \\ft The oldest editions of the Greek print this verse and the next inside double brackets, marking them as words the editors judge to have been added later than the rest but which they will not remove. They are printed here as text, with nothing to show the brackets. An angel fed and strengthened Elijah in a comparable hour (1 Kings 19:5-8).\\f*
\\v 44 And being in agony he was praying more earnestly; and his sweat became like drops of blood falling down on the ground.\\add [i.e. like drops of blood — the sweat running that heavily]\\add*
\\p
\\v 45 And getting up from the prayer, he came to the disciples and found them sleeping from grief.\\add [i.e. grief, not indifference — and asleep all the same, at the one hour he told them to pray]\\add*
\\v 46 And he said to them, "Why are you sleeping? Get up and pray, so that you do not come into testing."
\\s1 The Arrest
\\p
\\v 47 While he was still speaking — look, a crowd, and the one called Judas, one of the twelve, was going ahead of them; and he drew near to Jesus to kiss him.
\\v 48 But Jesus said to him, "Judas, with a kiss you hand over the son of man?"\\add [i.e. the greeting a disciple gave his teacher, used as the signal]\\add*
\\p
\\v 49 And when those around him saw what was going to happen, they said, "Lord, shall we strike with a sword?"
\\v 50 And a certain one of them struck the slave of the high priest and took off his right ear.\\add [i.e. and did not wait for the answer]\\add*
\\v 51 But Jesus answered and said, "Let it go this far." And touching the ear, he healed him.\\add [i.e. the last healing in the book, done to an enemy's servant, in the middle of his own arrest]\\add*
\\p
\\v 52 And Jesus said to the chief priests and officers of the temple and elders who had come out against him, "Have you come out with swords and clubs as against a robber?
\\v 53 Day after day I was with you in the temple, and you did not stretch out your hands against me. But this is your hour, and the authority of darkness."\\add [i.e. the hour that has been waiting since the wilderness]\\add*
\\s1 Peter Denies Him
\\p
\\v 54 And seizing him, they led him away and brought him into the house of the high priest; and Peter was following at a distance.\\add [i.e. still following, which is the discipleship word — but at a distance]\\add*
\\v 55 And when they had lit a fire in the middle of the courtyard and sat down together, Peter was sitting in the middle of them.\\add [i.e. in the firelight, where his face can be seen]\\add*
\\p
\\v 56 And a certain servant girl, seeing him sitting in the firelight and looking hard at him, said, "This man was with him too."
\\v 57 But he denied it: "I do not know him, woman."
\\p
\\v 58 And a little later someone else saw him and said, "You too are one of them." But Peter said, "Man, I am not."\\add [i.e. first he does not know the man, now he is not one of the group]\\add*
\\v 59 And after about an hour had passed, someone else was insisting, "In truth this man was with him too, for he is a Galilean."\\add [i.e. his accent gives him away]\\add*
\\p
\\v 60 But Peter said, "Man, I do not know what you are talking about." And at once, while he was still speaking, a rooster crowed.
\\v 61 And the Lord turned and looked at Peter, and Peter remembered the word of the Lord, how he had said to him, "Before a rooster crows today, you will deny me three times."\\add [i.e. close enough to be seen, and not a word spoken]\\add*
\\v 62 And going outside, he wept bitterly.\\add [i.e. and this is the beginning of the turning back he was promised]\\add*
\\s1 Mocked Through the Night
\\p
\\v 63 And the men who were holding him were mocking him and beating him.
\\v 64 And covering his face, they were questioning him: "Prophesy! Who is it that struck you?"\\f + \\fr 22:64 \\ft Many manuscripts add "they struck him on the face" before the question; the oldest Greek has only the covering and the demand. The irony is heavy: within the last few hours he has foretold the betrayal, the denial, and the crowing of the rooster, and every one of them has come true while these men were watching.\\f*
\\v 65 And they were saying many other things against him, blaspheming.
\\s1 Before the Council
\\p
\\v 66 And when day came, the council of elders of the people came together, both chief priests and scribes, and they led him away into their Sanhedrin,\\add [i.e. the supreme council, meeting formally now that it is light]\\add*
\\v 67 saying, "If you are the Christ, tell us." And he said to them, "If I tell you, you will never trust;
\\v 68 and if I ask, you will never answer.\\f + \\fr 22:68 \\ft Many manuscripts add "nor let me go" at the end; the oldest Greek does not. He is describing what has already happened: at 20:1-8 they asked him about his authority, he asked them one question in return, and they refused to answer it.\\f*
\\p
\\v 69 But from now on the son of man will be seated at the right hand of the power of God."\\f + \\fr 22:69 \\ft Daniel 7:13-14 and Psalm 110:1 in one sentence — the son of man who receives dominion, and the one invited to sit at the LORD's right hand. "The power of God" is a reverent way of saying God without saying the name. And the timing is the shock: from now on, said by a prisoner in the dock.\\f*
\\v 70 And they all said, "Are you then the Son of God?" And he said to them, "You say that I am."\\add [i.e. you have said it — and the word from heaven at the river said it first]\\add*
\\v 71 And they said, "Why do we still have need of testimony? For we ourselves heard it from his own mouth."\\add [i.e. exactly what he told them in verse 67 would happen]\\add*
\\c 23
\\s1 Before Pilate
\\p
\\v 1 And the whole mass of them got up and brought him to Pilate.\\f + \\fr 23:1 \\ft The council could pass a verdict but not carry out an execution; only the Roman prefect held that power. Pontius Pilate governed Judea from about AD 26 to 36, and came up from Caesarea to Jerusalem for the festivals, when the crowds made trouble likeliest.\\f*
\\v 2 And they began to accuse him: "We found this man turning our nation aside, and forbidding the giving of taxes to Caesar, and saying that he himself is Christ, a king."\\f + \\fr 23:2 \\ft The religious verdict has been translated into three political charges, because those are the only kind Rome will hear. The second is a flat lie, and the reader has watched it being manufactured: the men who sent the spies about the tax got the answer "give back to Caesar the things of Caesar" (20:20-25). The third is true, though not in the sense being urged.\\f*
\\v 3 And Pilate asked him, "You are the king of the Jews?" And he answered him and said, "You say it."
\\v 4 And Pilate said to the chief priests and the crowds, "I find nothing to accuse this man of."\\add [i.e. the first of three times he will say it]\\add*
\\v 5 But they were insisting, "He stirs up the people, teaching through the whole of Judea, and beginning from Galilee as far as here."
\\s1 Before Herod
\\p
\\v 6 When Pilate heard this, he asked whether the man was a Galilean.
\\v 7 And learning that he was out of Herod's jurisdiction, he sent him up to Herod, who was himself in Jerusalem in those days.\\add [i.e. a chance to hand the case to somebody else]\\add*
\\p
\\v 8 And when Herod saw Jesus he was very glad, for he had been wanting to see him for a long time, because he had heard about him, and he was hoping to see some sign done by him.\\add [i.e. the wish first recorded at 9:9, and what he wants is a performance]\\add*
\\v 9 And he questioned him at some length, but he answered him nothing.\\f + \\fr 23:9 \\ft He answered the council and he answered Pilate. To the man who killed John and wants a trick, he says nothing at all: "he was oppressed and afflicted, yet he did not open his mouth; like a lamb led to the slaughter... he did not open his mouth" (Isaiah 53:7).\\f*
\\v 10 And the chief priests and the scribes stood there, accusing him fiercely.
\\v 11 And Herod with his soldiers treated him as nothing and mocked him, dressing him in bright clothing, and sent him back to Pilate.\\add [i.e. gorgeous clothes, as a joke about a king]\\add*
\\v 12 And Herod and Pilate became friends with one another on that very day, for before that they had been at enmity with each other.\\f + \\fr 23:12 \\ft "The kings of the earth take their stand and the rulers take counsel together against the LORD and against his anointed" (Psalm 2:1-2). The apostles later quote that psalm of exactly this — Herod and Pilate, Gentiles and Israel, gathered against him (Acts 4:25-28). Two enemies are reconciled over the body of an innocent man.\\f*
\\s1 Pilate Gives In
\\p
\\v 13 And Pilate called together the chief priests and the rulers and the people
\\v 14 and said to them, "You brought me this man as one turning the people aside, and look — I examined him in front of you and found in this man nothing to accuse him of, none of the things you charge against him.\\add [i.e. the second time]\\add*
\\v 15 Nor did Herod, for he sent him back to us; and look — nothing deserving death has been done by him.\\f + \\fr 23:15 \\ft The oldest Greek reads "for he sent him back to us"; the later manuscripts have "for I sent you to him."\\f*
\\v 16 So I will punish him and release him."\\f + \\fr 23:16 \\ft The verse numbering jumps here. A verse 17 — "he had of necessity to release one to them at the feast" — stands in many manuscripts, supplied from Matthew and Mark to explain the crowd's demand; the oldest Greek of this Gospel does not have it. Note also what Pilate is proposing: a flogging for a man he has twice declared innocent.\\f*
\\p
\\v 18 But they shouted out all together, "Take this one away, and release Barabbas to us!"
\\v 19 (who had been thrown into prison for a certain uprising that happened in the city, and for murder).\\f + \\fr 23:19 \\ft The exchange is exact and merciless. Jesus is condemned as an agitator against Rome after Rome's own governor has three times found nothing in him; the man released in his place is a genuine insurrectionist and a killer. The name Barabbas means "son of the father."\\f*
\\p
\\v 20 And Pilate called out to them again, wanting to release Jesus.
\\v 21 But they kept shouting, "Crucify, crucify him!"
\\v 22 And he said to them a third time, "Why? What evil did this man do? I found nothing in him deserving death. So I will punish him and release him."\\add [i.e. the third time, and no one answers the question]\\add*
\\v 23 But they were pressing with loud voices, demanding that he be crucified; and their voices were winning out.\\add [i.e. it is decided by volume, not by evidence]\\add*
\\v 24 And Pilate decided that their demand should be granted.
\\v 25 And he released the one who had been thrown into prison for uprising and murder, whom they were asking for; but Jesus he handed over to their will.\\add [i.e. their will — while he himself had prayed, "not my will but yours"]\\add*
\\s1 Daughters of Jerusalem
\\p
\\v 26 And as they led him away, they took hold of Simon, a certain Cyrenian coming in from the countryside, and they laid the cross on him to carry behind Jesus.\\f + \\fr 23:26 \\ft The condemned man carried the crossbeam to the place of execution. Simon is press-ganged into it — and the writer notes that he carries it "behind Jesus," which is the exact picture used at 9:23 for what following costs. Cyrene is in North Africa, and had a large Jewish community.\\f*
\\v 27 And a great mass of the people was following him, and of women who were beating their breasts and lamenting him.
\\p
\\v 28 But turning to them, Jesus said, "Daughters of Jerusalem, do not weep for me — weep rather for yourselves and for your children.\\add [i.e. the man on the way to be executed telling the mourners that they are the ones in danger]\\add*
\\v 29 Because look — days are coming in which they will say, 'Blessed are the barren, and the wombs that did not bear, and the breasts that did not nurse.'\\add [i.e. in a world where childlessness was a grief, only one thing could turn it into a blessing]\\add*
\\v 30 Then they will begin to say to the mountains, 'Fall on us,' and to the hills, 'Cover us.'\\f + \\fr 23:30 \\ft Hosea 10:8, spoken over Israel's high places on the day judgment reached them.\\f*
\\v 31 For if they do these things in the green wood, what will happen in the dry?"\\add [i.e. if this is what is done to living wood, what will be done to a city ready to burn]\\add*
\\s1 The Crucifixion
\\p
\\v 32 And two others also, criminals, were led away with him to be put to death.\\add [i.e. and so "he was counted with the lawless" comes true in the plainest possible way]\\add*
\\v 33 And when they came to the place called The Skull, there they crucified him, and the criminals, one on the right and one on the left.\\f + \\fr 23:33 \\ft Crucifixion was designed for humiliation as much as for pain: the man was stripped, fixed to the wood, and left in public view to suffocate slowly under his own weight. Rome kept it for slaves and rebels. And the law said, "cursed is everyone who hangs on a tree" (Deuteronomy 21:23).\\f*
\\v 34 And Jesus was saying, "Father, forgive them, for they do not know what they are doing."\\f + \\fr 23:34 \\ft The oldest editions of the Greek print this sentence inside double brackets, as at 22:43-44, marking it as an addition the editors judge later than the rest but do not remove. It is printed here as text. It completes the last line of Isaiah 53:12, which he had quoted the night before: numbered with transgressors, and interceding for them.\\f* And dividing up his garments, they threw lots.\\add [i.e. "they divide my garments among them, and cast lots for my clothing" (Psalm 22:18)]\\add*
\\p
\\v 35 And the people stood there watching. And the rulers were turning up their noses, saying, "He rescued others; let him rescue himself, if this is the Christ of God, the Chosen One."\\add [i.e. "the Chosen One" — the very words the voice on the mountain used (9:35)]\\add*
\\v 36 And the soldiers also mocked him, coming up and bringing him sour wine\\add [i.e. "for my thirst they gave me vinegar to drink" (Psalm 69:21)]\\add*
\\v 37 and saying, "If you are the king of the Jews, rescue yourself."\\add [i.e. the wilderness test in its final form — use it for yourself]\\add*
\\v 38 And there was also an inscription over him: "This is the king of the Jews."\\f + \\fr 23:38 \\ft The placard stating the crime, standard Roman practice. Many manuscripts add that it was written "in letters of Greek and Latin and Hebrew"; the oldest Greek of this Gospel does not. What Rome posted as a charge is, in this book, a true notice.\\f*
\\s1 The Two Criminals
\\p
\\v 39 And one of the criminals who had been hanged was blaspheming him: "Are you not the Christ? Rescue yourself and us!"
\\v 40 But the other answered and rebuked him: "Do you not even fear God, since you are under the same judgment?
\\v 41 And we justly, for we are getting back what our deeds deserve; but this man did nothing out of place."\\add [i.e. and that is the fourth voice in this chapter to say he is innocent]\\add*
\\v 42 And he was saying, "Jesus, remember me when you come into your kingdom."\\f + \\fr 23:42 \\ft The oldest Greek reads "when you come into your kingdom"; many English versions have "in your kingdom." Either way, look at what is being said and where. He is looking at a naked man dying the same death he is dying, and calling it a kingdom. "Remember me" is how Israel's people ask God to act (Exodus 2:24; Psalm 98:3; 1 Samuel 1:11).\\f*
\\v 43 And he said to him, "Amen, I say to you, today you will be with me in paradise."\\f + \\fr 23:43 \\ft Greek was written without punctuation, and "today" can attach to either side. Taken here as "I say to you, today you will be with me"; the other reading is "I say to you today, you will be with me." Note the three things given, each larger than what was asked: not someday but today, not remembered but "with me," and paradise — the word the Greek Old Testament uses for the garden in Eden, and which by this period named the place where the righteous dead are kept.\\f*
\\s1 Darkness, and His Last Words
\\p
\\v 44 And it was already about the sixth hour, and darkness came over the whole land until the ninth hour,\\f + \\fr 23:44 \\ft Noon until three in the afternoon. Amos had said it exactly: "I will make the sun go down at noon and darken the earth in broad daylight; I will turn your feasts into mourning" (Amos 8:9-10). This is a feast day. And darkness had covered Egypt before the first Passover (Exodus 10:21-23).\\f*
\\v 45 the sun failing;\\f + \\fr 23:45 \\ft The oldest Greek says the sun failed or was eclipsed; the later manuscripts have "and the sun was darkened."\\f* and the curtain of the sanctuary was torn down the middle.\\f + \\fr 23:45 \\ft The inner curtain shut off the Most Holy Place, which the high priest entered once a year with blood, and no one else ever (Exodus 26:31-34; Leviticus 16). It is torn — the verb is passive, and no human hand is named. Either the barrier is gone or the presence behind it is, and both readings point the same way: what that curtain was for has ended.\\f*
\\v 46 And calling out with a loud voice, Jesus said, "Father, into your hands I place my spirit." And having said this, he breathed out.\\f + \\fr 23:46 \\ft Psalm 31:5, with "Father" added at the front. Psalm 31 is the prayer of a man surrounded by plotters and slanderers who hands himself over to God anyway. Mark and Matthew record the cry of abandonment from Psalm 22; this account has him die in the words of trust.\\f*
\\p
\\v 47 And when the centurion saw what had happened, he was glorifying God and saying, "Truly this man was righteous."\\f + \\fr 23:47 \\ft Where Mark 15:39 has the officer say "truly this man was God's Son," this one has "righteous" — a word that carries both innocence in law and rightness before God, and the same word used of Simeon (2:25) and of Joseph of Arimathea three verses below. A Roman soldier, one of the men who did it, glorifies the God of Israel and delivers the verdict the council would not.\\f*
\\v 48 And all the crowds who had come together for this spectacle, when they saw what had happened, went back beating their breasts.\\add [i.e. the gesture of the tax collector in the temple — the crowd whose voices prevailed, going home in mourning]\\add*
\\v 49 And all those who knew him stood at a distance, and the women who had followed him from Galilee, seeing these things.\\add [i.e. the women of 8:1-3, who will now be the one unbroken line of witness]\\add*
\\s1 Joseph of Arimathea Buries Him
\\p
\\v 50 And look — a man named Joseph, who was a council member, a good and righteous man
\\v 51 (this man had not agreed with their plan and their action) — from Arimathea, a town of the Jews, who was waiting for the kingdom of God.\\add [i.e. so the council's verdict was not the unanimous thing it claimed to be]\\add*
\\v 52 This man went to Pilate and asked for the body of Jesus.\\add [i.e. a member of the council publicly claiming the body of a man his colleagues had just condemned]\\add*
\\v 53 And taking it down, he wrapped it in linen and laid him in a tomb cut in the rock, where no one had ever yet been laid.\\add [i.e. an unused tomb, so that later there can be no confusion about whose body it held]\\add*
\\p
\\v 54 And it was the day of preparation, and the Sabbath was beginning to shine.\\add [i.e. Friday, with sundown coming and everything to be finished before it]\\add*
\\v 55 And the women who had come with him out of Galilee followed after and saw the tomb, and how his body was laid.
\\v 56 And going back, they prepared spices and perfumed oils. And on the Sabbath they rested according to the commandment.\\add [i.e. keeping the law even now, with the work of burial half done]\\add*
\\c 24
\\s1 The Tomb Is Empty
\\p
\\v 1 But on the first day of the week, at deep dawn, they came to the tomb bringing the spices they had prepared.\\add [i.e. coming to finish the burial of a corpse, which is all they expect to find]\\add*
\\v 2 And they found the stone rolled away from the tomb,
\\v 3 but when they went in they did not find the body of the Lord Jesus.
\\v 4 And it happened, while they were at a loss about this, that look — two men stood by them in flashing clothing.\\add [i.e. two, which is the number the law requires for testimony to stand]\\add*
\\p
\\v 5 And as they became afraid and bowed their faces to the ground, the men said to them, "Why do you look for the living among the dead?\\add [i.e. "the living one" — the phrase Israel's scriptures use of God himself]\\add*
\\v 6 He is not here, but has been raised. Remember how he spoke to you while he was still in Galilee,\\add [i.e. not new information — something already said and not held]\\add*
\\v 7 saying that the son of man must be handed over into the hands of sinful men and be crucified and on the third day rise."\\f + \\fr 24:7 \\ft A summary of what he told them three times over (9:22, 44; 18:31-33), with the same word "must" that this book has used from the boy in the temple onward. Nothing that happened was outside the plan; it was the plan, and they were told.\\f*
\\v 8 And they remembered his words.
\\s1 They Tell the Eleven
\\p
\\v 9 And going back from the tomb, they reported all these things to the eleven and to all the rest.\\add [i.e. eleven now, and the missing one is part of the story]\\add*
\\v 10 Now it was Mary Magdalene and Joanna and Mary the mother of James; and the rest of the women with them were telling these things to the apostles.
\\v 11 And these words looked to them like nonsense, and they would not trust them.\\f + \\fr 24:11 \\ft The word is a medical one, used of the raving of a person in a fever. The writer does not spare the apostles: the first report of the resurrection reaches the men who had been told three times what to expect, and they dismiss it as delirium. Whatever this belief was, it did not begin with people ready to believe it.\\f*
\\v 12 But Peter got up and ran to the tomb, and stooping down he sees the linen cloths alone; and he went away to his own place, marveling at what had happened.\\add [i.e. convinced the tomb is empty, and no further than that]\\add*
\\s1 On the Road to Emmaus
\\p
\\v 13 And look — two of them on that same day were going to a village sixty stadia away from Jerusalem, whose name was Emmaus.\\add [i.e. about seven miles, and in the wrong direction]\\add*
\\v 14 And they were talking with one another about all these things that had happened.
\\v 15 And it happened, while they were talking and arguing it out, that Jesus himself drew near and was going along with them;
\\v 16 but their eyes were held from recognizing him.\\add [i.e. held — by someone, and for a purpose that the next hour will show]\\add*
\\p
\\v 17 And he said to them, "What are these words you are throwing back and forth to each other as you walk?" And they stood still, looking gloomy.
\\v 18 And one of them, named Cleopas, answered and said to him, "Are you the only one staying in Jerusalem who does not know the things that happened in her in these days?"
\\p
\\v 19 And he said to them, "What things?" And they said to him, "The things about Jesus the Nazarene, who was a man, a prophet powerful in work and word before God and all the people,\\add [i.e. true as far as it goes, and it does not go far enough]\\add*
\\v 20 and how our chief priests and rulers handed him over to a judgment of death and crucified him.
\\v 21 But we were hoping that he was the one about to ransom Israel. But besides all this, this is the third day since these things happened.\\f + \\fr 24:21 \\ft "Ransom Israel" is the language of the exodus and of the songs at the start of this book (1:68; 2:38): the buying back of a captive people. They are not wrong to have hoped it; they are wrong about what killed the hope. And they mention the third day as though the passing of time settled the matter — the very day they had been told to watch for.\\f*
\\p
\\v 22 But some women from among us also astonished us: they were at the tomb early,
\\v 23 and not finding his body they came saying that they had also seen a vision of angels, who say he is alive.
\\v 24 And some of those with us went off to the tomb and found it just as the women had said; but him they did not see."\\add [i.e. they have every fact and none of the meaning]\\add*
\\s1 He Opens the Scriptures
\\p
\\v 25 And he said to them, "O foolish ones, and slow in heart to trust everything the prophets spoke!\\add [i.e. everything — you believed the half about glory and left out the half about suffering]\\add*
\\v 26 Did the Christ not have to suffer these things and go into his glory?"\\add [i.e. not suffering instead of glory, but suffering as the road into it]\\add*
\\v 27 And beginning from Moses and from all the prophets, he interpreted to them in all the scriptures the things about himself.\\f + \\fr 24:27 \\ft Not a handful of proof texts. The claim is that the whole of Israel's scripture, read as one story, runs toward a Christ who suffers and rises — the lamb provided in place of Isaac, the Passover blood, the covenant cut with blood at Sinai, the servant despised and rejected who bears the sin of many (Isaiah 52:13-53:12), the new covenant of Jeremiah 31, the son of man of Daniel 7, the raising on the third day of Hosea 6:2.\\f*
\\s1 Known in the Breaking of the Bread
\\p
\\v 28 And they drew near to the village where they were going, and he made as though to go on farther.\\add [i.e. not a deception — a traveler does not presume on a house; he waits to be asked]\\add*
\\v 29 And they pressed him: "Stay with us, because it is toward evening and the day has already declined." And he went in to stay with them.
\\p
\\v 30 And it happened, when he reclined at table with them, that taking the bread he blessed it, and breaking it he gave it to them.\\add [i.e. the guest doing what the host does, and doing it with the same four movements as at the supper]\\add*
\\v 31 And their eyes were opened up and they recognized him — and he became unseen by them.
\\v 32 And they said to one another, "Was not our heart burning in us as he was speaking to us on the road, as he was opening up the scriptures to us?"\\f + \\fr 24:32 \\ft One verb, "open," runs through this chapter: he opens the scriptures on the road, their eyes are opened at the table, and at verse 45 he opens their minds. Word and meal together, and both his doing. It is the shape the community's life takes in the second volume — "the apostles' teaching and the fellowship, the breaking of the bread and the prayers" (Acts 2:42).\\f*
\\s1 He Stands Among Them
\\p
\\v 33 And getting up in that same hour, they went back to Jerusalem and found the eleven gathered together, and those with them,\\add [i.e. seven miles back, at night, without waiting for morning]\\add*
\\v 34 saying, "The Lord really has been raised, and he was seen by Simon!"\\add [i.e. Simon, whose faith was prayed for and who has now been sought out first]\\add*
\\v 35 And they told what had happened on the road, and how he was made known to them in the breaking of the bread.
\\p
\\v 36 And while they were saying these things, he himself stood in the middle of them and says to them, "Peace to you."
\\v 37 But being terrified and full of fear, they thought they were looking at a spirit.\\add [i.e. a ghost is a category they have; a body out of a grave is not]\\add*
\\p
\\v 38 And he said to them, "Why are you troubled, and why do reasonings come up in your heart?
\\v 39 See my hands and my feet, that it is I myself. Handle me and see, because a spirit does not have flesh and bones as you see me having."\\add [i.e. the same hands and feet, with the marks in them]\\add*
\\v 40 And having said this, he showed them his hands and his feet.
\\p
\\v 41 And while they still would not trust it for joy, and were marveling, he said to them, "Do you have anything here to eat?"\\add [i.e. too good to take in, which is a different kind of unbelief from the morning's]\\add*
\\v 42 And they gave him a piece of broiled fish.\\f + \\fr 24:42 \\ft Many manuscripts add "and some honeycomb"; the oldest Greek does not.\\f*
\\v 43 And taking it, he ate it in front of them.\\add [i.e. the plainest possible proof, and the last of the meals that run all through this book]\\add*
\\s1 Everything Written About Me
\\p
\\v 44 And he said to them, "These are my words that I spoke to you while I was still with you — that everything written about me in the law of Moses and the prophets and the psalms must be filled full."\\f + \\fr 24:44 \\ft The three parts into which the Hebrew scriptures were divided — the law, the prophets, and the writings, named here by their largest book. It is a way of saying: the whole of it, with nothing left out.\\f*
\\v 45 Then he opened up their mind to understand the scriptures,
\\p
\\v 46 and he said to them, "So it is written, that the Christ suffers and rises out of the dead on the third day,
\\v 47 and that turning back for forgiveness of sins is proclaimed in his name to all the nations, beginning from Jerusalem.\\f + \\fr 24:47 \\ft This sentence is the outline of the second volume: Jerusalem first, then Judea and Samaria, then the nations (Acts 1:8). And it begins in the city that killed him — not because Jerusalem deserves the first hearing, but because that is where it happened. The reach to all nations is what Simeon saw in the temple (2:32) and what was promised to Abraham long before that.\\f*
\\v 48 You are witnesses of these things.
\\v 49 And look — I am sending out the promise of my Father upon you; but you, sit in the city until you are clothed with power from on high."\\f + \\fr 24:49 \\ft The promise is the Holy Spirit, as the second volume says outright (Acts 1:4-5) — what the prophets attached to the age of restoration: "I will pour out my Spirit on all flesh" (Joel 2:28-29), "I will put my Spirit within you" (Ezekiel 36:27). The order is fixed: go to every nation, but first sit still and wait. Nothing here is to be attempted on human strength.\\f*
\\s1 He Blesses Them and Is Carried Up
\\p
\\v 50 And he led them out as far as Bethany, and lifting up his hands he blessed them.\\f + \\fr 24:50 \\ft Lifting the hands over the people is the priest's act: "Aaron lifted up his hands toward the people and blessed them" (Leviticus 9:22; the blessing itself is Numbers 6:24-26). This book opened with a priest who came out of the sanctuary and could not speak the blessing (1:22). It ends with the blessing spoken.\\f*
\\v 51 And it happened, while he was blessing them, that he parted from them and was carried up into heaven.\\add [i.e. while he was still blessing — the words are never brought to an end]\\add*
\\v 52 And they, having bowed down to him, went back to Jerusalem with great joy;\\add [i.e. bowing down to him, which this book has said belongs to God alone (4:8)]\\add*
\\v 53 and they were continually in the temple, blessing God.\\f + \\fr 24:53 \\ft Many manuscripts read "praising and blessing God"; the oldest Greek ends with "blessing God." The circle closes: the story began in this temple with a priest struck silent among people who could only wait, and it ends here with a community that can speak, doing to God what he has just done to them. The temple has forty years left, and for this moment it is what Isaiah said it should be — a house of prayer.\\f*
`;
